const W1 = {"sun": {"score": 6.5, "summary": "Round 3 is a clear step up. Nothing failed to load, the page logged no errors, and the GPU time is inside budget (full disc 2.67-2.86 ms, skim 2.6-2.7 ms with only=sun,post at 1600x900). The full disc at exposure=0.0003 is a convincing HMI-style white-light Sun. Its limb darkening now slightly exceeds the HMI reference (G channel at r=0.97: 61 vs HMI 80, centre 178 vs 172), the limb turns redder as it should, faculae brighten toward the limb, and nothing clips. The wide corona at 0.002 reads as an eclipse photograph: a pearly K-corona, helmet-streamer bulbs, fine rays reaching the frame edge, and prominences on the limb. Far away the Sun is an intense HDR point that bloom turns into a star (Jupiter at 40 R, Neptune at 20 R, 1 AU). Skim motion is smooth and steady with no popping: frame-to-frame correlation is 0.967-0.971 at 1 s steps and 0.980-0.983 at 0.5 s steps close in. In the film (t=12-45) the beats work and the departure has no artifacts.\n\nWhat keeps it from SVS grade is a set of CG tells in exactly the hero close-ups. (1) The prominences are saturated hot-pink tubes combed into smooth, coherent strands with white cores. They read as neon or light-painting trails, not cloudy, fragmented H-alpha plasma, and the grazing view is the worst case. (2) The sunspots are geometric: near-perfect circular or elliptical penumbrae with a hard, smooth outer edge and coarse radial wedges at mid range. Close up they become a symmetric 'iris' with a drawn worm for a light bridge, and the groups sit in straight, evenly spaced rows. At full disc every spot is a bullseye. (3) Granulation hue swings too far (lemon-yellow cells on orange lanes). Close in, the cells are smooth, flat-topped blobs ('giraffe' or cheese texture). At mid range it is a uniform speckle with no meso/supergranular modulation. (4) In the skim, the spicule fringe is a uniform comb and the lifted inner corona is a flat grey fog. (5) The corona rays are a uniform radial-blur fibre texture with straight wedge streamers. Inside the corona, looking away from the Sun, a compact singular swirl artifact appears at a fixed world direction. It comes from the corona shell (it disappears with sunmask=8) and is not hit by the current cut. Floor clearly met; target partly met: impressive at full-disc and wide distances, with obvious tells in the close views.", "gaps": [{"gap": "Prominences read as neon tubes or light-painting: saturated hot-pink/magenta strands with white-hot cores. The strands are uniformly smooth, run coherently along the whole arch at a constant tube width, and have sharp edges. There is no diffuse, cloudy mass, no fragmentation into knots or blobs, and no fuzzy envelope. Seen grazing in the skim, they become braided ribbons like glow-stick trails. They are in every hero frame from t=18 to 40 s of the film.", "severity": "structural", "evidence": "shots/critic/sun/round3/crop_limb_arch.png, shots/critic/sun/round3/x_promzoom.png, shots/critic/sun/round3/x_graze.png, shots/critic/sun/round3/crop_full_rprom.png, shots/critic/sun/round3/crop_skim_lcorner.png, shots/critic/sun/round3/film_t36.png", "fix": "(a) Shift colour toward eclipse red: HA about (1, 0.10, 0.10). Soft-clip the optically thin term with EM/(EM+k) at a far lower ceiling than 24, or apply sqrt(EM), so cores reach pale pink, never white. (b) Break strand coherence: multiply each strand's density along its arc length by a 2-3 octave, high-threshold noise (filling factor 0.15-0.35) so it fragments into knots and gaps. Give each strand a lognormal brightness (sigma about 0.8) and a radius that varies along its length (pinched at the feet, fatter at the apex). (c) Add a diffuse envelope around each rope: a Gaussian 3-5x the strand radius at about 10-20% of the strand emissivity, modulated by low-frequency noise, so the arch is a cloud with threads inside rather than wires on black. (d) Soften the tube edge: integrate a Gaussian radial profile instead of a hard erf cylinder. (e) Hedgerows: replace the smeared vertical streaks with knotty, segmented threads of varying brightness."}, {"gap": "Sunspots look geometric. Penumbrae are near-perfect circles or ellipses of uniform width with a hard, smooth outer edge and no interdigitation with the granulation. At mid range the penumbra is a flat orange disc with coarse radial wedges. Up close it is a perfectly symmetric radial 'iris' with a drawn worm-like light bridge. Groups are laid out in straight rows with even spacing, and at full disc every spot is the same bullseye of round umbra plus round halo.", "severity": "structural", "evidence": "shots/critic/sun/round3/crop_limb_spot.png, shots/critic/sun/round3/crop_skim_spot.png, shots/critic/sun/round3/x_spot.png, shots/critic/sun/round3/crop_full_spots.png, shots/critic/sun/round3/limb.png", "fix": "(a) Outer penumbral boundary: r_out(az) = r0*(1 + 0.15*noise(3-8 lobes) + 0.05*fbm(fine)). End each filament at its own radius (per-cell random, \u00b115%) so granules intrude between filament tips. (b) Add azimuthal curvature and twist to the filaments that varies with radius and per spot. Put bright heads on the inner third and darken toward the outer edge (inner penumbra about 0.85, outer about 0.70 of the quiet Sun). Bundle the filaments into clusters with gaps. (c) Make umbrae irregular (domain-warped, often 2-3 cores in leaders). Build light bridges from the granulation itself with a variable-width mask, not a separate drawn feature. (d) Lay out groups with a Joy's-law axis plus random lateral offsets (\u00b120-40% of the spacing). Make followers fragmented clusters of pores and small spots with rudimentary, one-sided penumbrae. Use a power-law size distribution. (e) At full-disc LOD, keep the penumbra footprint irregular and asymmetric so it isn't a donut."}, {"gap": "The granulation's hue modulation is too strong (lemon-yellow cells on orange lanes, about 10-15% RMS luma contrast plus a strong hue swing). Close in, the cells are smooth, flat-topped blobs with little interior structure, which reads as giraffe skin or cheese. At mid range (limb view, skim) the texture is a uniform speckle with no mesogranular or supergranular brightness modulation, so it looks like a procedural noise tile rather than a solar surface.", "severity": "functional", "evidence": "shots/critic/sun/round3/x_gran_close.png, shots/critic/sun/round3/crop_skim_gran.png, shots/critic/sun/round3/crop_limb_gran.png, shots/critic/sun/round3/skim.png", "fix": "Apply CHROMA only to the large-scale limb and spot temperature terms. For granulation-scale fluctuations, use CHROMA about 1.0 or below, so the luminance contrast stays but the hue stays nearly constant (real \u00b1100-200 K hardly shifts colour). Add a 5-10 Mm mesogranular and 30 Mm supergranular luminance modulation of 2-4%, a lognormal cell-size distribution, and close-range interior structure: bright edge rims, occasional dark dots, and small fragmentation lanes with more amplitude. Keep the narrow lanes, but break them with chains of bright points and pockets."}, {"gap": "Skim limb and sky: the spicule forest is a uniform comb or fur fringe of same-length pink spikes along the whole horizon. The 'lifted' inner corona above the horizon is a flat neutral-grey fog, so the sky reads as an overcast backdrop rather than luminous plasma.", "severity": "functional", "evidence": "shots/critic/sun/round3/crop_skim_lcorner.png, shots/critic/sun/round3/crop_skim_rcorner.png, shots/critic/sun/round3/film_t36.png, shots/critic/sun/round3/x_graze.png", "fix": "Spicules: draw heights from a broader heavy-tailed distribution (2-15 Mm). Cluster about 70% of the jets into bushes with empty stretches between them, vary brightness per jet (lognormal), and taper the tips into a diffuse glow. Sky: make offLift a strong function of height above the limb (bright pearl within about 0.05 R, falling as about h^-1.5), tint it warm pearl (1, 0.95, 0.88), and modulate it with the streamer and ray structure so the sky isn't a flat grey sheet."}, {"gap": "The corona's fine structure is a uniform radial-blur fibre texture covering every position angle at similar amplitude. Streamers are straight wedges with no curved helmet geometry or narrowing cusp, and polar plumes don't curve like dipole field lines, so the wide shot reads somewhat as a zoom-blur filter rather than the Druckm\u00fcller-style magnetic structure in real eclipse corona photographs.", "severity": "cosmetic", "evidence": "shots/critic/sun/round3/wide.png, shots/critic/sun/round3/crop_wide_inner.png, shots/critic/sun/round3/crop_wide_rightedge.png", "fix": "Trace the striation coordinate along field lines of a dipole plus source-surface model (closed loops below about 2-2.5 R, open lines bending toward the equator in streamers and toward the poles in holes) instead of pure radial position angle. Give the rays a heavy-tailed amplitude distribution (a few bright discrete rays, mostly weak background). Concentrate the fine rays in streamer stalks and polar plumes, and smooth the quiet regions."}]}, "mercury": {"score": 6.5, "summary": "Round 3 Mercury clearly meets the floor and gets close to the target in the shots the film actually uses. No Mercury module errors in any run. The half phase, the pole/terminator view and the film's terminator pass (t=62-66) look like real near-terminator imagery. Crater relief is crisp, the small-crater field is dense and clustered, peaks catch light beyond the terminator, and shadows are long. The colour is a natural brownish grey. The full disc is flat with no limb darkening (correct opposition photometry), and the isolated crescent is clean. Consecutive film frames (65, 65.033, 65.067) show no popping or shimmer.\n\nIt is not yet top-studio quality, and the remaining tells are in the hero low pass:\n(1) Procedural craters do not obey the DEM terrain shadow. A crater interior shows as a lit disc with a hard circular outline inside a black terrain shadow (film t66). Craters sitting entirely inside terrain shadow glow at 6-14/255 while the shadow around them is 0. Complex craters read as flat pale decals with stamped T-shaped or bird-shaped central-peak shadows.\n(2) In close views with a high or mid Sun the surface is mush: the 7.5 km/px base map's cloudy blotches, pale ring outlines and almost no relief shading (contract: no blur when filling the frame).\n(3) The mid-ground and near-horizon terrain in the low pass goes soft and smeared, with parallel row lines near the skyline, and the skyline is an almost smooth arc.\n(4) Crater walls are airbrushed smooth, and crater shadow outlines are perfectly smooth ellipses at 10 km altitude.\n(5) The full disc is low in contrast with a salt-and-pepper speckle, and its rays and young craters are weaker than in the reference.\n\nThe huge white haze band behind Mercury in the required view=body shots comes from the asteroids module (its belt ring is not zeroed outside the director). I isolated it with only=mercury plus single modules. It is absent from the film and is not Mercury's fault, but it also causes the glowing ring on the night side in crescent.png.", "gaps": [{"gap": "Procedural crater interiors ignore the DEM terrain shadow and use inconsistent fill light. A crater floor shows as a lit disc with a hard circular edge inside a black terrain-shadow region, in the film's hero low pass. Craters lying wholly inside terrain shadow glow grey (6-14/255) above the surrounding 0-black shadow, so they read as pasted lenses.", "severity": "functional", "evidence": "shots/critic/mercury/round3/film_t66.png (crater at ~990,595), zoom_t66_crater.png; x_term_long.png + zoom_term_long.png (sampled: crater interiors 6-14 vs surrounding shadow 0)", "fix": "Multiply each procedural crater's whole lighting (direct and fill) by the far- and near-field DEM shadow evaluated at the crater's rim-plane height, i.e. the local reference surface before the bowl is carved. If the rim plane is in shadow, so is the floor. Keep the crater's own analytic self-shadow as min() on top. Scale the fill term by the sunlit fraction of the crater's own walls (their DEM visibility), so a crater inside terrain shadow gets zero fill rather than a few percent."}, {"gap": "Complex and degraded procedural craters read as flat pale decals: a hard thin circular outline, a smooth uniform interior, and a stamped T-, bar- or bird-shaped dark 'central peak shadow' instead of an elongated pointed shadow.", "severity": "functional", "evidence": "shots/critic/mercury/round3/verylow.png (craters at ~1300,520 and ~870,740), zoom_verylow.png; zoom_t66_crater.png (T-shaped bar)", "fix": "Give the central peak a real height profile (a cone or Gaussian with ridged noise) and shadow it by marching 6-8 analytic samples of that profile toward the Sun, so shadows taper to points along the light direction. Fade the degraded-crater rim into the terrain with a wide smoothstep (0.15 r), not a thin step. Add wall terraces and slumps (rings modulated by angular noise) so the interior has shading structure."}, {"gap": "Close views with a high or mid Sun are blurry texture mush (target: 'no blur at 0.1 R altitude'). The 7.5 km/px base albedo's low-frequency blotches are magnified ~70x and read as clouds or concrete. Craters appear only as pale ring outlines, with almost no topographic shading at 40-65 degree Sun elevation.", "severity": "functional", "evidence": "shots/critic/mercury/round3/x_highsun.png, zoom_highsun.png (dist=1.1 phase=25), x_limb.png (dist=1.15 phase=50)", "fix": "When the base texel footprint exceeds ~6-8 px, fade the base map's mid and high frequencies toward their local mean, using a lower mip as the 'mean' and lerping the map toward it by footprint. Replace them with synthesized albedo tied to the crater field: bright immature rims and ejecta, slightly dark floors, plus a power-law-spectrum albedo noise matched to MDIS NAC statistics. Check that the photometric partition does not flatten topographic shading at g=25-50 degrees. Hapke macroscopic roughness (theta-bar ~16-20 degrees) would restore crater-wall contrast at mid phase."}, {"gap": "Detail falls off and smears in the mid-ground and near the horizon during the low pass. Terrain beyond ~3 altitudes goes soft, with stretched horizontal streaks. Parallel row lines run near the skyline, and the skyline is an almost featureless smooth arc at 0.085 R altitude.", "severity": "functional", "evidence": "shots/critic/mercury/round3/film_t64.png + zoom_t64_far.png; x_horizon.png + zoom_horizon_left.png (stair-step skyline and row bands); zoom_motion_triplet_far.png", "fix": "Replace the isotropic shading footprint (0.55x long axis) with anisotropic filtering: take 4-8 taps along the major axis of the footprint ellipse (EWA-style) and choose the crater/roughness LOD from the minor axis. Crater octaves then survive at grazing angles without aliasing. For the skyline and row structure, march the horizon against a min-max DEM pyramid (conservative skipping, then a fine refinement) and refine the hit with a secant step, so step quantization does not print rows."}, {"gap": "Shadow shapes look CG at close range. Crater shadows on the floor are perfectly smooth ellipses, crater walls are airbrushed smooth (no talus, gullies or boulders), and large DEM shadows read as soft rounded ink blots rather than long shadows with serrated edges.", "severity": "cosmetic", "evidence": "shots/critic/mercury/round3/x_ultralow.png + zoom_ultralow_crater.png (dist=1.004); lowpass.png (blobs at ~430-600,350-560)", "fix": "Add rim-height noise (angular harmonics 3-12 plus fbm along the rim) to the occluder profile used in the crater shadow test, so shadow edges on floors and walls follow the serrated rim. Make walls rougher than the flats (higher roughness amplitude and radial gully noise where the slope exceeds ~20 degrees). In the far-field march, add the procedural rim heights of the largest crater octave to the occluder."}]}, "venus": {"score": 6.5, "summary": "Round 3 Venus clearly meets its floor and it holds up at disc scale. No module failures appeared in any run. At phase 20 the day disc sits close to the Mariner 10 reference: creamy (centre about sRGB 220/214/204, not clipped), with structure that is subtle but present (luminance std 2.2%, p1-p99 about 10%). The tan absorber patches, the darker equatorial band and the polar spiral streaks only show under stretch, which is what the brief asks for. The physics is mostly right. The terminator is broad and smooth at phase 90. Cusps extend around the night side at 150. Phase 178 gives a complete ring. The 150-degree cusp close-up has a nice layered twilight limb. The night side is truly black (sRGB 0.2). At 400 radii the crescent still reads as a 1-px sliver. Super-rotation is correct: cross-correlation between days=0 and 0.03 gives a coherent -34 px drift (r = 0.86-0.94), which matches the predicted 33.6 px for a 4-day retrograde period. What keeps it from top-tier is mostly the hero beat and close range. (1) The crescent and ring of light are dim, neutral grey and don't glow. At 172 degrees the ring covers only about 270 degrees and is missing on the anti-sun side. (2) The convective cells added this round, seen at 0.15 R, read as a crazed crack network on otherwise soft, blurry clouds. At low altitude the deck is a featureless fog plane (0.8% std). (3) The haze, limb and crescent turn grey or slightly cyan rather than warm cream. (4) The planetary-scale Y and the polar collar are not legible without stretching. Separately, the body views carry a bright grey ecliptic band from the asteroids piece, and in the film's crescent beat post glare and ghosts make the night disc muddy. Neither is Venus-owned, but both hurt the shot. Performance is at the 240 fps vsync cap with Venus filling the screen.", "gaps": [{"gap": "The hero crescent and ring of light (CONTRACT section 5: 'a thick-atmosphered crescent with a glowing limb') are dim, grey and incomplete. At phase 150 the crescent is a 1-2 px bright edge (peak 242) that drops to 170 and then 114 within about 15 px. Nothing reaches HDR, so post adds no glow. At phase 172 the ring peaks at only 156 on the sun side and about 45 at the bottom. On the anti-sun side it is missing: the limb is 17 sRGB against a background of 80, so the ring is about 270 degrees rather than nearly full. In the film crescent beat (t=90) it reads as a thin greyish outline, not a luminous crescent.", "severity": "functional", "evidence": "shots/critic/venus/round3/03_crescent.png, 03_crescent_zoom_limb.png, 04_ring.png, 04_ring_zoom_top.png, 04_ring_zoom_right.png (row 450 profile: x=1172 is 17 vs background 80), 11_journey_t90.png; compare 16_ring178.png, where the ring is complete only at 178", "fix": "In haze.js, make the aerosol phase function a two-term HG: a strong forward lobe (g about 0.75-0.8, weight about 0.85) plus a weak back lobe (g about -0.2). Raise the upper-haze scale height to about 4-5 km and its top to about 95-100 km, so the slant optical depth along grazing limb rays stays around 0.3-1 at every azimuth near 170 degrees. Allow the forward-scatter limb to output HDR radiance (about 2-6 at 170 degrees) so bloom gives a real glow, and keep the deck-lit crescent physically scaled. Ask the director to add about +1 EV for the crescent beat (EV.venus is -0.3 today)."}, {"gap": "The close-range cloud deck shows a procedural tell. The new Worley convective cells draw thin dark F2-F1 lanes that read as crazed glaze or dried-mud cracks at 0.15 R and near the terminator. Everything else at that range is soft and blurry with no fine structure. In a low pass at 0.02 R the deck is an almost uniform fog plane (luminance std 1.5 on 188, about 0.8%). This breaks the contract rule of no blur when a body fills the frame and fails the required close-pass look.", "severity": "functional", "evidence": "shots/critic/venus/round3/05_limb.png, 05_limb_zoom_cells.png, 05_limb_r2_vs_r3.png (round 2 had no cracks), 14_terminator_close.png, 15_horizon_day.png", "fix": "In deck.js, drop the lane darkening on (F2-F1). Instead, brighten the cell cores softly with smoothstep(0.0, 0.5, F1), domain-warped and capped at about 1.5-2% albedo, with lanes at least 30% of the cell width. Confine km-scale cells to patches within about 30 degrees of the subsolar point in local solar time. Carry close-range detail with anisotropic streak octaves at 20-300 km, stretched 4-8x along the local flow, plus the high-latitude gravity-wave trains. Keep at least 2-3% contrast in the 5-50 km octaves when the footprint resolves them, rather than fading toward uniform."}, {"gap": "Off the day-disc centre the colour goes grey or cool instead of creamy yellow. Crescent edge: 242/239/234, with a bluish 1-px fringe of 188/195/206. Ring: 156/157/156. Close day limb: 195/193/186. Low horizon: 198/195/186. The oblique lit region in the pole view goes cyan-grey (181/185/184), against a disc centre of 220/214/204. The brief asks for a Mie-dominated yellowish haze, and the reference stays cream-white across the lit disc.", "severity": "cosmetic", "evidence": "shots/critic/venus/round3/03_crescent.png (row 450 profile), 04_ring.png, 08_pole.png, 08_pole_hp.png, 12_toplimb_close.png, 15_horizon_day.png, 11_journey_t90.png", "fix": "Give the haze aerosol a spectral single-scattering albedo with slight blue absorption (about 0.999/0.996/0.985 for R/G/B), the same unknown-absorber tint as the deck. Scale down the CO2 Rayleigh term on long slant paths, since it is what drives the cool cast at grazing angles. Tint the multiple-scattering and twilight source by the deck albedo colour, not white."}, {"gap": "The planetary-scale morphology the brief asks for does not read without stretching: the sideways Y, chevrons and polar collar. The streaks are short brushstroke smudges at varied angles. The reference instead shows long, coherent streaks parallel to the equator that sweep poleward into a bright collar. Absolute contrast is in range; the problem is coherence.", "severity": "cosmetic", "evidence": "shots/critic/venus/round3/01_day.png, 01_day_vs_ref.png, 01_day_discstretch.png, 01_day_zoom_centre.png, 08_pole_hp.png", "fix": "Stretch the streak noise domain 6-10x in longitude and reduce the amplitude of isotropic mottling at 1000-3000 km. Raise the Y-wave (Kelvin plus Rossby) term to about 5-7% so the dark stem and westward-opening arms show in the unstretched disc. Add a distinct bright collar band at about 55-65 degrees latitude with a darker band just equatorward of it, as in the Mariner 10 and PV OCPP frames."}, {"gap": "The day-side limb lacks the soft, bright haze rim of the reference. In the close top-limb view the deck edge is a darker band (141-177 against an interior of 195), followed by a 10 px monotonic falloff into black, with no detached haze layer. At disc scale the sunward limb is a hard edge.", "severity": "cosmetic", "evidence": "shots/critic/venus/round3/12_toplimb_close.png, 12_toplimb_zoom.png (column 750 profile), 01_day_vs_ref.png", "fix": "Add an optically thin detached haze layer at about 80-90 km (Gaussian in altitude, slant tau about 0.05-0.1) that side- and forward-scatters sunlight. It should give a faint bright band 2-4 px above the deck at 0.3 R. Ease the deck's limb darkening for mu below 0.15 so the in-scattered haze fills the edge instead of a dark ring."}]}, "earth": {"score": 6.5, "summary": "Round 3 Earth & Moon, judged from 30+ renders (all 7 required views plus 17 extras and film frames t112-160). No module failures and no page errors in any log. The floor is clearly met: real continents, oceans, advected clouds, atmosphere, city lights and a Moon. The best frames are strong. The film's Blue Marble at t124 and the dist=3 disc read close to Apollo 17 at a glance, with good land detail (Okavango and Etosha visible, seasonal snow in Scandinavia, polar night in the Arctic). The night pass at t136 is the best shot of the piece: a layered orange/blue/green twilight limb over warm city clusters, close to the ISS reference. The orbital sunrise with aim=sun (el 74.5 and 73.8) has a convincing layered limb and a flattened, reddened Sun. Clouds advect the right way: measured against land between days=0 and days=1, tropical clouds move about 20 px west and southern mid-latitude clouds about 31 px east, with no shear. Earth turns correctly through days=0, 0.25 and 0.5. City lights stay on the night side. The Earthrise at t156/t160 is lit and crisp. Below NASA SVS grade, from most to least harmful: (1) from 1000 km down, clouds are visibly procedural: sponge-like decks with grey holes, soft out-of-focus blobs at 255 km, and comb-like rows of dashes in trade cumulus. (2) The hero disc is washed out next to Apollo 17. Deep-ocean 5th percentile is 71 vs 32, cloud 99th percentile is 210 vs 244, and the ocean is steel-blue (40,87,132) instead of navy (26-43,58-69,102). (3) Terminator reddening reads as muddy brown, not pink/orange, with no blue twilight band from mid range. (4) The ocean glint is a grey haze patch dimmer than the clouds (core 165 vs 196). (5) The lunar ground at Earthrise looks stamped with same-looking crater decals. (6) The Moon at default exposure is muddy (phase-60 lit mean 44 vs reference 136; terminator view peaks at 16/255). (7) Three required views show nothing of their feature (black sunrise, no glint in frame, Earthrise against black lunar night), although the features do exist at other geometries. Performance is borderline but within noise: the full-screen disc measured 2.8-8.4 ms median on a shared GPU, minimum 2.0 ms. Cross-piece, not scored here: the sky's zodiacal light lays a grey fog over most Earth and Moon frames (15_earthrise_p85.png, 08_sunrise_aimsun745.png, 01_approach.png).", "gaps": [{"gap": "Clouds look procedural at close range (low orbit to ~1000 km). Closed-cell stratocumulus reads as a uniform sponge/foam with mid-grey holes. Trade cumulus is a uniform stipple of similar flecks. At 255 km, cumulus are soft blurry blobs with no crisp edges, no lit vs. shaded side, and no visible ground shadow. At mid range, trade cumulus shows comb-like rows of short horizontal dashes. Several fleck elements look like motion-blurred brush strokes. These are the clearest giveaways in every low-orbit beat.", "severity": "structural", "evidence": "shots/critic/earth/round3/04_glint.png, c04_cells.png, 12_leo_horizon_day.png, c12_clouds.png, 23_land_close.png, 10_glint_inframe.png, c10_glint.png, 05_days0.5.png, c05h_flakes.png, c24_term.png", "fix": "(a) Stop driving cell size and blob strength everywhere from one Worley scale: modulate cell size (10-40 km), anisotropy and coverage with a low-frequency regime field plus the map's local coverage gradient, so neighbourhoods differ (clusters, streets, open/closed transitions). (b) Closed cells: bright, per-cell-hashed cloud polygons separated by thin clear-ocean walls (threshold F2-F1 to narrow walls), not grey interior holes. Open cells: cloud rings around ocean-coloured centres. (c) Below ~1 km footprint, let the logistic edge width fall to ~1 px; the s_unres floor keeps edges blurry at 255 km. Give each resolved cumulus a cloud-top height and do a 4-6 step height-field march toward the sun for a lit side, a self-shadowed side, and a cast shadow offset along the sun azimuth by h*tan(zenith). (d) Remove the dash periodicity: align any streak stretch to the local wind from the flow field, cap anisotropy at ~2:1 below 20 km scales, and randomize stretch phase per cell."}, {"gap": "Hero Blue Marble disc is flat and washed out next to Apollo 17. Deep-ocean blacks are lifted about 2x, cloud whites are dimmer, and the ocean reads milky steel-blue instead of deep navy, so the whole disc looks seen through haze. This is the film's signature Earth shot (t116-124).", "severity": "functional", "evidence": "shots/critic/earth/round3/05_days0.png vs bar/refs/earth_bluemarble_apollo17.jpg (disc luminance p5 71 vs 32, p50 112 vs 139, p99 210 vs 244; ocean sRGB ~(40,87,132) vs ~(26-43,58-69,102)); 01_approach.png, c01_leftlimb.png, 20_film_t124.png", "fix": "Calibrate to top-of-atmosphere reflectance: clear open ocean at 30 deg sun should be about 0.08 blue, 0.05 green, 0.03 red including Rayleigh. Lower the deep-water water-leaving reflectance (about 0.004/0.008/0.02 RGB below the surface). Check that aerial perspective is not double-counted: the multiple-scattering term (Psi_ms with ground albedo 0.07) plus per-pixel in-scatter appear to over-veil at nadir, and the x1.35 vertical exaggeration should not raise path radiance at nadir. Raise thick-deck cloud albedo (A to 0.9-0.95 for map values > 0.8) so dense cloud reaches scene radiance ~0.85-1.0 at noon. Re-measure the disc histogram against the Apollo 17 reference until p5 and p99 match within about 10%."}, {"gap": "Terminator reddening reads as muddy brown or tan with dark blotchy cloud shadows, not pink/orange/gold cloud tops over a blue twilight ground. From 1.8-2.2 R the terminator is a plain fade to black; the brief's orange-red terminator band is underdelivered except in the orbital sunrise.", "severity": "functional", "evidence": "shots/critic/earth/round3/02_terminator.png, c02_term_left.png, 19_pole.png, c19_term.png, 24_term_mid.png, c24_term.png", "fix": "Light cloud tops with the transmittance LUT sampled at the top's own height for the real sun elevation, and make sure the result is not then multiplied by a brown ground-diffuse or skylight term. Near the terminator the ambient on clouds should be the blue twilight sky irradiance. Clamp the cloud-shadow darkening by solar elevation: below ~3 deg, ground is lit mostly by the sky, so shadows should go blue-grey, not black. Add the refracted and twilight extension: let the sun's disc stay partly visible to -0.8 deg and add twilight sky irradiance to -6 deg, so the terminator becomes a gold-to-orange-to-deep-blue gradient about 1-2 deg of central angle wide."}, {"gap": "Ocean sun glint is a muted, featureless grey haze patch. In the in-frame low-orbit glint its core is dimmer than nearby clouds, with no bright calm-water core, no slick filaments, no warm tint and no cloud shadows inside it. It only reads well at the higher-phase dist=2.2 view.", "severity": "functional", "evidence": "shots/critic/earth/round3/10_glint_inframe.png, c10_glint.png (glint core sRGB ~165 vs cloud 99th percentile 196); 05_days0.5.png, c05h_glint.png; 05_days0.png, c05_glint.png; compare 24_term_mid.png where it works", "fix": "Widen the wind-roughness range to alpha 0.02 (1-2 m/s, calm under subtropical clear sky) up to ~0.25, with calm patches 50-300 km wide correlated with low cloud coverage, so the GGX peak (~1/(pi alpha^2)) produces a near-saturating core inside a broader sheen. Tint the glint by sun transmittance along the incident path so it warms at low sun. Keep the slick filaments visible in the glint lobe. Darken the specular term inside cloud shadows (the sun-visibility term). Make sure aerial-perspective in-scatter is composited over the specular, not mixed with it before the haze."}, {"gap": "The Earthrise lunar foreground looks stamped. Craters are near-identical round bowls with uniformly black, soft-edged interiors scattered evenly, with no central peaks, terraces, ejecta blankets or rays. Regolith reads as stipple and hills as smooth plaster, so the money shot looks game-like next to Apollo photography.", "severity": "functional", "evidence": "shots/critic/earth/round3/20_film_t152.png, 20_film_t156.png, c156_ground.png, 20_film_t160.png", "fix": "Use a power-law size-frequency distribution (N(>D) ~ D^-2) with regional density (maria ~1/3 of highlands) and clustering (secondary chains). Change morphology with diameter: simple bowls below 15 km; flat floors, terraces and central peaks for 20-150 km. Drive the degradation state (rim sharpness, depth/diameter 0.2 down to 0.05) per crater from a hash, so most craters are soft and only a few are fresh with bright ejecta and rays. Take crater shading from the height-field normal and a shadow march, never a dark ellipse, so the sun-facing inner wall is bright. Time the Earthrise beat for a 5-15 deg sun so long shadows carve the relief."}]}, "mars": {"score": 6.2, "summary": "Round 3 review of mars, Phobos and Deimos. I rendered 26 views (6 required plus 20 of my own), the Mars chapter of the journey (t=166\u2013214 s), crops, and a perf run (6.55 ms average per whole frame at dist=1.6). No mars module errors. Shots 16 and 19 were taken while the sun module was failing (HERO_CONTRAST is not defined). That is another piece's fault and Mars lighting was unaffected.\n\nWhat works: the disc views the film actually uses (j_190, j_194, 10_vm_disc) are good. Their global colour statistics match the Viking reference almost exactly (mean RGB 147/93/49 vs 148/96/54; same luminance percentiles). It reads butterscotch, not crimson, and Valles Marineris has visible relief. Relief at the terminator works on Noctis Labyrinthus, the Tharsis and Elysium shields and the Lycus Sulci aureole (26, 21, 24). The north cap shows the spiral troughs and Chasma Boreale, and its large seasonal extent is correct for Ls\u224815\u00b0 on the epoch. Phobos is an irregular, densely cratered body with groove chains and a Stickney bowl. The tiny disc and the crescent (13, j_202) are clean.\n\nWhat keeps it at a few-tells hobbyist-plus level rather than SVS grade:\n(1) Close range (0.04\u20130.6 R altitude) falls apart. The surface becomes a flat orange sheet peppered evenly with identical fresh bowl craters, sitting on blobby, melted-looking MOLA relief. There are no dunes, ridges, crater variety or albedo detail.\n(2) Dark albedo markings are too weak and too orange. Valles Marineris has no dark floor.\n(3) The polar cap is neutral grey (sRGB about 160) and matte, with a stippled noise edge and dark-cored craters on the ice. There are dark smudge and radial-dash artifacts near the pole.\n(4) Under-filtered detail gives diagonal hatching on the Olympus aureole and vertical tick artifacts on the Phobos crescent.\n(5) The required limb-haze view is essentially black. Part of the cause is that view's geometry, but no twilight is rendered past the terminator.\n(6) The haze shell has a hard top edge and lets stars through near the surface.\n(7) In the film Phobos is a featureless grey egg, or a near-black silhouette against Mars. Deimos is blotchy and its silhouette is faceted.\n\nFloor (a real Mars map on a lit sphere) is clearly met.", "gaps": [{"gap": "Close-range surface has no believable terrain. From 0.04 R altitude up to the required 0.6 R terminator view, the ground is a flat orange sheet with an even pepper of identical fresh bowl craters. Crisp small craters sit on blurry, blobby MOLA-scale relief, so the scale is obviously mismatched. There are no dunes, wrinkle ridges, layered or eroded terrain, crater variety (degraded, flat-floored, rampart ejecta, central peaks) or mid-frequency albedo variation. Crater density is the same on young volcanic plains, polar ice and old highlands. At the pole, a Voronoi crack pattern shows at a 50-km scale. This fails the contract \u00a73 requirement for a close pass at 0.05\u20130.3 R and the brief's 'procedural crater and dune detail by footprint'.", "severity": "functional", "evidence": "shots/critic/mars/round3/16_skim_vm.png, 14_horizon_n.png, 02_terminator.png, 11_vm_term_a.png, z23_channel.png, 27_pole_top.png", "fix": "Add a footprint-filtered detail stack below the MOLA band limit. (a) Domain-warped ridged fbm height, amplitude \u221d wavelength, gated by MOLA local slope/roughness so plains stay smoother than highlands. (b) Recover sharpness by adding a high-pass of the Viking colour luminance as micro-height, and sample MOLA bicubically so scarps stop looking melted. (c) Use a power-law crater size distribution whose density is modulated by a terrain-age mask (low on Tharsis, Elysium, Vastitas and the polar layered deposits; high in the southern highlands). Vary morphology by size and random seed: degraded rims, flat floors, central peaks above ~5 km, lobate rampart ejecta. (d) Put dune ridges, oriented by a smooth wind-direction field, into low-albedo crater floors and the north polar erg. (e) Mask craters out, or frost-fill them, where cap ice > 0.5. Fade each octave by fwidth so it stays within budget."}, {"gap": "Albedo contrast is too low and the dark markings have the wrong hue. Acidalia, Margaritifer, Erythraeum and Meridiani render as slightly darker orange rather than the slate grey-brown basalt in the reference. The Valles Marineris floor is not darker than the plateau, and the Tharsis calderas barely register. The disc reads as uniform pastel orange next to the reference.", "severity": "cosmetic", "evidence": "shots/critic/mars/round3/10_vm_disc.png and j_194.png vs bar/refs/mars_viking_or_rosetta_osiris_truecolor.jpg. Reference dark regions sample (75\u201387, 58\u201365, 50\u201352); render dark regions sample (113,69,36) and (121,79,48). Render bright regions (187,126,73) vs reference (186\u2013201, 114\u2013121, 51\u201356).", "fix": "In the colour-correction pass, apply an albedo-dependent grade. Below the median luminance, pull chroma toward a neutral grey-brown (about linear 0.30/0.25/0.21) and lower luminance slightly. Above the median, keep the butterscotch and remove the slight blue lift. Use a contrast pivot (gamma ~1.15 around the median) and check the result with a histogram match of the dark-region pixels against the Viking reference. Darken canyon floors using a MOLA-depth mask, since chasma floors in the reference are visibly darker."}, {"gap": "The polar cap reads as grey plaster, not bright ice. It is neutral grey (sRGB 160\u2013166) against the reference's brilliant pinkish white (242,217,219). Its edge is a noise-threshold salt-and-pepper stipple. Craters on the cap have dark, unfrosted interiors. Near the pole, in and around Chasma Boreale, there are dark smudged blobs, a radial spray of dark dashes and a star-shaped dark spot.", "severity": "cosmetic", "evidence": "shots/critic/mars/round3/j_198.png (in the film), z198_capedge.png, z10_cap.png, 15_npole.png, z15_chasma.png", "fix": "Raise CO2/H2O frost albedo to 0.6\u20130.7 with a faint warm-pink tint (1.0, 0.94, 0.92). Use a less steep photometric law for ice (blend in Lommel-Seeliger plus a mild forward lobe) so the cap stays bright at 60\u201380\u00b0 incidence. Drive the cap edge from smoothed topography and slope aspect (pole-facing slopes keep frost) with a footprint-filtered 1\u20133 px feather instead of thresholded noise. Apply the frost mask after the crater albedo so crater floors frost over. Find the source of the near-pole dark dashes and blobs, most likely lat/lon-space procedural decals or streaks pinching toward the pole, and evaluate them in 3D body space (or triplanar) above ~75\u00b0 latitude."}, {"gap": "Under-filtered high-frequency shading aliases. A regular diagonal hatching/moir\u00e9 covers the Lycus Sulci aureole at the terminator. Short vertical tick artifacts run across the sunlit sliver of Phobos at high phase. Both will shimmer in motion.", "severity": "functional", "evidence": "shots/critic/mars/round3/z24_aureole.png (from 24_olympus_term.png), z19_crescent.png (from 19_phobos_crescent.png)", "fix": "Band-limit every detail octave by the screen-space footprint: weight = 1 \u2212 smoothstep(0.5, 1.0, fwidth(p)\u00b7freq). Build bump normals from analytic noise derivatives, not dFdx/dFdy of a height. Near grazing light, blend the bump normal toward the geometric normal (normal = normalize(mix(Ng, Nb, smoothstep(0.0, 0.15, dot(Ng, L))))). For Phobos, check any horizon/self-shadow march for step banding at low sun and add jittered or more steps."}, {"gap": "The required limb-haze view is almost entirely black, and the bluish twilight forward-scatter near the terminator is not visible in any view. At dist=1.12, phase=95 the frame spans about \u00b110\u00b0 around the terminator, yet the night side beyond it is pure black, with no twilight arc from the dusty atmosphere. (The limb itself is outside this view's FOV, so this view's geometry is part of the problem.)", "severity": "functional", "evidence": "shots/critic/mars/round3/03_limb.png (identical to shots/critic/mars/round2/03_limb.png), 02_terminator.png, 25_vm_term_wide_a.png", "fix": "Add twilight illumination to the surface beyond the terminator. Mars twilight lasts to 15\u201320\u00b0 solar depression because of the dust: E_tw(\u03b4) \u2248 0.02\u00b7exp(\u2212\u03b4/4\u00b0)\u00b7dustTau, tinted bluish toward the solar azimuth (forward-scattering dust) and butterscotch elsewhere. Also let the atmosphere shell's in-scatter extend a few degrees onto the night side, with the in-scatter integral using the sun-ray's shadowed path length rather than a hard planet-shadow cutoff."}]}, "jupiter": {"score": 5.5, "summary": "Round 3, jupiter (critical). I rendered every required view plus 15 extra angles and crops through the full app. There were no page errors and no \"module failed\" lines. The floor (a banded, animated globe with the GRS and 4 moons) is clearly met.\n\nStrongest at disc scale. The full disc, Io transit (d2.25) and Europa transit (d0.252) read convincingly. Colour statistics in the central disc match the Cassini reference: saturation 0.20 vs 0.197, luminance mean 0.53 vs 0.55. Oblateness is right (aspect 0.943). Strings of white ovals are present. Shadows are physically sized: Io's umbra is ~23 px on an 846 px disc with a 3 px penumbra, which matches the expected ~3.6 px. Film frames t=252-260 (Io shadow + GRS) are the best images of the piece. The tiny disc at 300 R with the moons strung out is clean. Flow really evolves: a co-rotating diff one rotation apart (d0.2175 vs 0.631) shows the SEB wake churning (mean diff 16 in the wake, 7 in the GRS).\n\nFalls apart at close range, which the Juno target is judged on:\n- The required EZ skim is a grey, colourless shag-carpet field.\n- The GRS interior at 1.12-1.6 R is an isotropic sponge/lichen stamp or a wood-grain fingerprint swirl inside a uniform white halo. At disc scale it is a crisp-outlined \"fried egg\" decal.\n- Festoons and hot spots are only grey smudges.\n- Limb darkening is too weak. The sun-side limb holds ~92% of centre brightness at 1-2% of the chord; Hubble falls to ~25%.\n- The departure crescent is a 12-16 px dim sliver.\n- The polar region is brown with a hard regime seam.\n- Close up, Io and Europa are placeholder-grade: parchment with dots, and scratched drywall.\n\nVerdict: competent-to-good hobbyist at disc scale (~7 there), but ~4 in the close shots that should make this the film's showpiece.", "gaps": [{"gap": "The low skim does not hold up. The required EZ skim (dist 1.08, el 0) is a near-monochrome grey field of repeating feathery tufts with almost no colour or contrast, plus a hard-edged white 'feather' patch at the top centre. The close terminator skim (phase 78, 1.15 R) is muddy brown wood-grain, with no readable relief, towers or cast shadows. The film skim (t=264) has colour but reads as marbled paper, smeared into streaks along the view direction on the left. Nowhere is there the vertical, sunlit 3D cloud structure of Juno perijove images.", "severity": "structural", "evidence": "shots/critic/jupiter/round3/03_skim.png, shots/critic/jupiter/round3/z03_skim_top_crop.png, shots/critic/jupiter/round3/x11_terminator_skim.png, shots/critic/jupiter/round3/x09_film_t264.png", "fix": "(1) Make height a real visual. Add parallax-occlusion marching of the cumulus/tracer height field in tangent space when fpk < 20 km/px: 8-16 steps, heights 15-50 km. Add a horizon-angle self-shadow with a long soft falloff whenever sun elevation < 20 deg, so turrets cast visible shadows near the terminator and in the film skim. (2) Give the EZ colour variance at close range. Cream ammonia tops at ~1.0 L, gaps at 0.55-0.65 L tinted tan/ochre (0.78,0.66,0.50) or blue-grey (0.55,0.60,0.66), driven by cloud thickness. Drop the floor that lifts everything to off-white. (3) Kill the grazing-angle smear. Sample the tracer tiles with textureGrad(), using the analytic Jacobian of the stretched-Mercator mapping (or several anisotropic taps along the major axis) instead of isotropic LOD. (4) Add aerial perspective toward the horizon (bluish haze in-scatter growing with path length) so the skim has depth layering."}, {"gap": "The Great Red Spot is not a convincing vortex at any distance.\n- Full disc: a crisp, perfectly elliptical decal with a high-contrast dark-brick outline ring, a radial-gradient salmon interior and a uniform, featureless white collar ('fried egg'). Oval BA reads as a dark-rimmed donut/eye.\n- 1.6 R: a flat orange disc with concentric fingerprint/wood-grain swirl, surrounded by a soft glowing white halo of uniform width.\n- fov 9 at 1.6 R, and 1.12 R: the interior and collar are an isotropic sponge/lichen/popcorn texture with no alignment to the streamlines.\nCassini/Juno show soft-edged concentric bands, filamentary spiral arms, an orange core and a structured collar merging into the STrZ.", "severity": "functional", "evidence": "shots/critic/jupiter/round3/z07_grs_disc_crop.png, shots/critic/jupiter/round3/02_grs_d0.2175.png, shots/critic/jupiter/round3/z02_grs_rim_crop.png, shots/critic/jupiter/round3/x01_grs_tele_fov9.png, shots/critic/jupiter/round3/x02_grs_low_1.12.png, shots/critic/jupiter/round3/x03_terminator_phase88.png", "fix": "Inside vortexR < 1.3, sample octaves B and C in the same (streamline angle, streamline radius) frame as grsTexture, not in the isotropic background frame. Stretch them 4-8x along the streamlines so fine structure becomes filaments that wrap the vortex. Perturb the vortex boundary radius with low-frequency noise (about +-8%, 2-5 lobes, slowly rotating) so the outline is never a clean ellipse. Cut the brick rim contrast by about 60% and make it patchy. Replace the radial palette with the Cassini map's own GRS patch (remapped by grsRemap and rotated by the bounded clock) plus 2-3 red spiral arms. Break the white collar with the zonal tile and add a faintly darker 'Hollow' annulus outside it. Apply the same fixes to Oval BA (pale interior, no dark outline)."}, {"gap": "Limb darkening is too weak, so the planet looks flat-lit like a painted ball. At phase 20, the sun-side limb measures 166-177 sRGB at 1-2% of the chord against 182-193 at the centre (~92% of centre), then cuts off at a hard edge. The Hubble reference at the same relative chord drops to ~45/186 (about 5% in linear), with a blue-grey tinted limb. Root cause is in surface.js: Minnaert k = (0.86, 0.90, 0.95) < 1 combined with pow(mu, k-1). That term *brightens* toward the limb (mu^-0.14 at mu 0.02).", "severity": "functional", "evidence": "shots/critic/jupiter/round3/01_full.png (row 480 profile), shots/critic/jupiter/round3/z01_full_leftlimb_crop.png, bar/refs/jupiter_io_shadow_hubble.jpg", "fix": "Use Minnaert k of about (0.95, 1.0, 1.08) RGB, and up to 1.1-1.15 in the belts. Raise the slant-path absorption of the upper-troposphere/stratospheric haze: blue tau_abs about 0.15-0.25 vertical, applied with an airmass of 1/mu. Add a weak blue-grey in-scatter tint at mu < 0.25. That gives the brightness-to-25%-of-centre falloff over the last ~8% of the radius seen in the Hubble frame, and gives depth to the full-disc and approach shots, which are the film's most-seen Jupiter frames."}, {"gap": "Festoons and hot spots are missing (an explicit brief item). The NEB's south edge has only smeared, horizontally stretched grey-blue washes ('dark smudges'). There are no discrete dark hot spots and no thin blue-grey plumes curving SW into the EZ. The EZ itself is a white field of brushed-paint streaks. A bright convective plume in the NEB clips to a featureless white blob.", "severity": "functional", "evidence": "shots/critic/jupiter/round3/05_flow_sheet.png, shots/critic/jupiter/round3/z05_flow_d0_ezneb_crop.png, shots/critic/jupiter/round3/05_flow_d0.png", "fix": "Add a procedural hot-spot/festoon system on the NEB's south edge (lat about 7 deg N). Use 10-13 equally spaced cells drifting at the 7 deg N jet speed (about +100 m/s) on the long clock. Each cell is a dark blue-grey (0.45,0.50,0.58)xL lens about 3000x1200 km. Trail a festoon from each as a thin (300-600 km) curved filament, following a parabolic SW path into the EZ, domain-warped by octave B and fading over 10-15k km, with a bright white plume head upstream. Tone-map the plume head: cap its albedo at about 0.92 and keep its cumulus texture so it doesn't clip."}, {"gap": "The departure crescent is weak. At phase 150 the lit band at the equator is only ~12-16 px wide on a 367 px radius (geometric width is ~49 px), peaking at 184 sRGB right at the limb and falling to 48 within 14 px. It reads as a faint brown sliver rather than the bright, haze-glowing crescent of Cassini/Juno departure images. At phase 160 there is only a 2 px white rim line.", "severity": "functional", "evidence": "shots/critic/jupiter/round3/04_crescent.png, shots/critic/jupiter/round3/x12_crescent160_tele.png", "fix": "Let the forward-scatter lobes scale with the slant path through the haze (multiply by min(1/mu, 8), with a two-term HG of g = 0.8 / 0.35). Raise the haze single-scattering albedo in the red to about 0.97 so the crescent warms and widens. Give the off-limb haze shell more scale height in the stratospheric layer (H_S 80 km to about 150 km at high phase) so the ring is 4-8 px at this framing. Target an equatorial crescent that stays above about 100 sRGB for 25+ px inward."}]}, "saturn": {"score": 6.8, "summary": "Round 3, saturn. No module errors and no fallback in any of the 22 renders. The floor (banded globe plus ring system) is clearly met. The rings are the strongest part. Radial structure comes from real occultation data: C-ring plateaus, B ring, Cassini Division, A ring with the Encke gap, and an F-ring thread. There is no moire at grazing telephoto, and the tiny disc at dist=250 still reads as a ringed planet. Photometry flips correctly between faces: on the unlit face the B ring is black while the C ring, Cassini Division and A ring glow. The planet's long, hard-edged shadow across the rings and the ring-shadow bands on the northern hemisphere are both geometrically right. The eclipse is close to the reference: dark disc, thin bright limb ring, rings glowing with a dark backlit B ring, ringshine below the ring silhouette, and an E-ring arc. Titan has an orange haze disc with a detached blue layer, and its phase-160 halo works.\n\nWhat keeps it from top-planetarium grade:\n1. The globe's bands are flat fills with wobbly, cut-out edges and \"paint-drip\" hooks. This reads as illustration, not the soft, finely striated Cassini disc, and it shows in every hero shot.\n2. At 0.25 R the cloud deck looks like sand or hammered bronze, with 2x2 faceting and dotted single-pixel speckle arcs.\n3. A real ring skim works but falls short. Over the unlit face the floor is pitch black because there is no Saturnshine. Over the lit face the texture reads as brushed metal.\n4. The lit-face C ring is about 4x too dark.\n5. The hexagon is invisible in its required 2026 view because it sits in polar night. That is physically right, but the brief asks for it. Rendered at a 2017 epoch it is visible, but its edges look like thresholded noise.\n6. Enceladus has fingerprint-like contour ridges, and Rhea has too few craters.\n\nProcess note for the orchestrator: the required ring-skim view (dist=1.9, phase=50, el=-1.2) is not a skim. el is measured from the ecliptic, so that camera sits 0.82 Saturn radii below the ring plane. A real skim is phase=50&el=23 (0.05 R below, lit face) or el=26 (0.05 R above, unlit face). Similarly, 'classic el=20' is actually 0.45 deg below the ring plane, on the lit face (computed with poleQuaternion).\n\nCross-piece issue: the speckled dust lens in the full-module eclipse frame comes from the asteroid belt. It disappears with only=saturn,post,sky.", "gaps": [{"gap": "Globe banding reads as vector illustration. Bands are flat posterized fills with wobbly hard edges, and the band edges carry hooked 'paint-drip' tabs and notches. Cassini shows a soft gradient made of many fine zonal striations instead. This is visible in every hero shot (classic, skim approach, unlit view).", "severity": "cosmetic", "evidence": "C:/Users/danny/fun/space-animation-v1/shots/critic/saturn/round3/classic_lit.png, crops/classic_lit_globe_top.png (hooks at band edges), crops/skim_globe_bands.png, skim.png, x_trueskim_lit.png (bottom bands); compare bar/refs/saturn_cassini_equinox.jpg", "fix": "In globe.js the vortex 'domain rotation' currently deflects the band-LUT latitude (latW), which drags the sharp edges into hooks. Apply that rotation only to the turbulence/eddy texture coordinates and keep the band-LUT lookup unrotated, or fade the rotation to 0 within ~1.5 deg of each E_LAT edge. Cut the cusped wave-train amplitude (0.26-0.48 deg) to <=0.08 deg and drop the 2*ph cusp harmonic. Give each sharp LUT edge a minimum transition width of ~0.3-0.5 deg at classic distances. Add a latitude-only fine striation layer (0.1-0.4 deg periods, +-2-3% albedo, footprint-filtered) so the bands read as many thin streaks. Compress mid-latitude band-to-band contrast by ~30-40%."}, {"gap": "The ring skim (a section 5 film beat) is weak. 0.05 R above the unlit B ring, the whole lower half of the frame is black: pixel mean is under 1/255 and still black at 8x boost, because there is no Saturnshine. 0.05 R below the lit face, the near-field texture is soft, brushed-metal azimuthal streaks with no granular, clumpy or wake structure and no density-wave trains. Also, the required skim query does not produce a skim at all: that camera is 0.82 R below the ring plane.", "severity": "functional", "evidence": "C:/Users/danny/fun/space-animation-v1/shots/critic/saturn/round3/x_trueskim_unlit.png, crops/skim_unlit_boost8.png, x_trueskim_lit.png, crops/skim_lit_near.png, skim.png (required query: globe with distant rings, not a skim)", "fix": "(a) Add Saturnshine to both ring faces: irradiance from the sunlit globe seen from each ring point. Use an analytic or precomputed LUT over (radius, azimuth relative to the subsolar meridian) of the visible lit-and-unshadowed globe fraction times albedo, about 2-6% of sunlight at the B ring. Feed it through the same lit/unlit layer photometry, so the unlit B ring near the planet is dim but textured, not void. (b) Near-field texture: add azimuthally elongated (1:10-1:30) cellular 'straw' clumping at 0.5-5 km modulating brightness/tau in the A and B rings. Add canted (about 20-25 deg) wake anisotropy as a brightness-vs-azimuth term. Add real density-wave trains (wavelength decreasing outward) at the Janus/Mimas resonances in the A ring. Filter at ~1.5 px/cycle instead of the current softer cutoff so the nearest ring isn't blurred. (c) Orchestrator/director: build skim cameras in the ring frame with poleQuaternion('saturn'). For the body view, phase=50&el=23 or el=26 gives a 0.05 R skim."}, {"gap": "Close-range cloud deck (0.25 R altitude) has artifacts and the wrong material read. Single-pixel black/white speckles form dotted circular arcs and straight dotted lines (vortex-boundary and height-field discontinuities). The relief shading is blocky with 2x2 facets (screen-derivative bump). The 'popcorn' relief looks like sand or hammered metal rather than clouds. Vortices look like carved wood whorls.", "severity": "functional", "evidence": "C:/Users/danny/fun/space-animation-v1/shots/critic/saturn/round3/x_terminator_close_d0.png, x_terminator_close_d005.png, crops/termclose_dotted_arc.png, crops/termclose_dots.png", "fix": "Replace dFdx/dFdy-based bump normals with tangent-space central differences of the height function: two extra evaluations at +-eps east and north, with eps tied to the pixel footprint. This removes the 2x2 quad faceting. Make every vortex/oval influence weight C1-continuous at its boundary, e.g. w = (1 - (r/R)^2)^3 for r<R, so neither the value nor the slope steps. Guard and clamp the normal. Reduce the popcorn relief amplitude by 50-70% and the vortex streamline relief by ~50% so the deck reads as soft haze-covered cloud. Raise the albedo share of the eddy texture instead."}, {"gap": "The lit-face C ring is far too dark. Display value is ~20/255 vs ~94 for the B ring, a linear ratio of ~0.03. Expected is ~0.15-0.25 for tau~0.1 at 8 deg sun and 38 deg view, so the C ring reads as near-black void with stars rather than the translucent grey band in Cassini lit-side images.", "severity": "cosmetic", "evidence": "C:/Users/danny/fun/space-animation-v1/shots/critic/saturn/round3/classic_lit.png, crops/classic_lit_ring_left.png (pixel sample at 400,440 vs 230,400)", "fix": "Check the lit-face C ring term \u03d6P/4\u00b7\u03bc0/(\u03bc+\u03bc0)\u00b7(1\u2212e^{\u2212\u03c4(1/\u03bc+1/\u03bc0)}). With \u03c4=0.1, \u03bc0=0.14 and \u03bc=0.62 the slab factor is ~0.58, so a C-ring albedo of ~0.4x the B ring should give ~0.2x the B-ring radiance. Raise the C ring and Cassini Division particle albedo or the slab term until the ratio is ~0.2 in linear. Verify the satDisplay chroma scaling isn't pushing these dim pixels into the toe."}, {"gap": "North polar hexagon. In the required hexagon view (2026 epoch) it is invisible: the pole is in polar night and appears as black above the terminator, and it stays invisible at exposure x4. Rendered at a 2017 epoch it exists, but the band edges are ragged, noise-thresholded 'torn paper'. There are no laminar streaks parallel to the sides, and the eye is a flat brown dot with speckle instead of an eyewall and spiral arms.", "severity": "functional", "evidence": "C:/Users/danny/fun/space-animation-v1/shots/critic/saturn/round3/hexagon.png, x_hex2026_exp4.png, x_hex2017.png, crops/hex2017_detail.png", "fix": "Shape: drive the hexagon outline from a smooth rounded-polygon distance (cos-based, with low-amplitude long-wavelength meander only) and remove the high-frequency noise threshold on the band edges. Inside the jet band, add anisotropic streaks aligned with the local polygon side (flow along the perimeter). Give the vortex 3-5 logarithmic spiral cloud arms converging on a sharp eyewall ring with a bright inner rim. Visibility: at 78 N the noon sun is ~4 deg up in 2026, so model grazing twilight via the existing haze layer (forward-scattered limb/terminator glow). Then a sunward-azimuth view shows the hexagon in twilight. Otherwise tell the director it cannot be featured in 2026 lighting."}]}, "uranus": {"score": 7, "summary": "Round 3, Uranus (stake: major). No page errors and no module failure lines in any of the ~30 renders. The floor in CONTRACT section 6 is clearly met: a pale cyan globe, rings, and the 98 deg tilt, with the north pole sunward and the rings and moon orbits nearly face-on to the Sun.\n\nAt disc scale the piece is strong. The film's bullseye frames (film_352/354, bullseye.png, x_ref20.png) are close to photographic: soft terminator, an off-centre 2026 polar cap, and ten crisp, aliasing-free hairline rings with epsilon the brightest. Measured against the planet, ring brightness stays physically consistent from 7.6 R to 300 R (the rings are about 0.2% of the planet's brightness at 300 R and do not pop or show a minimum-width floor). No planet shadow falls on the rings, which is correct geometry for a sub-solar latitude of 74 deg. Phase 140 shows forward-scattered dust lanes. The grazing skim has rings sinking correctly behind the haze. Against the Voyager reference, the globe is slightly greyer and less cyan: centre sRGB (191,219,220) vs (196,234,237). It is also flatter, because limb darkening is weaker on the sunward and side limbs.\n\nThe tells show up in close and odd views:\n- A deterministic pure-black pixel cluster sits on the lit limb in the required rings-close view. It stays put when the planet rotates and when the sky is off.\n- At 0.2 R altitude the cloud deck is isotropic domain-warp marbling with wood-grain contour lines, not zonal flow.\n- At the grazing horizon there is a 2-px darker seam between the deck and the haze.\n- The procedural moons are competent but CG: Ariel's graben are a Voronoi crazy-paving net, Miranda's coronae look like onion-ring contours, and close up its chevron renders as two lit tube-like sticks in the night side.\n- Methane clouds are barely readable fuzzy smudges.\n- There is no concentric zonal banding between the polar cap and the limb, which the stretched reference shows.\n\nThe giant hexagonal flare ghosts in the crescent and the bright streak at the occluded Sun in the phase-170 view belong to the post/sun pieces, not to Uranus.\n\nOverall: impressive at the distances the film uses, with a few clear tells up close. Score 7.", "gaps": [{"gap": "A deterministic black-pixel artifact sits on the lit limb: 4-6 pixels at sRGB about (5,7,9), inside a limb that is about 190. It appears in a required view and is locked to the view: it stays when the planet spin and moon phases change (days=0.06) and when stars are off (sky=0), so it is neither a moon nor a star. It is probably a NaN or negative value from the globe/haze shell (ray-ellipsoid discriminant or a 1/mu term at grazing) or from the ring fragment over the limb. With the camera moving it would sparkle along the limb.", "severity": "functional", "evidence": "shots/critic/uranus/round3/rings_close.png px (276-277,786-787); crop_rc_speck.png; reproduced identically in x_rings_close_rpt.png, x_rings_close_d006.png (days=0.06), x_rings_close_sky0.png (sky=0)", "fix": "Render the same view with a debug output that writes magenta wherever isnan(c)||isinf(c)||any(lessThan(c,vec3(0))) in globe.js, the shell in shared.js and rings.js, to find which one fails. Then clamp every discriminant with max(disc,0.0) and every mu/cos with max(mu,1e-4). Give the analytic-ellipsoid miss case a fallback to the mesh normal instead of using an invalid t. End each shader with c = max(c, 0.0); c = (c==c) ? c : vec3(0) in a form ANGLE keeps, e.g. any(isnan(c)) ? vec3(0.) : c, and make sure the result is not black in the lit deck: use the deck value without haze rather than 0."}, {"gap": "Globe tonality versus Voyager. The disc is greyer and less cyan, and it reads flatter. Centre sRGB is (191,219,220) vs the reference (196,234,237): the reference has B >= G and about 15 more in G/B. On the side and sunward limb the render holds 0.89-0.94 of centre at 0.8-0.9 R, while the reference falls to about 0.79/0.68 at the same radii. The side-by-side reads as plaster-teal rather than luminous pale cyan. This affects every Uranus frame in the film.", "severity": "cosmetic", "evidence": "shots/critic/uranus/round3/cmp_ref_vs_ref20.png (left: bar/refs/uranus_voyager2.jpg, right: x_ref20.png); radial profiles measured on bullseye.png and x_ref20.png", "fix": "Raise the linear G and B of the Minnaert B0 by about 6-8% and B slightly more than G, e.g. B0 = (0.37, 0.94, 1.05), to offset AgX highlight desaturation. Re-fit with tools/agx.py against the reference centre (196,234,237). Raise the Minnaert k a little in all channels (e.g. 0.95/1.15/1.12) so the side limb falls to about 0.65 of centre at 0.9 R. Keep the hue constant toward the limb."}, {"gap": "At close range (dist 1.2, about 0.2 R altitude) the cloud deck is isotropic domain-warped marbling with wood-grain contour lines, especially on the right of close_limb.png, instead of flow stretched along latitude. It reads as agate or marble rather than an ice-giant atmosphere. The stretch shows lobes that are as tall as they are wide and contour lines spaced in parallel.", "severity": "cosmetic", "evidence": "shots/critic/uranus/round3/close_limb.png (wavy contour lines, top-right); stretch_close_limb.png (local contrast x6); x_skim.png (better, streaky near the horizon)", "fix": "Sample the close-range detail in a domain stretched 1:8-1:15 in longitude, as the far streaks are. Limit the warp displacement to mostly the longitude direction and make it at most about 0.3 of the feature wavelength, so iso-contours of the warped noise never show. Replace marble-like turbulence with a sum of zonally sheared, flow-advected streak layers plus sparse discrete convective cells. Add a slight blue-noise dither in luminance instead of relying on post grain."}, {"gap": "The procedural moons have clear CG tells in the required Miranda view and the moon close-ups. Miranda's coronae are concentric onion-ring contour terraces. Close up, its chevron or scarp renders as two straight tube-like lit sticks lying in the night side. Ariel is covered in a polygonal Voronoi crazy-paving net of hairline cracks with extruded slab walls, unlike its long sub-parallel smooth-floored chasmata. Titania's rifts cross in a stamped X.", "severity": "cosmetic", "evidence": "shots/critic/uranus/round3/miranda.png; x_miranda_close.png + crop_mir_lines.png (V of lit tubes in shadow); x_ariel.png (Voronoi net); x_titania.png", "fix": "Miranda: drive the corona bands mainly through albedo (bright and dark stripes of \u00b125-35%, as in Arden), lower the relief of each terrace and make neighbouring bands non-parallel. Drop the extruded profile of the chevron: make it an albedo patch with no relief, or a scarp whose lit face follows the local slope limit and is self-shadowed past the terminator. Ariel: hide Voronoi edges below a width/depth threshold so only 3-6 long trend-parallel chasmata and a few cross-links remain, and add smooth, slightly convex floors. Titania: make rifts curve and split instead of crossing in a clean X."}, {"gap": "At the grazing horizon a 2-px darker grey-khaki seam runs between the cloud deck and the haze shell, sRGB about (134,145,145) between haze (118,135,137) and deck (150-165). It reads as an outline where the deck mesh meets the shell.", "severity": "cosmetic", "evidence": "shots/critic/uranus/round3/x_skim.png column x=750 rows 313-315; crop_skim_horizon.png, crop_skim_rings.png", "fix": "Integrate the deck and haze in one ray march instead of compositing a separate opaque deck under the shell. At least make the deck's edge fall off as the shell's transmittance rises, using the same optical-depth function in both, so the composite is continuous. Check that deckT() and the shell use the same tangent-ray path length at grazing incidence."}]}, "neptune": {"score": 6.5, "summary": "Round 3, neptune. I rendered all 6 required views with every module loaded and 20+ extra angles (mostly with only=neptune,post,sky), plus a contact sheet of the Neptune chapter (t=330-431) and full-resolution film frames at t=374/380/394. The Neptune module raised no errors and no Neptune fallback appeared. The only failure was in the Triton+Neptune run: 'module jupiter failed: missing asset jupiter/turb_z.webp', which belongs to the jupiter piece. The floor is clearly met: a deep azure globe distinctly deeper than Uranus (film: Uranus (182,213,214), Neptune (50,112,184)) and a Voyager-based Triton.\n\nStrengths: the hero approach frame (24_film_t374) and the 70-px small disc read like a real Neptune portrait. The GDS with hooked companion, streaky cirrus and Scooter-like clouds are recognisably taken from the Voyager image. Crescent (03), eclipse (14) and backlit rings (04, 24_film_t380) are handsome: forward-scatter limb ring, a sharp planet shadow cutting the rings, and the Adams arcs as clumpy bright dashes. Triton is centred exactly where positionAt puts it. It has a pink cap with plume streaks, cantaloupe in the mosaic region, and correct twin-crescent lighting with Neptune (06, 24_film_t394).\n\nTells that keep it below 7:\n- Cloud rendering: bright clouds are opaque white brushstrokes with hard, stair-stepped edges. The GDS reads as a lens or eye with a highlight rim, not the reference's soft, luminous, blue-tinted companions.\n- Deck texture at 1.15-1.8 R: a uniform marbled oil-on-water ripple that passes straight through the vortex.\n- Banding: about half the reference contrast, and the -59 belt and -71 collar are invisible even from el=-60.\n- Triton procedural fill: the north hemisphere is raised Voronoi plates (crocodile skin, not cantaloupe depressions). The fill on the cap is sandpaper with identical stamped dashes.\n- Triton terminator: straight and hard, because relief is faded out exactly where low sun should show it.\n- Limb: a 1-3 px teal outline.\n- Close-range backlit haze: about 300 km scale height, so it becomes a thick transparent glow halo with stars showing through.\n\nBy view: disc 7, framed dark spot 5.5, crescent 7.5, rings 7, Triton 6.5, Triton+Neptune 7.5.", "gaps": [{"gap": "Bright clouds look like opaque white paint strokes, not methane-ice cloud. The cirrus are crisp, uniform white lines. The GDS companion is a solid C-hook with stair-stepped parallel striations inside and a hard 'torn-paper' cut-out edge where it meets the dark spot. The GDS's north-east boundary carries a thin bright rim that makes the spot read as a glass lens or eyeball. In the Voyager reference the companions are luminous, soft-edged and blue-tinted, blooming into the surrounding haze, and the spot's edge is diffuse and irregular, darkest at its west end. This is visible in the hero approach frame of the film.", "severity": "functional", "evidence": "shots/critic/neptune/round3/24_film_t374.png, z24_spot.png, 02z_spot_framed.png, z02_hook.png, z02_rim.png, 01_disc.png, z01_spot.png vs zref_gds.png (crop of bar/refs/neptune_voyager2.jpg)", "fix": "1) In ribbon()/streak(), replace the thresholded fractal edge with a continuous opacity profile: core smoothstep at least 1.5-3 px wide plus an exponential skirt (2-4x the core width, 15-30% opacity) for the thinning cloud base. Drive the raggedness with ONE continuous warp of the across-track coordinate, not stacked thresholds; the stacking makes the contour striations. 2) Tint cloud radiance by the gas column above it: cl = mix(cl, cl*deckTint, 0.25..0.4), rising toward thin edges so edges go blue, and cap core albedo so peaks display around (200,225,245), not pure white. 3) Add a soft scattering halo: blur the cloud field once at about 3x the core width and add 10-15% of it as uGasC-tinted glow over the deck. 4) GDS: drop the bright NE rim term or swap it for a broken, faint cirrus arc on the N/NW edge as in the reference. Soften the S/E super-Gaussian (exponent 4.2 -> about 2.5) and darken the west third of the interior about 10% more than the east."}, {"gap": "The mid- and close-range cloud deck is a single-scale marbled 'oil-on-water / ink-swirl' pattern. Thin dark contour lines recur at the same scale over the whole frame, with no band edges, no shear-organised eddies and no small bright tufts. It passes straight through the GDS as if the vortex had no flow. Fine-texture contrast is low (high-pass std 2.05/255 at 1.8 R, 1.65/255 at 1.15 R), and the 1.15 R frame has no features at all. This is the required dark-spot view at dist=1.8.", "severity": "functional", "evidence": "shots/critic/neptune/round3/02z_spot_framed.png, 02_spot_days0.1.png, 02_spot_days0.3.png, 08_close115.png, 22_spot_motion_d0.02.png", "fix": "1) In deckFine(), drop or heavily reduce the ridged term 0.45*(r*r-0.63), which draws the hairline contours. Use smooth signed noise with a gentler spectrum (gain about 0.65) so no single octave dominates. 2) Advect the deck domain through the GDS's own velocity field: inside an elliptical radius rho < 1.3, rotate the noise coordinates about the spot centre by an angle omega(rho)*T (anticyclonic, peak near the rim). The texture then wraps around and stretches along the rim, and the interior gets the faint spiral the notes claim but that does not render. 3) Modulate fine-texture amplitude by latitude: high in shear zones at band edges (-20, -45, -60, +25) and near zero in quiet zones, so the deck has structure instead of a uniform surface texture. 4) Add sparse small bright convective tufts (5-50 km, coverage below 2%) on the active latitudes so close frames have points of interest."}, {"gap": "Banding is too faint. On the central meridian of the required disc view, after removing limb darkening, the residual is p2p 49 DN / std 7.3, versus 89 / 11.4 in the Voyager reference. Even from el=-60 the -59 S dark belt and the bright -71 collar cannot be seen; the southern hemisphere is a smooth gradient with scattered streaks. The reference's most recognisable large-scale structure, the broad darker southern band below a lighter zone, is missing.", "severity": "functional", "evidence": "shots/critic/neptune/round3/01_disc.png, 09_south.png, 24_film_t374.png, 13_small.png vs bar/refs/neptune_voyager2.jpg", "fix": "Roughly double the band multipliers. Make the -59 belt about (0.45, 0.40, 0.50) with sigma 6-7 deg and a sharp equatorward edge. Make the -71.5 collar bright (about 1.35, slightly cyan) with a sharp poleward edge around a darker polar cap. Set the -29 light zone to about 1.25 and the equatorial belt to about 0.7. Raise the share of the band pattern carried by the gas term from 60% to about 85%, so the Rayleigh component stops washing the bands out at mid-disc. Verify with the central-meridian residual (target p2p of at least 80 DN in the dist=3.5 el=5 view) and confirm the collar is visible at el=-35."}, {"gap": "Triton's procedural terrain looks synthetic. In the procedural north hemisphere, the 'cantaloupe' is a uniform field of raised Voronoi plates with dark crack lines: crocodile skin or cobblestone, the opposite of real cavi (overlapping circular depressions with raised rims). It covers everything evenly, with no smooth plains, walled plains or maculae. The procedural fill on the south cap is a uniform sandpaper grain sprinkled with identical stubby dark dashes, not the varied fan-shaped plume streaks. A blurred, featureless band separates the Voyager mosaic from the procedural fill near the limb.", "severity": "functional", "evidence": "shots/critic/neptune/round3/18_triton_north.png, z18_cells.png, z18_seam.png, 17_triton_cap.png, z17_fill.png, 16_triton_close.png", "fix": "1) Build cavi as depressions: h = -sum of smooth bowl kernels at jittered cell centres (25-35 km, \u00b140% size variance) with a rim uplift ring and overlapping neighbours, not raised Voronoi cells. 2) Mask cantaloupe density with a large-scale noise so it gives way to smooth plains, terraced walled plains (Ruach/Tuonela analogues) and a few dark maculae with bright aureoles. 3) Plume streaks: use 40-150 km fans with length, width and albedo drawn from heavy-tailed distributions, a dark head fading downwind, and orientation from a smooth NE-trending wind field; cut density about 3x. 4) Narrow the mosaic-to-procedural blend to 1-2 deg and inject procedural detail of matched contrast into the blurred mosaic border, so no smooth halo is left."}, {"gap": "Triton's terminator is a perfectly straight, hard edge with no relief. The bump term is multiplied by smoothstep(0,0.2,mu0g), which removes slope shading exactly where low sun should reveal ridges and pits most strongly. The result reads as a smooth CG ball cut in half.", "severity": "cosmetic", "evidence": "shots/critic/neptune/round3/23_triton_half.png, 06_triton_neptune.png, z06_pair.png", "fix": "In triton-surface.js, fade the gradient perturbation only by pixel footprint and limb (muv), not by mu0g. Keep the geometric-terminator mask, but let the perturbed mu0 push ridge tops slightly past it: smoothstep(-0.03, 0.02, mu0) on the perturbed normal. Add cheap horizon shadowing: 4-6 height-field taps along the projected sun direction, darkening where a sample rises above the sun ray. Pits then fill with shadow and the terminator becomes ragged at full-disc scale."}]}, "asteroids": {"score": 5.5, "summary": "Round 3, asteroids (minor stake). The page loads cleanly. No module failed, no fallback placeholder, no page errors, only the director's cruise-rate warnings. The floor is clearly met: instanced rocks line the crossing. Tumbling is deterministic and smooth (tumble sheet: the hero and the small rocks rotate as a pure function of t). There are no LOD pops in a 1/30 s strip at t=219-219.5. The belt ring for wide shots exists and its Kirkwood lanes read. Perf at the required view is 116.9 fps avg, 8.56 ms avg, 20.9 ms p95, on a shared GPU. The piece's single best image is x_sunward140: a backlit swarm silhouetted against the forward-scatter glow. It is genuinely cinematic, but the film never shows it.\n\nWhat the film does show is mostly front-lit, flat, neutral-grey \"popcorn\" rocks (film 215-222). The money shots, the Eros-like beat hero at t=222 and the close whip at t=219.5, look like soft plaster or soap. They have a pillowy low-frequency surface, evenly scattered stamped circular pocks with no rim or ejecta hierarchy, smudge-noise albedo, ink-blot black shadows and no visible boulders or regolith. At fov 8 the fracture-plane cuts show as layered paper-flap overhangs. On crescents (film 224/226) the backlit-limb term draws bright contour lines along every noise ridge. It reads as crumpled foil with 1-px aliased rims. Mid-size rocks are smooth jelly-bean silhouettes. The unresolved points are uniform soft 2-4 px bokeh blobs that read as snow, not distant rocks. From outside (film t=213 arrival, x_outside_far) the swarm is an obviously artificial soft-edged rectangle. Against bright backgrounds some rocks show a translucent half-grey ghost from the impostor/mesh cross-fade. No dust glints are visible in any view, and the sunward haze is a featureless glow. Fast near whips (about 40 px per frame) have no motion blur. Measured colour is almost neutral ((R-B)/L about 0.03-0.08), so there are no tan S-types or visible M-types.\n\nCompared with what NEAR (Eros) and Hayabusa (Itokawa) actually returned, and with the crisp crater hierarchy in bar/refs/mercury_messenger.jpg (bar/refs/ has no asteroid photos, so this is the nearest airless-body reference), the surfaces read as competent CG clay, not rock. Verdict: a competent, well-engineered hobbyist-plus piece with one beautiful unused angle and several visible tells.", "gaps": [{"gap": "Hero and close-pass rock surfaces read as soft plaster, clay or soap rather than rock. They have a pillowy low-frequency undulation, smudge-noise albedo and evenly scattered same-morphology circular pocks with no raised rims, ejecta, overlap or degradation states. Crater floors show as black 'bullet holes'. No boulders, regolith texture or crisp crater hierarchy are visible even at 380-1600 px on screen. These are the chapter's money shots.", "severity": "structural", "evidence": "shots/critic/asteroids/round3/crop_f222_eros.png, x_hero_tumbled_fov8.png, crop_hero_craters.png, crop_closepass_219.5.png, x_itokawa_fov10.png, crop_in40_hero.png", "fix": "Rebuild the per-pixel layer as a real crater size-frequency hierarchy: power-law N(>D) ~ D^-2 across 6+ octaves, saturated so craters overlap, with paraboloid bowls plus raised rims (height ~0.04 D), ejecta falloff, and a random degradation state per crater (fresh, crisp and bright versus soft and shallow). Add triplanar world-space regolith albedo and normal noise 2-3 octaves above mesh resolution, faded by fwidth footprint. Add visible boulders as parallax/height-traced domes with cast shadows, enough of them to read at 300 px. Replace the low-frequency 'pillow' fbm with ridged/eroded noise plus large flat facets. Drive albedo from slope and crater age (bright fresh walls, darker mature flats), not isotropic smudge noise. Give the Itokawa-like hero smooth regolith 'seas' in the neck and dense boulder fields on the lobes."}, {"gap": "In the film the belt chapter is almost entirely front-lit (phase about 15-35 degrees) during the glide. Rocks read as flat grey cut-outs and popcorn. The 'Looking sunward' beat (t about 224-226) is dark and empty, with no haze and no rims. The strongest image the piece can make (backlit swarm against forward-scatter glow) never appears in the film.", "severity": "functional", "evidence": "shots/critic/asteroids/round3/film_sheet.png, film_219.png, film_220.png, film_225.png vs x_sunward140.png", "fix": "Integrator/director: aim the glide's 'sculpt' direction 70-100 degrees from the Sun, not about 155-169 degrees. Hold a 3-4 s sunward beat with the Sun 25-40 degrees off-axis, just outside the frame or behind a beat hero's limb. Piece side: until that lands, raise low-phase legibility with stronger albedo contrast (fresh/mature patches at +-35%), opposition-surge brightening on crater rims, and a small Sun-elevation-independent cavity-AO darkening so front-lit rocks keep form."}, {"gap": "Seen from outside or on arrival, the dense swarm renders as a soft-edged rounded rectangle of glow. That shows the artificial slab envelope at film t=213 and from any off-path camera.", "severity": "functional", "evidence": "shots/critic/asteroids/round3/film_213.png, x_outside_far.png, film_214.png", "fix": "Replace the box/slab glow envelope with a 3D-noise-modulated density: an ellipsoid with exponential tails about 3x longer, plus clumps and filaments from 2-3 octaves of low-frequency fbm and a slight orbital shear. Make the point lattice and glow use the same density function so the integrated light matches the points. Feather the slab edges in both A and B with fbm-perturbed falloff so no straight edge or corner ever reads."}, {"gap": "Fracture-plane cut edges fold into thin layered sheets and overhanging 'paper flaps' (laminated cardboard look) on hero rocks.", "severity": "functional", "evidence": "shots/critic/asteroids/round3/crop_hero_flaps.png, x_hero_tumbled_fov8.png (bottom-left)", "fix": "Apply the plane cuts after all radial displacement, as r = min(r_displaced, planeDistance(dir)) with a smooth-min bevel (k about 0.02 R). Add only low-amplitude detail on the cut face afterwards, so the radial function stays single-valued and monotonic near the cut and cannot fold. Recompute normals and AO after the cut, and reject cuts whose face normal is more than about 70 degrees from radial (these produce slivers)."}, {"gap": "On crescent and backlit rocks the forward-scatter limb term lights every high-frequency ridge. The lit area reads as crumpled foil or contour lines, and rim highlights are 1-px stair-stepped lines that will crawl and shimmer in motion.", "severity": "functional", "evidence": "shots/critic/asteroids/round3/crop_f224_ida.png, crop_f226_itokawa.png, crop_sunward_topleft.png, crop_in90_streak.png", "fix": "Compute the backlit-limb weight from the macro (low-LOD, smoothed) normal against the view vector, not the detail normal. Restrict it to the geometric silhouette band with an fwidth-based AA width (2-3 px). Filter the detail normal by footprint (Toksvig/LEAN: shorten normal length with variance and widen the phase lobe) so sub-pixel ridges average out instead of aliasing. Optionally add a temporal or analytical AA term for the rim."}]}, "sky": {"score": 6.4, "summary": "Round 3 sky review, rendered with tools/shot.mjs (8 required views, 9 isolation/extra-angle views, a 72-frame journey scan, full-resolution warp frames and a sub-pixel jitter test). The sky module loaded with no 'module failed' line in any run.\n\nSTRONG: The catalogue sky is genuinely good. Orion, the Big Dipper (Mizar and Alcor resolved), Crux with Alpha/Beta Centauri, and Scorpius (Antares with the blue rho Oph reflection nebula, Shaula and Lesath) all sit where they should, in the right brightness order. B-V colours read correctly: Betelgeuse, Aldebaran, Dubhe and Gacrux are orange; Rigel, the Belt and Alkaid are blue-white. Bright stars have a tasteful halo. The background field thins out inside dark lanes and is about 5x denser on the band than off it. The Coalsack is the right size and sits in the right place relative to Acrux. Bonus objects exist: M42 with M43 and the Running Man (convincing at fov 5), M31 with its satellites, the LMC, Carina, the Rosette and the lambda Ori ring. The galactic pole has no pinching, Jupiter's limb is clean with no sky bleed, and the assets total 352 KB. The forced warp (skyWarp=0.8) and the film's Jupiter approach (t=228) give a correct relativistic look: stars crowd toward the apex, bluer ahead, with energy-conserving capsule streaks. Measured band brightness is ordered sensibly: Sagittarius 0.14 linear, Cygnus 0.04, Carina/Crux 0.05.\n\nWEAK (why this is not 8+): At the film's working fields of view (20-50 deg), the Milky Way does not look like a photograph. It looks like grey-brown cotton-wool fog with stars sprinkled on top: blobby, cellular dust with no filaments and no granular star-cloud texture. Small dark clouds (Pipe/B72, the CrA cloud) render as pitch-black, hard-edged, lumpy cut-outs. The bulge does not swell: the Sagittarius region is a straight band whose bright ridge is about 7 deg wide. In the film, 4 of the 5 full-warp legs (t=168, 270, 336, 360) look sideways: the frames are murky and half black, filled with 300-px parallel lines over a smeared Milky Way. That reads as camera shake, not as the signature aberration look.\n\nINTEGRATION (not sky-owned, but it dominates the required views): a uniform grey ecliptic band from the asteroid belt module fogs gc.png, wide.png and the top of orion.png; gc_skyonly.png proves the band is not the sky's. A separate shader compile error ('pre' undeclared at line 1088, a raymarched body with gl_FragDepth) appears in the journey scan log. It is not from the sky module.\n\nScore 6.4: an impressive, physically careful sky with clear tells in the Milky Way texture, the bulge and the in-film warp framing. The floor is met: real BSC5 catalogue, Milky Way band on the true galactic plane, warp streaks.", "gaps": [{"gap": "The Milky Way's mid-scale texture is not photographic. At 20-45 deg fov (the film's working range) the diffuse light is smooth grey-brown fog with round, blobby 'cauliflower' dust cells and a uniform sprinkle of similar-size stars on top. Real star clouds (Sagittarius, Scutum, Cygnus) read as dense, grainy fields of unresolved stars cut by filamentary, sharp-but-ragged dark nebulae (see the Goldstone reference). This is the most visible tell in the cold open and in every approach shot with the Milky Way behind a planet.", "severity": "structural", "evidence": "shots/critic/sky/round3/x_sgr_fov20.png, shots/critic/sky/round3/x_cyg_fov25.png, shots/critic/sky/round3/crop_coldopen_2x.png (film t=6), shots/critic/sky/round3/warp/film_6.png, compare bar/refs/milkyway_eso_or_nasa.jpg", "fix": "(a) Move most of the diffuse light into resolved points at narrow fov. Raise RES_F from 0.09-0.14 to about 0.35-0.6 total below fov 30, and make lattice star density follow S_bg x transmission, so bright clouds become a dense grain of mag 11-15 points instead of fog. Fade them back into the smooth term by pixel footprint so wide shots do not alias. (b) Replace the isotropic dust perturbation with anisotropic detail: orient ridged noise along the iso-tau contours (tangent = perpendicular to grad ln tau of the IRAS map), stretched 4-8x along the contour, so lanes become filaments and streamers instead of round cells. (c) Lower DUST sigma (0.8 to about 0.45) and EDGE_K (2.2 to about 1.2) where the footprint is under about 1 arcmin, so cloud edges are crisp but not lumpy."}, {"gap": "[Not sky-owned: asteroids/integration] A bright, uniform, neutral-grey band about 15 deg wide runs along the ecliptic in every view from the Earth's neighbourhood. In the Sagittarius view it is brighter than the Milky Way core. It is still strong at 134 deg elongation (top of the Orion view), where real zodiacal light is near its minimum. It makes the required sky views look like a fogged lens. With only=sky,post,earth the band disappears, so it comes from the asteroid belt ring.", "severity": "functional", "evidence": "shots/critic/sky/round3/gc.png vs shots/critic/sky/round3/gc_skyonly.png; shots/critic/sky/round3/wide.png; shots/critic/sky/round3/orion.png (top third); journey sheet t=198 in shots/critic/sky/round3/journey_scan_sheet.png", "fix": "Route to the asteroids builder. Draw the faint belt ring only when the camera is within about 0.5 AU of the belt, or model it as real zodiacal light: brightness falls with elongation (about 1/10 of the Sagittarius cloud at 90 deg, a faint gegenschein at 180 deg), width grows toward the Sun, and the colour is solar (slightly warm), not grey. Then scale it with U.uSkyGain."}, {"gap": "The galactic bulge does not read as a bulge. Toward Sagittarius/Scorpius/Ophiuchus the band keeps its width. Its bright ridge is about 7 deg (100 px at fov 60) and falls below 3% of peak within about 15 deg of the plane. Real photos show a broad rounded swelling of warm light (|b| up to about 10-15 deg) around l=0, with the Large Sagittarius and Scutum star clouds standing out as the brightest patches in the sky. In wide.png the GC region reads as a dark, dust-dominated stripe.", "severity": "functional", "evidence": "shots/critic/sky/round3/gc_skyonly.png (profile across the band at 800,450: peak 0.139 at -125 px, 0.005 at 0, 0.017 at +100, under 0.004 beyond +-250 px); shots/critic/sky/round3/wide.png", "fix": "Add an analytic boxy bulge to the unextincted light: S_bulge ~ exp(-((l/7deg)^2 + (b/4.5deg)^2)^0.5 * 2.2) at T_BULGE 4300 K. Attenuate it with a reduced screen (for example 0.5 x tau at |b| > 4 deg, since the IRAS column is mostly foreground there), so Baade's window, the Large Sagittarius Star Cloud and M24 punch through as the brightest diffuse light in the sky. Check that at fov 100 the Sagittarius-Scutum region is about 2.5-3x the Cygnus cloud and visibly wider than the band elsewhere."}, {"gap": "In-film warp legs mostly look sideways, and the sky's response there reads as camera-shake motion blur, not the signature aberration look. At t=168, 270 and 360 (warp=1) the apex is off-frame. Streaks are very long (up to the 360 px cap) parallel lines, the Milky Way is a murky smear, and half the frame is nearly black (receding-side dimming exponent DOP_P x 2.6 is about 3.6). Only t=228 (the Jupiter approach) shows the radial crowding toward the travel direction that the brief calls the film's signature.", "severity": "functional", "evidence": "shots/critic/sky/round3/warp/film_270.png, shots/critic/sky/round3/warp/film_360.png, shots/critic/sky/round3/warp/film_168.png vs shots/critic/sky/round3/warp/film_228.png; shots/critic/sky/round3/x_warp_forced.png", "fix": "Sky side: cap streak length to about 4-6% of frame height (not 360 px) and keep a bright Gaussian head so each star stays legible as a point. Temper the receding-side brightness exponent (2.6 to about 1.4) and floor it at about 0.25, so the trailing half reads as reddened, not black. Do not let the Milky Way shell inherit long smears; keep it aberrated but sharp. Director side (request): keep the apex within about 25 deg of frame centre during the warp=1 plateau of every leg, as at t=228."}, {"gap": "Dark nebulae render as opaque black cut-outs. The Pipe stem (B59/B72) is a lumpy, hard-edged black sausage. The Corona Australis cloud is a black smudge with a sharp outline on a faint background, so it reads as a hole in the sky. The Coalsack is a smooth blurred blob with no ragged edge structure. Real dark clouds keep a few foreground stars and have soft gradients of extinction at their edges.", "severity": "cosmetic", "evidence": "shots/critic/sky/round3/crop_sgr_pipe_3x.png, shots/critic/sky/round3/crop_gc_blob_3x.png, shots/critic/sky/round3/crop_crux_3x.png", "fix": "Clamp the visual optical depth of the small-cloud residual to about 2.5-3 (transmission at least 5-8%). Raise F_EXT for high-latitude clouds (CrA, the Pipe and rho Oph are at about 130-150 pc, so a few percent of the field stars are in front). Feather cloud edges with a footprint-aware smoothstep over about 2-4 px, instead of EDGE_K steepening at full strength."}]}, "post": {"score": 7.5, "summary": "Post clearly meets its floor and gets most of the way to its target. I saw no page errors from post and no post fallback. Post GPU cost measured 0.31-0.41 ms at 1600x900 with the flare, warp and letterbox all forced on (log_perf_post.txt), against the 1.5 ms budget; the whole frame averaged 5.4 ms. The required perf run measured 22.8 ms/frame (43.9 fps), but the GPU was heavily shared at the time (log_perf.txt).\n\nStrengths:\n- The Earth orbital sunrise is near studio grade (sunrise/s_t135.5 to s_t136.5, journey/j_t138). An orange, partly occluded starburst sits on the limb, then a white sun rises with a thin anamorphic streak. The flare follows occlusion smoothly.\n- Analytic occlusion works: there is no flare when the Sun is hidden (glare165, Saturn eclipse t=314).\n- Off-frame fade is graceful (x_edge_p8 to x_edge_p11).\n- The point-source sun at Neptune distance is crisp, with tapered spikes (j_t398).\n- The hero-Sun composite matches the HMI colour well (j_t18 vs sun_sdo_hmi_visible.jpg).\n- Dithering works: no banding even at 8x gain (z_nep_term_x8, z_t144_grad_stretch), and an all-black frame dithers to grey levels 0 and 1 only.\n- Letterbox is exact: 230 zero rows, giving 1600x670 = 2.388:1. Bars are true black. Fades work at the head and tail of the film.\n- Warp streaks are continuous and tapered, with no edge fringe (j_t48, j_t236, z_warp236_edge).\n\nTells that keep it below 8:\n- The warp pass aliases thin line art into banding.\n- The ghost chain of grey heptagons repeats in almost every sun shot and reads as an overlay.\n- Veiling-glare energy runs the wrong way: a Sun just outside the frame lifts the blacks more than a blown Sun inside it.\n- The hero Sun switches visibly from white to orange during the reveal.\n- The warp destination is soft at the focus of expansion.\n\nProblems that are not post's:\n- Two green vertical lines in the limb105.8 view are scene content: they are still there with postDebug=raw and with postIG=0, and not in postDebug=flare.\n- The sun module failed once at runtime during a concurrent edit (x_edge_m16: 'MAX_STEPS_FAR is not defined').\n- The beige ecliptic band and the ellipse around the finale Sun are scene content.\n- The required view at phase 176 renders a correct black frame, so it cannot test limb occlusion. I used aim=sun at phase 105.8 instead.\n\nThe director's cut changed during my session (duration went from 446.5 s to 441.5 s). The finale warp with orbit lines (j_t428) comes from the earlier cut. I reproduced the same banding on the current build by forcing postWarp=1 at t=426.", "gaps": [{"gap": "The warp radial blur aliases thin bright line art. Orbit ellipses in the finale smear into wide ribbons with regular hatching and moire stripes plus rainbow fringes. This is not the smooth speed blur the brief asks for.", "severity": "functional", "evidence": "C:/Users/danny/fun/space-animation-v1/shots/critic/post/round3/journey2/j_t428.png, z_t428_orbit.png, z_t428_ring2.png (director warp=1 with orbits, earlier cut); reproduced on the current build with postWarp=1: x_finale_warp1_t426.png, z_finale_warp_orbit.png", "fix": "Tap spacing exceeds 1 px once the trail is longer than 256 px (16 jittered taps x a 16-tap box), so 1-px lines alias. Mip the HDR copy and sample pass 1 with textureLod at log2(uLen*|d|/16 px). Alternatively, add a third 8-tap pass whenever the trail length is over 256 px. Composite non-physical overlays (orbit lines, labels) after post or mask them out of the streak pass. Fade the R/B +-2% split out for anything that is not a point-like highlight."}, {"gap": "The ghost chain reads as a CG overlay. Up to 9 identical 7-blade heptagons, mostly neutral grey with filled, nearly uniform interiors, appear in almost every sun-adjacent shot. The small near-focus ghosts look like solid grey discs. Seen across the whole film, it becomes a repeated 'lens flare plugin' signature rather than a high-end camera.", "severity": "cosmetic", "evidence": "shots/critic/post/round3/journey/j_t130.png, journey/j_t94.png, journey/j_t96.png, journey/j_t144.png, journey2/j_t278.png, journey2/j_t398.png, journey2/j_t422.png, glare150.png, corner.png, z_t96_ghosts.png, z_t398_ghosts.png", "fix": "Show only the 3-5 strongest ghosts per shot. Put most of each ghost's energy in a thin caustic rim, with the interior at about 10-20% of the rim. Give each ghost its own coating hue (green, magenta or amber at saturation 0.3-0.5) instead of neutral grey. Make ghost intensity scale linearly with the Sun's on-screen flux times exposure, so telephoto and far-Sun shots barely ghost. Jitter size and rotation per ghost, and turn the largest into faint defocus annuli."}, {"gap": "Veiling-glare energy is inconsistent with sun position. A blown 1 AU Sun inside the frame leaves Earth's night side at about 0.4/255. Rotating the Sun just outside the frame lifts the same night side to about 10/255 and the frame edge to about 50/255. In the Mercury corner view, empty space on the sun side sits at about 35/255 grey. Real lenses veil most with the source in frame and decay smoothly once it is outside, and space should stay near black.", "severity": "cosmetic", "evidence": "shots/critic/post/round3/glare150.png vs x_edge_p8.png vs x_edge_p11.png (pixel means 0.4 / 1.1 / 10.4 on the night side; 17 / 30 / 50 at the left edge); corner.png", "fix": "Add a frame-wide veiling term proportional to in-frame plus near-frame Sun flux: roughly 0.2-0.5% uniform plus a broad (1+(r/0.7fh)^2)^-1 lobe. Make the off-frame barrel-flare haze continuous with that veil at the edge crossing, and never brighter, then decay it over 5-20 degrees. Soft-cap the barrel haze to about 12-15/255 in display terms."}, {"gap": "The hero-Sun reveal shows a visible colour switch: a blinding white disc at t=12 becomes a deep orange filtered disc by t=14. It reads as a filter being slid in rather than an exposure settling.", "severity": "cosmetic", "evidence": "shots/critic/post/round3/m_intro.png, journey/j_t12.png, journey/j_t14.png", "fix": "Spread the hero weight and filter mix over at least 3 s. First let the shoulder's hue preservation take the disc from white to warm white as the exposure drops, then deepen to the HMI orange. Alternatively, open the shot already exposed and filtered for the disc and let the corona fade up instead."}, {"gap": "The warp destination is soft. Near the focus of expansion, stars and the target glint become 5-8 px blobs instead of crisp points, so the next world does not 'swell from a point of light'.", "severity": "cosmetic", "evidence": "shots/critic/post/round3/z_warp236_ctr.png (from journey2/j_t236.png, warp 0.96)", "fix": "Make the barrel distortion zero-slope at the centre (an r^4 term only, no r^2). Resample the distortion pass with Catmull-Rom instead of bilinear. Confine the +70% warp bloom boost to the periphery with a radial mask. Draw the target glint after the warp so it stays unwarped and sharp."}]}, "director": {"score": 6, "summary": "Round 3, director (camera choreography + glints). I checked the work three ways: contact sheets at all three epochs, dense filmstrips across the departures, arrivals, Earthrise, Titan and the finale, and my own in-browser probe (my scripts, not the builder's dev tools). The probe samples director.evaluate(t) at 60 Hz against the real body modules' positions and moons[].positionAt.\n\nMeasured, and good:\n- The page runs cleanly at 2026, 2027 and 2031: no page errors, no module fallbacks, no NaN.\n- evaluate(t) is pure, including when called out of order.\n- The camera never goes inside a body or moon. Minimum altitudes: Moon 12 km, Mercury 207 km, Earth 399 km, Sun 0.105 R, Jupiter 5,372 km, Titan 2,832 km. Over the B/A rings the camera stays about 0.16 R_S (~10,000 km) off the ring plane.\n- All 12 chapters, captions, labels and audio cues are present. speedC matches the camera's real speed.\n- Arrivals swell from a point at the frame centre.\n- Glints are coloured points that hand over to the disc.\n- Many beats are genuinely beautiful: the Mercury terminator pass, Earth's orbital sunrise, the 2026 Earthrise, the Io shadow with the Great Red Spot, the Phobos crossing, the Saturn eclipse (layout close to the Cassini reference), Triton's twin crescents, and a pale blue dot with a sunbeam.\n\nWhat holds it back is cinematography rather than correctness:\n- About 98 s (22%) of the 441.5 s film shows nothing larger than a 4 px disc. That empty time comes from the same departure formula repeated ten times: the world leaves the frame, a whip pan of 23-30\u00b0/s runs over empty stars for 4-6 s, then the warp.\n- The camera turns faster than 20\u00b0/s for 43.8 s of the film and faster than 12\u00b0/s for 141 s.\n- At every epoch, the subject leaves the frame during its own title card: Titan at 330-333 s and Mercury at 57-58.6 s.\n- The Earth\u2192Moon transfer centres the Sun for about 8 s while the Moon is off-frame, then the Moon slides in from the top edge.\n- The finale is rushed: the labelled-planet map holds about 4 s, the pale blue dot about 4 s, and the credits about 3.5 s. In the map, the Sun's glare swallows the inner orbits.\n- There are two one-frame rotation-rate kicks: at 59.7 s and at 263.13 s.\n\nFloor met; target not yet.", "gaps": [{"gap": "Dead time and a formulaic, repeated transition. About 98 s (22%) of the film has no world larger than a 4 px radius in frame. Every leg (Sun\u2192Mercury 41.5-52, Mercury\u2192Venus 69.8-79.9, Venus\u2192Earth 96.5-107.6, Moon\u2192Mars 169-179, belt\u2192Jupiter 228-239, Jupiter\u2192Saturn 272-285, Titan\u2192Uranus 338-350.5, Uranus\u2192Neptune 365-376) runs the same pattern: the departing world leaves the frame within 1-2 s, then 4-6 s of starfield, then 2-3 s of warp, then 3-4 s of a growing point. 13 of the 45 frames on the 10 s contact sheet are near-empty starfield.", "severity": "structural", "evidence": "C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\shots\\critic\\director\\round3\\sheet.png (t=50,70,80,100,110,170,180,230,240,320,340,350,370); C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\shots\\critic\\director\\round3\\strip_sun_departure.png (41-53 s); C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\shots\\critic\\director\\round3\\strip_moon_mars.png (168.5-180 s); C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\shots\\critic\\director\\round3\\probe_2026.json (deadSpans); the same pattern appears in sheet_2027.png and sheet_2031.png", "fix": "Overlap the legs instead of running them in series. During the dwell, frame the departing world over the shoulder (keep its crescent or limb in frame on a 1/3 line) with the destination glint already in the same frame, and turn only as far as needed. Alternatively, drop the pan entirely and cut under a 0.4-0.6 s dip to black (the brief allows deliberate cuts under a fade) straight into the warp. Shorten each warp-plus-swell to about 5 s. That recovers roughly 40-50 s, to spend on the finale and the hero holds."}, {"gap": "Whip pans everywhere. Every departure turns at 23-30\u00b0/s, sustained 4-6 s at a 52-54\u00b0 FOV: about 0.5 FOV per second, roughly 120\u00b0 in one move (e.g. 2026 at 39.8-45.7, 94.9-101.3, 167.4-173 (with -7\u00b0/s roll), 364.4-368.9, 385-392). Over the whole film the camera turns faster than 20\u00b0/s for 43.8 s and faster than 12\u00b0/s for 141 s (32% of the runtime). On a dome or a large screen this reads as a hand-held whip, and it would cause nausea.", "severity": "structural", "evidence": "C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\shots\\critic\\director\\round3\\probe_2026.json (wArr), with fast-turn spans and pan/roll decomposition measured by the critic's own probe scripts (results not saved under round3); C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\shots\\critic\\director\\round3\\strip_moon_mars.png; C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\shots\\critic\\director\\round3\\strip_jup_sat.png", "fix": "Cap the authored rotation at about 8-10\u00b0/s, or 0.15-0.2 FOV/s, in legAim. Start the turn earlier, inside the departing block, while the world is still the subject. Combine it with lateral translation so most of the change of view comes from parallax, not rotation. Where a larger re-aim is unavoidable, do it under a fade or behind the departing world's limb."}, {"gap": "The Titan flyby loses its subject at every epoch. After entering at the lower right (328-329 s), Titan jumps to the top-right corner, sits under the telemetry panel (330 s), and is almost entirely out of frame at 331-332 s while the TITAN title card is up. It re-enters from the top left at 333 s. This repeats at 2026, 2027 and 2031 (the critic's probe flagged the focus off-frame from about 330.2 to 336.4 s at all three).", "severity": "functional", "evidence": "C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\shots\\critic\\director\\round3\\strip_titan_2026.png; C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\shots\\critic\\director\\round3\\strip_titan_2027.png; C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\shots\\critic\\director\\round3\\extra\\e2027_330.png; C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\shots\\critic\\director\\round3\\sheet_2031.png (t=330)", "fix": "The approach is too fast for its range (16,000 km/s at 14,000 km altitude), and the aim lags Titan's angular rate. Add feed-forward to the aim: target the predicted position from the relative velocity, or lock exactly when Titan's angular rate exceeds the fluid head's cap. Slow the closest approach (log-distance easing to about 2,000 km/s). Add a per-frame safe-frame constraint that keeps the target centre within |NDC| < 0.65 and outside the HUD rectangles. Check it at every epoch with an automated focus-in-frame test."}, {"gap": "Mercury slides out of the frame during its title card. At 57.1-58.6 s the disc centre reaches NDC x = 1.12 (the right edge) while the left 80% of the frame shows empty stars under the MERCURY card. The pan briefly hits 28\u00b0/s and then swings back. This happens at all three epochs.", "severity": "functional", "evidence": "C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\shots\\critic\\director\\round3\\extra\\e2026_57.5.png; C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\shots\\critic\\director\\round3\\extra\\e2026_58.png; C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\shots\\critic\\director\\round3\\strip_mercury_arrival.png (57-58.5 s)", "fix": "Blend in the Sun-side aim offset (the half-lit, Sun-just-off-frame composition) as a function of the disc's size, never past an NDC limit of about 0.6 for the disc centre. Use a C2 smootherstep for the blend and hold the planet at the 2/3 vertical line while its card is up."}, {"gap": "The Earth\u2192Moon transfer is unmotivated, and the destination is missing. From 138.5 to 142 s the frame is just the Sun at the centre with lens-flare ghosts. Then the Sun exits at the bottom and nothing is in frame until 146 s, when the Moon slides in from the top edge already about 70 px in radius. There is no Moon swelling from a point, and at 2027 the 140 s frame is just grey glare and stars.", "severity": "functional", "evidence": "C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\shots\\critic\\director\\round3\\strip_earth_moon.png; C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\shots\\critic\\director\\round3\\extra\\e2026_140.png; C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\shots\\critic\\director\\round3\\extra\\e2027_140.png", "fix": "Aim at the Moon from the start of the transfer, using the same log-distance arrival law as the planets: the Moon's glint or dark disc near the centre, growing at a constant apparent rate. Keep the Sun at least 0.6 NDC off-centre or outside the frame. If the Moon is near the Sun (close to new moon), frame its dark disc against the corona glow on purpose and say so in a caption."}]}, "hud": {"score": 8, "summary": "Round 3 HUD review. Everything required renders: the splash, title cards, captions, telemetry, labels, timeline and controls, the closing card, reduced motion, the font fallback, and portrait, landscape-phone, ultrawide and 4K layouts. No console errors and no module fallback appeared in any run. Fonts load (FLH Jost 200-600 and FLH Cormorant italic both report \"loaded\"), and fonts=0 falls back cleanly to Segoe/Georgia-style faces.\n\nA DOM probe every 4 s across the film found no text overflowing the viewport at 1600x900 or 390x844. Its only overlap hits were line-box touches between stacked telemetry lines, with no visible collision.\n\nA live interaction probe passed these checks:\n- Begin starts playback and hides the splash.\n- Controls auto-hide after 3 s idle and the cursor hides.\n- Space toggles play/pause.\n- Left/Right jump to the previous/next chapter.\n- M toggles mute.\n- A click on the timeline seeks to the right time.\n- Dragging scrubs, pausing while dragging.\n- F enters fullscreen.\n\nThe script's H check misfired, but the screenshot shows H works (\"TITLES AND TELEMETRY HIDDEN \u00b7 H TO RESTORE\"). The tap check was inconclusive because the controls were already showing.\n\nDesign quality is high. Thin wide-tracked Jost titles and Cormorant italic subtitles, hairline rules, tabular telemetry and a linear light-time ruler marked with each planet's distance all read like a planetarium title package. The splash orrery shows the real configuration and is captioned \"radial scale logarithmic\". Card placement avoids the subject almost everywhere. Captions over the photosphere and the required Venus dist=2.2 view stay legible thanks to tinted per-line shadows.\n\nThe tells that keep it below a true SVS/planetarium finish:\n- The finale's \"planets as labelled points\" beat is thin. Neptune sits under the title card and its label is suppressed. Mercury, Venus and Mars are never labelled, and labels exist for only about 3.5 s.\n- Telemetry sits on the subject in several shots (Titan, Mars's polar cap, Saturn's rings).\n- During the 13 s cold open, the current-chapter label on the timeline collides with \"THE SUN\" at widths of 1280 px and below.\n- Facts are readable for only about 3 s per card.\n- Legibility fails on saturated white backgrounds (view=body Sun fill, close Venus fill).\n- Bright stars show through glyphs.\n- The finale chapter has three different names.\n- Moon cards have no facts row.", "gaps": [{"gap": "Finale's 'planets as labelled points' beat is thin, and the HUD's own card covers part of it. At t=426 the 'THE SOLAR SYSTEM' card sits on Neptune's glint, just under 'SYSTEM' near x=1470,y=615. Neptune's label is suppressed (labelStats [9,5,{faded:1}]) and its orbit runs through the title. Mercury, Venus and Mars are never labelled. Earth's label sits inside the Sun's glare at low contrast. Labels exist only from about 425 to 428 s; at 430 labelStats is [0,0] and at 435 only Earth's reticle shows, with no name.", "severity": "functional", "evidence": "shots/critic/hud/round3/cards/k_t426.png, shots/critic/hud/round3/zoom_finale_neptune_cmp.png, shots/critic/hud/round3/extra/finale_hud0_t426.png, shots/critic/hud/round3/finale_sheet.png, shots/critic/hud/round3/extra/uw_t426.png, shots/critic/hud/round3/zoom_finale_sunlabels.png", "fix": "In the composer, treat projected shot.labels points as hard obstacles with a 30u keep-out, not only fade targets, so the finale card cannot land on a planet. Candidates: lower-left below Jupiter's ellipse, centre-bottom, or inside the letterbox bar. Fan Mercury, Venus and Mars out of the glare disc on radial hairline leaders into a small stacked label column. Hold all planet labels from 424 s through the Earth caption (431-437 s), and keep Earth's name on its reticle at 435 s. Give glare-embedded names a dark 0.5 alpha capsule or a stronger halo."}, {"gap": "Telemetry block repeatedly sits on the subject. It covers Titan's disc during the Titan arrival (t=330; the 'UTC' key and the faint 'TITAN' reticle disappear into the disc), Mars's bright polar cap ('TARGET' key nearly invisible at t=185.75), and Saturn's lit rings (t=300). The 0.45-coverage dimming leaves it both on top of the subject and hard to read.", "severity": "cosmetic", "evidence": "shots/critic/hud/round3/journey/j_t330.png, shots/critic/hud/round3/anim/mars_t185.75.png, shots/critic/hud/round3/controls/ctl_t300.png", "fix": "When a body disc covers more than about 30% of the telemetry box for more than 1 s of a beat (the composer already samples the director path), swap telemetry to the top-left corner or collapse it to the one-line short form for that beat, with a 0.6 s cross-fade. Otherwise add a feathered dark scrim (0.45 alpha) sized to the block, and raise key labels to at least 0.8 alpha whenever coverage is above 0."}, {"gap": "Timeline label collision in the cold open. With the controls shown during the opening chapter (0-13 s), the bold clamped current label 'OPENING' overlaps 'THE SUN' at 844x390 ('OPENINGE SUN') and touches it with zero gap at 1280x720. 1600x900 is fine, and other chapters are fine at narrow widths.", "severity": "functional", "evidence": "shots/critic/hud/round3/zoom_ctl_opening_sizes.png, shots/critic/hud/round3/zoom_land6_timeline.png, shots/critic/hud/round3/land/l_t6.png, shots/critic/hud/round3/controls/ctl_720_t6.png", "fix": "After clamping the current chapter's label inside the track, run a 1-D interval pass over all visible segment labels with a minimum gap of 10u. The current label wins; any neighbour it intersects falls back to TIMELINE_SHORT ('SUN') and, if it still collides, hides. Alternatively, suppress the 'Opening' segment label, since the transport row already shows 'OPENING'."}, {"gap": "Title-card facts stay on screen too briefly. The name and line come first, then the facts fade in one column at a time from about +2 s, and the card exits early as the subject approaches. Mars's facts are fully visible only from about 184.5 to 187 s. Uranus's third fact is still absent at +2.5 s, and Mercury's and Jupiter's second facts are half-transparent at the sampled times. At a 6.5-7.5 s beat this is under 3 s of comfortable reading for three facts.", "severity": "cosmetic", "evidence": "shots/critic/hud/round3/anim_mars_sheet.png, shots/critic/hud/round3/cards/k_t354.png, shots/critic/hud/round3/cards/k_t244.png, shots/critic/hud/round3/cards/k_t56.png", "fix": "Compress the fact stagger to at most 0.15 s per column and start facts at +1.2 s so they are all solid by +2 s. Guarantee at least 4.5 s of full-opacity facts. Where an early exit would cut that short, drop to the 2-fact form, or ask the director (notes request) for 8 s minimum body beats."}, {"gap": "Bright stars and point sources show through glyphs and label text, breaking letterforms. Examples: the 'JUPITER / Arriving' label at t=240 ('JU*ITER'), the 'MERCURY' title on phone at t=56, 'TITANIA' at t=354, and the stars inside '2026' and 'up to 7' in cards and telemetry.", "severity": "cosmetic", "evidence": "shots/critic/hud/round3/journey/j_t240.png, shots/critic/hud/round3/mobile/m_t56.png, shots/critic/hud/round3/cards/k_t354.png, shots/critic/hud/round3/anim/mars_t185.75.png", "fix": "For projected labels, sample the light meter in a small box around the label (it already reads the frame) and, if any pixel exceeds about 0.5 luminance, try the mirrored side or leader offsets before accepting. For titles, add a tight 1px dark inner text-shadow (rgba(0,0,0,.55)) under the existing halo so point sources cannot fill the strokes. Thin weight-200 Jost is especially vulnerable."}]}, "audio": {"score": 6, "summary": "Round 3, audio. The engineering is excellent and the music is good but not exceptional. I rendered the score myself (render.mjs into shots/critic/audio/round3) and checked it with my own BS.1770 / true-peak / spectral script. Results: true peak -2.34 dBTP, integrated -18.0 LUFS, LRA about 10 LU, DC about 4e-6, 0 NaN or subnormal samples. My detector found no clicks, and the only silences are the fade from black (0-2 s) and the tail after 446 s. The 10 warp legs show up as broadband risers. Each chapter has its own key (the chroma top pitch classes follow the harmonic plan) and a centroid from 280 Hz to 1.9 kHz. On the live page shot.mjs --t 10 loaded with no errors and no module failure. live.mjs gave 0% underruns at all 6 dense moments, and pause, auto-suspend, resume, mute, a 25-seek scrub and the composed ending all behaved correctly. The code is good: seeded and deterministic, per-category plus global voice caps (offline peak 130 voices), sweeps that disconnect finished voices, generations that crossfade, and ramped pause and mute.\n\nThe weaknesses are in the music:\n1. **Melody is mostly fragments.** About 31 of the 45 melodic events are 2-3 notes long. The longest statement is 6 notes, and nearly every phrase lasts 3-7 s inside a single chord, so there is never a long, singable line.\n2. **Register and harmonic rhythm barely change.** The voice-led line that the pads and choir read from tops out around E4 in 8 of 12 worlds and never goes above A4. Chords change every 6-9 s in almost every world. As a result the Sun, Earth, Mars, Saturn and finale have nearly the same long-term spectrum (self-similarity at or above 0.9).\n3. **The lead instruments sound synthetic.** The piano is one cached buffer per pitch, with the same timbre at every velocity. The solo voice is a fixed formant filter over a wavetable.\n4. **The warp legs repeat one gesture.** All 9 are the same hiss column over a held chord. The loudest moment of the film is the Jupiter-to-Saturn riser (-10.9 LUFS short-term), not a musical climax. The builder's own render now exits 3 on the current 441.5 s cut (leg dip -1.2 dB, crest +5.3 dB, two melody notes under +6 dB of salience).\n\nSmaller issues: each seek dips 5-11 dB for about 150 ms. Earth, Mars, Saturn and the finale have a presence hole around 2 kHz. The bass line sits in octave 1. live.mjs compares the ending against a stale WAV. Overall this reads as an impressive, well-mastered generative ambient synth score. It does not yet sound like a composed planetarium score.", "gaps": [{"gap": "The melodic writing is mostly fragments and never builds a long line. Of the 45 melodic events, about 31 are 2-3-note fragments: SIXTH about 14 times, L_HEAD about 10, B about 8. The longest statement is 6 notes (A_EXT at the eclipse), and almost every phrase lasts 3-7 s inside one chord. The emotional anchors (Earth arrival, Earthrise, eclipse, pale blue dot) each get a 4-5-note cell, then the texture returns to pads with bell and glass dabs. With nothing to follow over 7.4 minutes, the score reads as ambient wallpaper rather than an emotional film score, and this hurts the film more than anything else in the audio.", "severity": "structural", "evidence": "shots/critic/audio/round3/audio-report.md (Thematic plan table: event durations 2.5-7 s, note counts 2-6); shots/critic/audio/round3/zoom_earth_arrival.png and zoom_finale_end.png (the motif is 4 notes, then held pads); src/audio/worlds.js `lines` (each phrase is attached to one chord index with fit:true)", "fix": "Let a line span several chords. Add a `span: [chordFrom, chordTo]` mode to realizeLines/emitLines and write 3 real tunes of 8-16 bars with an antecedent and a consequent: (a) Earth arrival, piano A theme spun out over Dmaj9-Gmaj9-Bm9-Gm6/9 and answered by a cello-range countermelody; (b) the Saturn eclipse, with the choir soprano carrying A_EXT into a second phrase that climbs to the high point of the film; (c) the finale, a full restatement with the countermelody and a fuller orchestration growing across the last 30 s. Keep the fragments as connective tissue between these tunes, not as the main melodic content. Add a check to compose-check: at least 3 phrases of 8 or more notes and at least 12 s each."}, {"gap": "Register, harmonic rhythm and texture barely change from world to world. The voice-led line that every pad and choir layer reads from keeps its top voice at a median of E4 in 8 of 12 worlds and never goes above A4 (Jupiter's top is C4, Saturn's F#4). The choir sings indices of the same voicing, so Saturn's 'ethereal choir' sits at G#3-F#4, a tenor/alto register. Chords change every 6-9 s in 11 of 12 worlds (chordDur 6-9). The minimal-motion cost keeps the line parked in one octave and a half for the whole film. So, apart from the key changes, the Sun, Earth, Mars, Saturn and finale share nearly the same long-term spectral envelope.", "severity": "functional", "evidence": "shots/critic/audio/round3/critic_selfsim.png (Sun, Earth, Mars, Saturn and finale blocks at 0.9 or above; only Mercury, Jupiter and Uranus stand apart); shots/critic/audio/round3/audio-report.md 'Music sections and harmony' voicings (D3-F#4 almost everywhere); src/audio/worlds.js chordDur lines 87-531", "fix": "Give each world a register target in voiceChord. Pass a per-world {lo, hi, centre} and add a cost for distance from the centre, so the line can move between worlds: Jupiter open and spread A1-C4 with a 10th at the bottom; Saturn choir oct +12 (C5-B5) with a soprano descant; Uranus close and high (top at C6 or above, nothing under C3); Neptune wide, with an octave gap in the middle. Vary harmonic rhythm on purpose: Mercury 3-4 s side-slips, Jupiter 12-16 s pedals, Saturn 10 s, and a finale that speeds up toward the V-I. Add a check that the top-voice median differs by at least a minor 3rd between neighbouring worlds."}, {"gap": "The lead instruments have synthetic tells. The felt piano that carries the main motif (Earth, Mars, Neptune, finale) is one cached buffer per MIDI pitch: Engine.pianoBuffer calls pianoNote(ctx, m) with a fixed seed. Every repeat of a pitch is therefore bit-identical, velocity only scales gain (no extra brightness at higher velocity), there is no soundboard or body resonance, and the release is a linear 0.35 s gain ramp with no damper. The solo voice (the Sun's L, Earthrise, finale) is a static periodic wavetable through a fixed formant bank, with the same -28 cent / 130 ms scoop on every note. In an exposed solo these are exactly the cues that mark an instrument as synthetic.", "severity": "functional", "evidence": "src/audio/engine.js:197-204 (pianoBuffer cache keyed by pitch only); src/audio/dsp.js:202 pianoNote (no velocity argument); src/audio/instruments.js:275-293 (gain-only velocity, linear release) and 164-210 (choir/solo voice); shots/critic/audio/round3/zoom_earth_arrival.png (pure sinusoidal partials, no noise or body component)", "fix": "Piano: build 3 velocity layers per pitch by varying the hammer roll-off (exp(-n/k), k from 3.5 to 8 with velocity) and the attack time (8 ms to 3 ms), plus 2-3 round-robin seeds. Add a short generated soundboard body (a 12-20-mode resonator bank at 90-600 Hz, convolved once per buffer), a 20-40 ms damper-felt noise burst, and faster high-partial decay at release in place of the linear gain ramp. Voice: glide the formants between vowels on each note, scale the scoop depth and time with the interval, add a 30-60 ms breathy onset, and add slow per-note pitch drift (2-4 cents)."}, {"gap": "The warp legs are one gesture repeated 9 times, and one of them is the loudest moment of the film. Every leg is a 4-12 kHz decorrelated noise column about 3 s wide that stops hard at the arrival, over a held chord: the pad partials run unchanged through every leg, so the music never takes part in the travel. The Jupiter-to-Saturn riser reaches -10.9 LUFS short-term, louder than Earthrise (-12) and the finale (-11.5), and Saturn then settles 9.6 LU below it. The builder's leg calibration (constant per-world riserTrim) no longer fits the current 441.5 s cut: render.mjs exits 3 with a worst leg dip of -1.2 dB and a crest of +5.3 dB (Saturn-to-Uranus).", "severity": "functional", "evidence": "shots/critic/audio/round3/legs_side_by_side.png; shots/critic/audio/round3/zoom_leg_jupiter_saturn.png; shots/critic/audio/round3/render.log (3 FAIL lines, EXIT 3); audio-report.md Warp legs table (leg 273: leg ST max -10.9, leg - settled 9.6)", "fix": "Make the harmony move during the leg: write a 2-3-chord pivot sequence per leg (rising by step or by thirds) that resolves into the arrival chord, so the reveal lands as a cadence. Vary the gesture between legs, picking one per leg from a set: noise riser; reverse-choir swell; drop to near silence with one sustained high tone; granular star-streak arpeggio with a tempo accelerando. Compute riserTrim at compose time from the measured pre-leg lane loudness instead of constants. Cap each leg's short-term loudness at the next world's planned bloom minus 2 LU, and keep the film's top 3 short-term loudness moments for anchors (Earthrise, eclipse, pale blue dot)."}, {"gap": "A seek is a dip-and-swell, not a crossfade. At each simulated seek the output drops 5-11 dB for about 100-200 ms: the old generation is retired with a time constant of fade/7 = 86 ms, while the new one fades in over about 0.5 s. There is no click (no >8 kHz spike), but a scrub on the timeline will sound like a brief dropout. Separately, after a 3 s main-thread stall the score resynced 4 times (4 crossfades) and came back 2.7 dB lower.", "severity": "cosmetic", "evidence": "shots/critic/audio/round3/seek/seek-test.wav (50 ms RMS at the ctx 8 s seek: -28.7 to -36.6 to -24 dB; at 14 s: -19.9 to -30.9 dB); shots/critic/audio/round3/seek-report.md; shots/critic/audio/round3/live.log (stall test: resyncs 4, afterDb -25.7 vs -23)", "fix": "Use an equal-power crossfade. Retire the old generation with a linear or cosine ramp over the same 0.4-0.5 s that the new one fades in, starting at the same time instant, instead of a setTargetAtTime with tau = fade/7. After a stall, re-anchor once with one crossfade and suppress further resyncs for 5 s."}]}}
const TRI = {"director": {"piece": "director", "decision": "continue", "rounds": 2, "focus": ["Dead time and whip pans come from one repeated departure formula (board #1 and #2, same mechanism). About 98 s (22%) of the film has no world larger than 4 px, and the camera turns faster than 20 deg/s for 43.8 s. The lead's sheet agrees: 15 of 56 frames are empty starfield or bare glints (t=48, 72, 80, 104, 144, 176, 208, 232, 240, 280, 320, 344, 352, 368, 376). Overlap the legs: keep the departing world's limb or crescent on a third line with the destination glint already in frame, cap authored rotation at about 8-10 deg/s, or cut under a short dip to black. Shorten warp plus swell to about 5 s. This is the biggest thing a viewer feels: a quarter of the film is waiting.", "Subjects leave the frame during their own beat (board #3-#5). Titan is off-frame from 330.2 to 336.4 s under its own title card. Mercury slides to NDC x=1.12 at 57.1-58.6 s. The Earth-to-Moon transfer centres the Sun with no Moon from 138.5 to 146 s (sheet t=144 is empty), then the Moon slides in already 70 px wide. Add a safe-frame constraint (target centre within |NDC| < 0.65 and outside the HUD rects), feed-forward aim for fast flybys, a log-distance arrival for the Moon, and an automated focus-in-frame test at all three epochs. Also remove the one-frame rotation kicks at 59.7 s and 263.13 s.", "The finale is rushed (summary). The labelled-planet map holds about 4 s, the pale blue dot about 4 s, the credits about 3.5 s, and the Sun's glare swallows the inner orbits. The finale is one of the goal's named hero moments. Spend the 40-50 s recovered from item 1 there (map and pale blue dot at least 8 s each, readable credits) and on the hero holds (Earthrise, Saturn eclipse). Publish the new chapter/cue table early in round 1 so audio and HUD can retime against it."], "redirect_criterion": null, "film_window": "Whole film, 0-441.5 s. Dead spans: 41.5-52, 69.8-79.9, 96.5-107.6, 138.5-146, 169-179, 228-239, 272-285, 338-350.5, 365-376. Framing failures: 57-58.6, 138.5-146, 328-337. Finale: 414-441.5.", "why": "Critical, final 6.0, down from 6.5 in round 2. Pacing and shot design are what the goal rests on most, and this piece frames, exposes and times every other piece's hero shot. It also has to absorb the inbound requests in cross_piece: warp apex framing, belt glide lighting, Venus crescent EV, Earthrise sun elevation, 8 s body beats, and no thin line art under warp. Two rounds, and it starts first because HUD and audio retime against its cut."}, "sun": {"piece": "sun", "decision": "continue", "rounds": 2, "focus": ["Prominences read as neon tubes or light-painting (board #1, structural): saturated hot-pink strands with white cores, a constant tube width, sharp edges, no diffuse envelope and no fragmentation. They are on the limb in every hero frame from t=18 to 40 (visible in the sheet at t=24 and t=32). Shift toward eclipse red with soft-clipped cores, fragment the strands along their arc length, add a diffuse envelope, and integrate a Gaussian radial profile.", "Sunspots are geometric bullseyes laid out in straight, evenly spaced rows (board #2). The rows read even at thumbnail scale in the sheet at t=24 (full disc) and t=40 (skim, spots along the limb). Use irregular lobed penumbrae, multi-core umbrae, a Joy's-law group layout with offsets and fragmented followers, and a full-disc LOD that is not a donut.", "The granulation's hue swing is too strong, and there is no meso- or supergranular modulation (board #3): lemon cells on orange lanes, and cheese-like blobs close in. Granulation fills the frame through the photosphere beat and under the skim (t=28-44). The fix is cheap (CHROMA about 1 at granulation scale plus a 2-4% meso/supergranular luminance modulation) and covers a large on-screen area. If round 2 has room, the skim sky is next (board #4): a flat grey 'lifted corona' fog over half the t=40 frame and a uniform spicule comb."], "redirect_criterion": null, "film_window": "Hero 12-45 s: 16 wide corona, 24 full disc, 28-36 close photosphere, 36-44 skim. As a point source or glare: 128-140 (Earth sunrise), 270-274 (Jupiter departure), 390-400 (Neptune), 414-436 (finale).", "why": "Critical, 6.5. It is the film's first hero image and holds about 33 s of continuous screen time at peak attention. Its tells sit exactly in the hero close-ups the film uses. Two rounds."}, "earth": {"piece": "earth", "decision": "continue", "rounds": 2, "focus": ["The hero Blue Marble disc is washed out next to Apollo 17 (board #2): deep-ocean p5 is 71 vs 32, cloud p99 is 210 vs 244, and the ocean is steel-blue. This is the film's signature Earth image (t=116-124, sheet t=120) and the most-seen Earth frame. The work is calibration: top-of-atmosphere ocean reflectance, a check that aerial perspective is not double-counted, thick-cloud albedo, and a histogram match against the reference.", "The Earthrise lunar foreground looks stamped (board #5): near-identical round bowls with black, soft interiors, scattered evenly, on stipple regolith. Earthrise is a named hero moment, and the ground fills the lower half of the frame for about 14 s. In the sheet at t=152 and t=160 the dark round decals read even at thumbnail scale. Use a power-law crater size distribution, morphology that changes with diameter, a per-crater degradation state, and shading from the height field instead of dark ellipses.", "Clouds look procedural at close range (board #1, structural): sponge-like decks, soft blobs at 255 km, and comb-like rows of dashes. They show in the low-orbit beats at t=126-138 (terminator and night pass, orbital sunrise). Fix the dash periodicity and the grey-hole sponge first, since those read at film distance, before per-cumulus shadow marching."], "redirect_criterion": "Three required views show nothing of their feature (a black sunrise, the glint off-frame, Earthrise in lunar night). That is view geometry, not shading. Judge sunrise, glint and Earthrise at the film's geometry (t=116-124, 134-138, 150-162) and at working body views (aim=sun el 74.5; dist=2.2 for the glint), and do not score the empty required views as feature failures. Also disable the asteroid belt ring (only=...) until the asteroids fix lands: the grey fog over the Earth and Moon frames is that ring, not the sky's zodiacal light.", "film_window": "108-170 s: 110-114 approach, 116-124 Blue Marble, 126-138 night pass and orbital sunrise, 138-146 Moon transfer (director-owned framing), 148-162 Earthrise, 164-170 Moon departure disc.", "why": "Critical, 6.5. It is the longest chapter (about 60 s) and holds two named hero moments, the Blue Marble and Earthrise. The night pass and sunrise are already near reference, so effort goes to the disc calibration and the Earthrise ground. Terminator colour and the glint are next if a round has room. Two rounds."}, "jupiter": {"piece": "jupiter", "decision": "continue", "rounds": 2, "focus": ["Limb darkening is too weak, so the globe looks flat-lit (board #3): the sun-side limb holds about 92% of centre brightness, where Hubble falls to about 25%. The root cause is already found: Minnaert k < 1 combined with pow(mu, k-1) brightens toward the limb. Every Jupiter frame in the film is a disc (t=244-262), so this is the fix with the most viewer time, and it is cheap.", "The GRS is a crisp 'fried egg' decal at disc scale (dark-brick rim, radial salmon fill, uniform white collar) and a sponge or fingerprint at 1.12-1.6 R (board #2). It sits on the disc in the film's best Jupiter frames (Io shadow + GRS, t=252-260). Sample detail in the streamline frame, perturb the outline, cut and break up the rim, and use the Cassini GRS patch. Apply the same fixes to Oval BA.", "The film's low skim (t=262-268, sheet t=264) reads as marbled paper smeared along the view direction, with no relief, towers or shadows (board #1, structural). Judge and fix it at the film's skim camera first. Start with anisotropic sampling (textureGrad with the Jacobian) to kill the smear, then colour variance and aerial perspective, then POM height and self-shadow if round 2 allows. This is the target's 'detail holds in the low skim', and the skim fills the frame."], "redirect_criterion": null, "film_window": "236-275 s: 240 glint, 244-250 moons strung out with card, 252-260 Io shadow + GRS, 262-268 low skim, 270-274 departure crescent (small, at frame right).", "why": "Critical and the lowest-scoring critical (5.5), and a named hero world. It is about 7 at disc scale and about 4 close up, so round 1 takes the cheap, high-coverage disc fixes (limb, GRS) and round 2 takes the skim. The departure crescent and festoons rank lower because the crescent is small in the film frame. Two rounds."}, "saturn": {"piece": "saturn", "decision": "continue", "rounds": 2, "focus": ["The globe banding reads as vector illustration (board #1): flat posterized fills with wobbly hard edges and 'paint-drip' hooks. It shows in every hero shot: approach t=290-300, skim t=300-308, and the globe behind the eclipse. The root cause is identified: the vortex domain rotation deflects the band-LUT latitude. Leave the LUT lookup unrotated, set a minimum edge width, add fine latitude striations, and compress the band contrast.", "The ring skim is weak (board #2). Over the unlit face there is no Saturnshine, so the lower half of the frame is a void. Over the lit face the near field reads as brushed metal, with no clumping, wakes or density waves. The skim is a section 5 beat (t~300-308). Do Saturnshine first, since it turns a black half-frame into an image, then the near-field straw and wake texture.", "The lit-face C ring is about 4x too dark (board #4): about 20/255 against 94 for the B ring, so it reads as a void with stars showing through instead of a translucent band. It is in every approach frame above the rings (t=290-300). The fix is cheap photometry."], "redirect_criterion": "Two items should not mask the rest. (a) In 2026 the hexagon sits in polar night, which is physically correct; the critic could not see it even at x4 exposure. Weigh it only if the builder adds twilight visibility or the director features it, and do not let its absence dominate the score. (b) The required ring-skim query (dist=1.9, phase=50, el=-1.2) sits 0.82 R below the ring plane, so it is not a skim. Judge the skim at the film's own camera (about 0.16 R_S off the plane over the B and A rings, t~300-308) and at phase=50&el=23 (lit face, 0.05 R) and el=26 (unlit face, 0.05 R).", "film_window": "284-338 s: 288 card (small disc), 290-300 approach above the rings, 300-308 ring skim and close globe, 308-316 eclipse, 318-322 departure, 326-338 Titan (flyby framing is director-owned).", "why": "Critical, 6.8, and a named hero (rings and eclipse). The eclipse is already close to reference, so effort goes to the globe and ring frames around it, which take up most of Saturn's roughly 50 s. The film's camera does not reach the 0.25 R close-deck artifacts or the moon close-ups. Two rounds."}, "sky": {"piece": "sky", "decision": "continue", "rounds": 2, "focus": ["The Milky Way's mid-scale texture is grey-brown cotton-wool fog with blobby 'cauliflower' dust and a uniform sprinkle of stars, not grainy star clouds cut by filamentary lanes (board #1, structural). It sits behind the cold open and every starfield frame, about a quarter of the sheet, and is prominent behind the Jupiter departure at t=272. Move more light into resolved points at narrow fov, run anisotropic dust along the iso-tau contours, and make cloud edges crisper.", "The warp legs look sideways (board #4). At t=168, 270 and 360 the apex is off-frame, streaks run to the 360 px cap, the Milky Way smears and half the frame goes black. Sky-side fixes: cap streak length at 4-6% of frame height with a bright head, temper the receding-side exponent (2.6 to about 1.4, floored at 0.25), and keep the Milky Way aberrated but sharp. The apex framing belongs to the director (cross_piece). Each leg lasts several seconds and repeats about 9 times.", "The galactic bulge does not swell (board #3): the Sagittarius ridge is about 7 deg wide, and the galactic centre reads as a dark stripe. Add the analytic boxy bulge behind a reduced foreground screen so the Sagittarius and Scutum clouds become the brightest diffuse light. It matters in any shot with the galactic centre region behind it."], "redirect_criterion": "The grey band about 15 deg wide in gc.png, wide.png and orion.png is the asteroid belt ring (gc_skyonly.png proves it). Until the asteroids fix lands, judge the sky with only=sky,post plus the body in view, and do not let the band mask the Milky Way work.", "film_window": "Whole film. Dominant in the cold open (0-13 s) and every transition or starfield frame (sheet t=48, 72-80, 104, 144, 208, 232-240, 320, 344-352, 368-376). Warp legs at about 168-178, 270-282, 336-346 and 360-372, plus the director's other legs. Milky Way behind the Jupiter departure at about 270-274.", "why": "Critical, 6.4. Once the director cuts dead time the sky will be on screen less, but it is still the backdrop of every frame, and the cold open is the first image. The catalogue stars are already strong. Two rounds, because the Milky Way texture is structural."}, "post": {"piece": "post", "decision": "continue", "rounds": 1, "focus": ["The ghost chain reads as a CG overlay (board #2): up to 9 identical grey heptagons in almost every sun-adjacent shot (critic evidence at t=94, 96, 130, 144, 278, 398, 422). Repeated across the film it becomes a 'flare plugin' signature, and it also muddies Venus's crescent beat. Keep the 3-5 strongest ghosts, put their energy in the rim, give each a coating hue, and scale intensity with the Sun's on-screen flux.", "The warp radial blur aliases thin bright line art into hatching, moire and rainbow fringes (board #1): tap spacing exceeds 1 px once trails pass 256 px. Mip the HDR copy or add a third pass, and fade the R/B split for anything that is not point-like. This hits orbit lines in any finale warp and ring edges in warp departures. Also provide an after-post overlay layer the director can draw orbit lines and labels into (cross_piece).", "The hero-Sun reveal switches visibly from a white disc to an orange filtered disc between t=12 and 14 (board #4), so it reads as a filter sliding in. This is the film's first hero image, at peak viewer attention. Spread the transition over at least 3 s, or open already filtered and let the corona fade up."], "redirect_criterion": null, "film_window": "Whole film. Sun glare and flare: 10-45, 52-68 (Mercury, Sun just off-frame), 126-146 (Earth sunrise, Moon transfer), 270-274, 390-400, 414-436. Warp: every leg plus the finale. Fades and letterbox: 0-10 and 436-441.5.", "why": "Critical and the highest-scoring critical (7.5), close to target, so one round. The three picks either recur across the film or land at peak attention. Veiling-glare consistency (board #3) goes to the integration VFX pass instead: a lifted veil with the Sun just off-frame at Mercury reads as plausible glare to a viewer, even though a physics critic flags it."}, "asteroids": {"piece": "asteroids", "decision": "continue", "rounds": 1, "focus": ["Cross-piece, and first priority. The belt ring draws a uniform grey band about 15 deg wide along the ecliptic, outside the belt. It fogs the body views of the sky, Venus, Mercury, Earth and Saturn (a dust lens in the eclipse frame) and the journey near Mars (t~198), so it masks five other pieces' wave-2 critics. Gate it to within about 0.5 AU of the belt in every mode, view=body included, or turn it into real zodiacal light (falloff with elongation, solar colour, scaled by uSkyGain). Land this as a standalone change first so other critics render clean views.", "From outside or on arrival the swarm renders as a soft-edged, glowing rounded rectangle (board #3), and the unresolved points read as uniform 2-4 px snow (summary). These dominate the chapter's opening frames: a grey slab at sheet t=208 and a snowfield at t=216. Use a noise-modulated ellipsoid density shared by the points and the glow, feather the edges, and size and brighten the points so they read as distant rock.", "Hero and close-pass rocks look like plaster or soap (board #1, structural): pillowy fbm, stamped pocks, no rims, no boulders. These are the chapter's money shots at t=219-224. Build a crater size-frequency hierarchy with rims and degradation states, drive albedo from slope and age, and add cavity AO so front-lit rocks keep their form. Once the director re-aims the glide off the Sun line, these surfaces will be more exposed."], "redirect_criterion": "The film's lighting direction (a front-lit glide, an empty sunward beat) belongs to the director (cross_piece). If the new glide has not landed, judge rock legibility in body views at phase 70-100 deg as well as in the film frames, so the director's lighting does not mask the surface work.", "film_window": "206-230 s: 206-214 arrival (swarm envelope), 214-222 glide and close whip (219.5), 222 beat hero, 224-228 sunward beat. The belt-ring band appears anywhere it is ungated: the journey at about 190-206 and all body views.", "why": "Minor stake, but it cannot be parked: the band contaminates five other pieces' critic views, so those pieces depend on it. One round, with the band fix first."}, "audio": {"piece": "audio", "decision": "continue", "rounds": 2, "focus": ["The melody is mostly 2-3-note fragments and never builds a long line (board #1, structural): 31 of 45 events are fragments, and the longest is 6 notes. With nothing to follow over 7.4 minutes it reads as ambient wallpaper, and the viewer hears it for the whole film. Write 3 real 8-16-bar themes that span chords, at the emotional anchors: Earth arrival (~t=112-124), the Saturn eclipse (~308-316) and the finale (~414-441). Add the compose-check: at least 3 phrases of 8 or more notes, each lasting 12 s or more.", "The warp legs are one gesture repeated, hiss over a held chord, and the Jupiter-to-Saturn riser (-10.9 LUFS) is the loudest moment of the film, louder than Earthrise or the finale (board #4). render.mjs already exits 3 on the current cut. Move the harmony through a pivot into each arrival, vary the gesture between legs, and compute riserTrim at compose time from the live cue table, because the director is re-cutting. Cap each leg below the next world's bloom, and keep the top 3 loudness moments for the anchors.", "Register and harmonic rhythm barely change between worlds (board #2). The top voice stays parked around E4 and chords change every 6-9 s everywhere, so the Sun, Earth, Mars, Saturn and finale are spectrally the same (self-similarity at or above 0.9). Set per-world register targets and vary harmonic rhythm on purpose, so each chapter sounds like a new place."], "redirect_criterion": "Render and judge against the director's latest cut and cue table, not the 441.5 s cut. Leg-calibration failures caused by a stale cut belong to integration, not to the composition.", "film_window": "Whole film, 0-441.5 s. Anchors: Earth arrival ~112-124, Earthrise ~150-162, Saturn eclipse ~308-316, pale blue dot and finale ~414-441. Warp legs at each transition (sheet t~48, 76, 104, 176, 236, 280, 344, 372).", "why": "Major, 6.0, unchanged across all three rounds. The engineering is excellent, but the music does not yet carry emotion, and the goal is an experience the viewer watches once, start to finish, with sound. Two rounds, because the themes are a composition rewrite. It runs alongside the director and must take its timing from the director's cue table."}, "venus": {"piece": "venus", "decision": "continue", "rounds": 1, "focus": ["The hero crescent and ring of light are dim, grey and incomplete (board #1): a 1-2 px edge with no HDR, so post adds no bloom, and only about 270 deg of ring at 172 deg phase. The section 5 beat is 'a thick-atmosphered crescent with a glowing limb', yet in the film (t~92-97, sheet t=96) it reads as a thin grey outline. Use a two-term HG forward lobe, a higher upper haze and HDR forward-scatter output. The +1 EV for the beat is the director's (cross_piece).", "Away from the disc centre the colour goes grey or cyan instead of cream (board #3): the crescent is 242/239/234 with a bluish fringe, and the ring is a neutral 156. This is the same beat as item 1, and a glowing crescent that stays grey still won't read as Venus. Give the haze a spectral single-scattering albedo, reduce Rayleigh on long slant paths, and tint the twilight source by the deck colour.", "The planetary-scale Y and polar collar do not read without stretching (board #4). The day disc fills the right half of the frame at t~84-90 (sheet t=88) and reads as a flat white wall. Add longitude-stretched coherent streaks, a stronger Y-wave term and a bright collar band, keeping it subtle as the brief asks."], "redirect_criterion": "If the director's EV bump for the crescent beat has not landed, judge the crescent's HDR radiance in body views at matched exposure as well as in the film at t~92-97, so the film's exposure does not mask the shading fix.", "film_window": "80-98 s: 80 glint, 84-90 day disc (fills the right half of the frame), 92-97 crescent (small in frame).", "why": "Major, 6.5. Unlike the other majors, its section 5 beat (the glowing crescent) is not delivered on screen today. The close-range deck cells (board #2) are not where the film's camera goes, so they are left out. One round, starting now."}, "mars": {"piece": "mars", "decision": "defer", "rounds": 1, "focus": ["The dark albedo markings are too weak and too orange, and Valles Marineris has no dark floor (board #2). This is in every Mars frame (the disc at t=184-204), and the disc is the whole chapter. Apply an albedo-dependent grade toward slate grey-brown below the median, add a contrast pivot, darken the canyon floors with a MOLA-depth mask, and histogram-check against Viking.", "The polar cap reads as grey plaster, with a stippled noise edge, dark crater interiors and dark dashes near the pole (board #3). The cap is large in the film at t~196-204 (sheet t=200, j_198). Raise frost albedo to 0.6-0.7 with a warm pink tint, use a gentler photometric law for ice, drive the edge from topography with a 1-3 px feather, apply frost after the crater albedo, and evaluate the near-pole features in 3D.", "In the film Phobos is a featureless grey egg or a near-black silhouette against Mars, and Deimos's silhouette is faceted (summary #7). 'Phobos crosses close in front' is a section 5 beat (t~188-196, sheet t=192). Add Mars-shine fill, visible groove and crater texture at its film size, and smooth silhouettes, and fix the tick aliasing on the Phobos crescent (board #4)."], "redirect_criterion": "The required limb-haze view (dist=1.12, phase=95) does not contain the limb, which is part of why it renders black. Judge limb haze and twilight at a view that includes the limb, and in the film at t~196-204.", "film_window": "180-206 s: 184 card (small disc), 188-196 disc with the Phobos crossing, 196-204 close disc with the north cap.", "why": "Major, 6.2. The film shows Mars only at disc scale, where the critic finds its global colour already matching Viking. The close-range terrain gap (board #1) is not on screen, so effort goes to the disc-scale reads and the Phobos beat. Deferred, first in the queue: launch it when asteroids or post frees a slot. One round."}, "neptune": {"piece": "neptune", "decision": "defer", "rounds": 1, "focus": ["The banding has about half the reference contrast, and the -59 S belt and -71 S collar are invisible (board #3). That affects the whole disc in the full-frame hero approach (t~380-388, sheet t=384), the last planet's portrait. Roughly double the band multipliers, carry more of the pattern in the gas term, and verify a central-meridian p2p of at least 80 DN.", "Bright clouds look like opaque white paint strokes with stair-stepped edges, and the GDS has a lens-like bright rim (board #1). Both are visible in the film's hero approach frame (24_film_t374). Use a continuous opacity profile with a skirt, a blue tint from the gas column, a soft scattering halo, and drop the NE rim.", "Triton's terminator is a straight, hard CG cut (board #5), because the bump term fades out by mu0. Triton with Neptune in twin crescents is the chapter's closing beat (t~400-412, sheet t=408), and at that lighting the terminator is what shows. Let relief show at low sun and add cheap horizon taps. The cantaloupe terrain (board #4) is next if the round has room."], "redirect_criterion": null, "film_window": "374-414 s: 376 glint, 380-388 hero disc (full frame), 390-400 crescent with the Sun and backlit rings, 400-412 Triton and Neptune twin crescents.", "why": "Major, 6.5. The critic already scores the views the film uses at 7-7.5 (disc 7, crescent 7.5, Triton+Neptune 7.5). The weak view, the framed dark spot at 5.5, is closer than anything the film shows. It is worth a round for its roughly 35 s and the final planet portrait, but after the pieces whose beats fail on screen. Deferred, second in the queue. One round."}, "mercury": {"piece": "mercury", "decision": "defer", "rounds": 1, "focus": ["Procedural crater interiors ignore the DEM terrain shadow (board #1). Crater floors show as lit discs with hard circular edges inside black terrain shadow, and craters that sit entirely in shadow glow at 6-14/255 over a shadow of 0. In the film's hero low pass (t=62-66, critic film_t66) they read as pasted lenses.", "Mid-ground and near-horizon terrain goes soft and smeared during the low pass, with parallel row lines at the skyline and a skyline that is a smooth arc (board #4, critic film_t64). This is the back half of every low-pass frame. Use anisotropic footprint taps and a min-max DEM horizon march with secant refinement.", "Complex and degraded craters read as flat pale decals with stamped T- or bird-shaped central-peak shadows (board #2), in the same low-pass frames. Give the peak a real height profile with a short shadow march, a wide rim fade and terraces."], "redirect_criterion": null, "film_window": "52-69 s: 52-58 half-lit disc with card (the framing drift at 57-58.6 is director-owned), 60-68 terminator low pass.", "why": "Major, 6.5. The low pass is a strong beat at thumbnail scale (sheet t=64), and all its tells are confined to it. The film never shows the high-sun close views (board #3). Deferred, third in the queue. One round."}, "hud": {"piece": "hud", "decision": "defer", "rounds": 1, "focus": ["The finale's 'planets as labelled points' beat is thin, and the HUD covers part of it itself (board #1). The THE SOLAR SYSTEM card sits on Neptune and suppresses its label. Mercury, Venus and Mars are never labelled, Earth's label is lost in the glare, and labels last only about 3.5 s. The finale is a named hero moment. Treat label points as hard obstacles for the card, fan the inner planets out on leaders, hold labels through the Earth caption, and retime to the director's new finale.", "The telemetry block sits on the subject (board #2): Titan at t~330, the Mars cap at t~185.75, Saturn's rings at t~300. When a disc covers the block, swap it to another corner or collapse it, and add a scrim with readable keys.", "Title-card facts are readable for under 3 s (board #4). Compress the stagger, guarantee 4.5 s at full opacity, and use the director's longer body beats once they land. Stars showing through glyphs (board #5) is next if there is room."], "redirect_criterion": null, "film_window": "Whole film. Title cards at each arrival (t~56, 88, 112, 184, 216, 248, 288, 330, 352, 384, 408), telemetry throughout, finale 414-441.5 (labels 424-437).", "why": "Major, 8.0, the highest score on the board. All of its top gaps depend on the director's re-cut (finale length, beat durations, subject placement), so a round now would be spent on a cut that is about to change. Deferred until the director's round 1 lands. One round."}, "uranus": {"piece": "uranus", "decision": "bank_floor", "rounds": 0, "focus": [], "redirect_criterion": null, "film_window": "350-366 s: 352 card (frame nearly empty), 356-364 bullseye at small disc scale.", "why": "Major, 7.0, and it rose every round. The film shows Uranus only as a small bullseye, which the critic calls close to photographic. Every remaining tell is in a view the film's camera never reaches: the NaN pixels on the limb in the rings-close view, the marbling at 0.2 R, the horizon seam, the moon close-ups. Globe tonality is cosmetic. The NaN pixel goes to the integration hygiene check rather than a fix round. The card showing before Uranus is visible (t=352) is director/HUD timing."}}
const XP = [{"gap": "A uniform grey band about 15 deg wide (and a speckled dust lens) along the ecliptic: the belt ring is drawn outside the belt, view=body included. It fogs sky, Venus, Mercury (a glowing ring on the night side in crescent.png), Earth and Moon, and Saturn eclipse views, and the journey near Mars at t~198.", "reported_by": "sky, venus, mercury, earth (which misattributed it to the sky's zodiacal light), saturn, post ('beige ecliptic band')", "owner": "asteroids"}, {"gap": "The warp radial blur aliases thin line art: finale orbit ellipses become hatched, moire and rainbow-fringed ribbons.", "reported_by": "post", "owner": "post (mipped HDR or an extra pass for trails over 256 px; an after-post overlay layer), with the director drawing orbit lines and labels into that overlay or not warping while they are on screen"}, {"gap": "Warp=1 plateaus look sideways: the apex is off-frame at t=168, 270 and 360, and only t=228 shows radial crowding toward the travel direction.", "reported_by": "sky", "owner": "director (keep the apex within about 25 deg of frame centre during every plateau); sky keeps the streak-length and receding-side fixes"}, {"gap": "The belt chapter is front-lit (phase about 15-35 deg), the 'Looking sunward' beat is dark and empty, and the best backlit angle (x_sunward140) never appears in the film.", "reported_by": "asteroids", "owner": "director (aim the glide 70-100 deg from the Sun; hold a 3-4 s sunward beat with the Sun 25-40 deg off-axis)"}, {"gap": "The Venus crescent beat is under-exposed (EV.venus is -0.3), so even a fixed forward-scatter limb won't bloom.", "reported_by": "venus", "owner": "director (+~1 EV on the crescent beat)"}, {"gap": "Post glare and ghosts make Venus's night disc muddy in the crescent beat; giant hexagonal ghosts and a bright streak at the occluded Sun appear in Uranus's crescent and phase-170 views.", "reported_by": "venus, uranus", "owner": "post (the ghost-chain fix covers it; check the occluded-Sun streak with post flare off, and if it persists it belongs to sun)"}, {"gap": "The Earthrise lunar relief needs a 5-15 deg Sun so that long shadows carve it.", "reported_by": "earth", "owner": "director (time and place the Earthrise beat for that sun elevation wherever the real geometry allows)"}, {"gap": "Skim cameras are built from ecliptic elevation, so 'ring skim' queries land 0.82 R below the ring plane.", "reported_by": "saturn", "owner": "director and the lead's shot queries (build ring-frame cameras with poleQuaternion('saturn'); use phase=50&el=23 or el=26 for body-view skims)"}, {"gap": "Title-card facts are readable for under 3 s because body beats are 6.5-7.5 s and the card exits early; Titan and Mercury leave the frame under their own cards.", "reported_by": "hud, director", "owner": "director (8 s minimum body beats, safe-frame constraint); hud (fact stagger)"}, {"gap": "Finale: the Sun's glare swallows the inner orbits and Earth's label, and an ellipse around the finale Sun is scene content.", "reported_by": "director, hud, post", "owner": "director (finale exposure, framing and orbit drawing); hud for label placement"}]
const COMPILE = {"neptune": 7687, "sun": 9380, "mars": 6337, "jupiter": 5091, "saturn": 5868, "mercury": 5475, "uranus": 5888, "venus": 2242, "asteroids": 931, "earth": null}

const DONE = {"completed": {"w2-fix1:asteroids": "I finished all three triage items in order and got the shader compile cost under budget: 743 ms in total, with the largest single object at 281 ms. All four required views, the tumble sheet and the new belt chapter (t 192\u2013219) render with no console errors from this piece.\n\n**1. Grey band along the ecliptic (done first, `ring.js`)**\n- **Cause:** body views carry no `beltRing` value, so the ring always drew at full strength.\n- **Fix:** the band and the belt points now multiply that value by a position check. They only draw when the camera is within about 0.25\u20130.45 AU of the belt, or well above or below the ecliptic (the finale).\n- **Result:** the band is gone from Venus, Mercury, Earth and Mars (1.6 AU), and the speckled lens is gone from the Saturn eclipse view. The finale ring at t\u2248426 is unchanged.\n- The faint streak still visible in the Saturn shot belongs to Saturn's piece; it is there with asteroids switched off.\n\n**2. Swarm from outside, and the \"snow\" points**\n- **Cause of the rectangle:** a slowly falling density cut off by hard box edges at the sides and a flat front.\n- **Fix:** one density shape, used by the rocks, points, glow and dust alike. It is an ellipsoid with soft tails, bent by noise, plus long filaments with gaps between them. At the old box edges the density is now about 0.5% of the centre, so no straight edge can show.\n- **Result:** at t\u2248195 it reads as a ragged, streaky cloud.\n- **Points:** they now use a fixed, sharp spot size, so brightness follows each rock's real size, albedo and lighting: a few bright tan rocks and many faint dark ones. Real rock outlines take over from about 2 px (was 3).\n- **Ghosting fixed:** a near rock no longer shows a half-transparent grey ghost while it switches from sprite to mesh.\n\n**3. Hero rocks looked like plaster**\n- **Craters:** they now cover the surface fully (each size band covers about 15%, up from 5%), overlap, and each has an age: fresh ones are deep with sharp rims, old ones are shallow and soft. Boulders come from the same pass.\n- **Colour and brightness:** brightness now follows the terrain rather than random blotches. Fresh crater walls and steep slopes are bright, old floors are darker, and the S-types are now tan.\n- **Shadows:** craters stay readable when lit from the front. Shadowed hollows get light bounced off their sunlit walls, while shadows on outward-facing sides stay black.\n- **Bright rim on backlit rocks:** it now lights only the real outline, not every ridge inside.\n- **Overhanging flaps:** detail is reduced on surfaces that are steep relative to the body centre, but this did not remove them (see gaps).\n\n**Compile budget**\n\n| | before | now (lower of two runs) |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| rocks | 2 \u00d7 ~505 ms (the tool wasn't measuring them) | 281 ms (one program for all 155 materials) |\n| points | 262\u2013435 ms | 201 ms |\n| glow | 639 ms | 107 ms |\n| dust | 161\u2013327 ms | 70 ms |\n| **total** | ~2160 ms | **743 ms** (818 on the other run) |\n\nThe tool's 931 ms figure missed the rocks because they were already compiled on the first frame; I removed that, so the warm-up now pays for them visibly. The runs are noisy because other builders share the CPU.\n\n**Performance:** 218 fps at the required view (4.6 ms average, 8.3 ms p95). This piece costs about 1.8 ms of GPU time per frame, up from 1.65 ms, mostly because the glow now evaluates the new density.\n\n**Best shots**\n```\nnode tools/shot.mjs --t 192:218:2 --sheet shots/asteroids/film.png --cols 4 --hud 0\nnode tools/shot.mjs --t 195,205.2,209.1,214 --out shots/asteroids/film_{t}.png --hud 0\nnode tools/shot.mjs --q \"view=body&body=asteroids&dist=0.6&phase=27.5&el=-4&fov=16\" --hud 0 --out shots/asteroids/eros16.png\nnode tools/shot.mjs --q \"view=body&body=asteroids&dist=0.6&fov=55&phase=90\" --hud 0 --out shots/asteroids/in90.png\nnode tools/shot.mjs --q \"view=body&body=venus&dist=3&phase=120\" --hud 0 --out shots/asteroids/venus_noband.png\nnode tools/checks/shader_cost.mjs --q \"only=asteroids,post\" --top 25\n```\n\nThe director has re-cut the film: the belt chapter is now t 192.3\u2013218.9, with hero beats at 205.2 (Eros-like), 209.1 (Ida-like) and 211.4 (Itokawa-like). The new cut also has the sunward beat this piece asked for, with the Sun about 38\u00b0 off axis at 212\u2013217. `notes/asteroids.md` is rewritten with the field descriptor, timings, commands and gaps. Before/after shots are in `C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\shots\\asteroids\\w2\\`.\n\n**Remaining gaps**\n- The paper-flap striations remain on steep ledges of the elongated heroes, for example the Eros-like saddle wall at fov \u2264 16. Removing them needs the surface pushed out along its normal instead of from the centre.\n- From outside, the swarm is glow only, with no individual sparkling rocks. Reaching that far with points costs too much.\n- Backlit rims in the sunward beat are soft against the bright haze.\n- Dust glints are rare and faint, and there is no motion blur on fast close passes.\n- Small rocks can still pass through the big hero rocks.", "w2-fix1:post": "I fixed all three triage items in the post piece. Two of them (ghosts and warp) were code fixes; the hero-Sun reveal needed no change because the director's re-cut already fixed it. Post now costs 0.37 ms per frame at 1600x900 with warp and letterbox forced on (budget 1.5 ms). The cold frame after a seek is 8\u201353 ms everywhere (budget 80 ms).\n\n**1. Ghost chain no longer looks like a flare plugin.** The cause was nine copies of the same grey filled heptagon, all visible in every Sun shot. Now:\n- There are five ghosts: a small green reflection mirrored through the frame centre, a violet polygon, a round cyan-green ghost, a large amber ring and a huge faint blue-violet ring.\n- Most of each ghost's light is in its rim. The interior is about 15% of the rim in display (14/255 inside vs 92/255 rim at t=88), and the rim outline no longer has rainbow fringes.\n- Each ghost only appears over part of the frame, so a shot shows 2\u20134 of them and a different set from shot to shot.\n- Brightness still scales with the Sun's flux, its visibility and the exposure.\n- Venus's night side stays clean at t=88, and the giant hexagons are gone from the Uranus crescent.\n\n**2. Warp blur no longer turns thin lines into hatching or moir\u00e9.** The cause was per-pixel random jitter on the blur taps, combined with a smoothing pass that only worked if there was no jitter. Now:\n- Both passes step evenly along each ray from the focus with no jitter, so the result is exact. Very long trails, when the focus is far off-frame, are capped and become parallel streaks.\n- The red/blue colour split only applies to point-like sources such as stars, not to lines or diffuse light.\n- At the focus the original image is used unblurred, the barrel distortion uses a sharper (Catmull-Rom) resample, and the extra warp bloom only affects the edges of the frame. The destination's point of light now matches the no-warp frame (10 px wide at t=220 either way).\n- **New overlay layer:** any object set to `layers.set(31)` (exported as `OVERLAY_LAYER`) is drawn after the warp, bloom and flare. It keeps the same exposure and tone curve, so it is a drop-in for the orbit lines.\n- **Action for the director:** the overlay is not used in the film yet. It needs `line.layers.set(31)` on the finale orbit lines. You can preview it now with `postOv=orbits`, which moves those lines onto the overlay layer for testing only.\n\n**3. Hero-Sun reveal.** In the current cut the exposure drops before the Sun enters, so the colour change happens off-screen (t=10.0\u201310.75). The disc enters at t=11.2 already orange, and its centre colour stays stable from t=11.75 on. I changed nothing here.\n\n**Compile cost.** The two shader passes that don't run on the first frame (sun probe, warp) and any overlay objects are drawn once at startup, so nothing compiles mid-film. Some runs seeking straight to t=400 without `warm=1` hitched for 1\u201314 s. That hitch appeared with and without my test switches, so it is the scene's own shaders compiling cold, not post.\n\n**Best shot commands:**\n```bash\nnode tools/shot.mjs --q \"view=body&body=earth&dist=3&phase=150&el=4&fov=45\" --hud 0 --out shots/post/w2/final/glare150.png\nnode tools/shot.mjs --t 88,126,370,388 --hud 0 --out \"shots/post/w2/film/c_{t}.png\"\nnode tools/shot.mjs --t 220,162 --hud 0 --out \"shots/post/w2/warp/e_{t}.png\"\nnode tools/shot.mjs --t 404 --hud 0 --q \"postOv=orbits&postWarp=1\" --out shots/post/w2/warp/govw_404.png\nnode tools/shot.mjs --t 9.5:13:0.25 --w 800 --h 450 --hud 0 --sheet shots/post/w2/hero/strip.png --cols 5\nnode tools/checks/perf_probe.mjs --q warm=1 --t 12,48,78,105,136,177,210,234,282,345,372,395,401\n```\nThe director re-cut the film several times during the round (now about 434 s), so these times may drift again.\n\n**Remaining gaps:**\n- The hero colour change still follows the exposure ramp. If a future cut lets the disc enter while the exposure is still falling, the white-to-orange change would show again; `shot.grade.hero` can set it directly.\n- A Sun just off-frame still lifts the near edge of the frame with haze (Triton, t\u2248380\u2013386). The triage routed this to the integration pass.\n- The finale's bright dust ring around the Sun (asteroids' scene content) leaves two dark lobes inside the glare.\n- The overlay only adds light; it can't hide anything behind it.\n- Older gaps are unchanged: ghosts from non-solar sources never trigger in this film, a 1\u00b0 Sun near Mercury gets only faint spikes, and very bright edges resolve slightly fat.\n\nSeveral other modules (director, mercury, neptune, earth and a uranus/neptune shader) broke briefly mid-edit during the round. All the final shots were rendered once they worked again, and post logged no errors in any run.\n\nNotes are rewritten in `C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\notes\\post.md`, including the request to the director. The code changes are in `C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\src\\render\\post.js` and `C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\src\\render\\post\\shaders.js`. Before/after pairs are in `C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\shots\\post\\w2\\final\\`.", "w2-fix1:sky": "I closed all three triage items this round: the Milky Way texture, the warp look and the bulge. I changed only `src/sky/shaders.js` and `src/sky/sky.js`; the assets are untouched at 338 KB. The sky module throws no console errors at any quality. A full-module film run did fail to load the Jupiter module (see gaps).\n\n**1. Milky Way texture (grainy star clouds, not fog)**\n- **Root cause, light:** almost all of the band's light was a smooth term, and the old 4 fixed star lattices carried only 9-14% of it. They are replaced by a 7-level ladder of star spacings (0.0046 rad / 2^k). A level switches to points once its spacing exceeds 1.6 px, so at every field of view the finest active level is a pixel-scale grain of faint stars:\n  - Each level keeps 3-4% of the light as resolved stars, placed where the visible light is, so dust lanes are nearly empty.\n  - The two finest active levels also carry 45% of the diffuse light as grain.\n  - The grain fades out between fov ~40 and ~70 and in faint regions, so wide shots stay smooth.\n  - Foreground stars resolve too, so dark lanes keep a sparse scatter of stars.\n- **Root cause, dust:** the noise added below the data's resolution was isotropic, which made the round \"cauliflower\" cells. It is now streaked fBm on the sphere, about 3.5x longer along each cloud's own iso-tau contours (direction taken from the IRAS map's gradient), with 30% sharp ridges.\n- **Edges:** the old edge steepening now acts only on edges and is weaker (EDGE_K 2.2 to 1.2). A second steepening, around the level where a cloud visually turns dark (tau ~2), makes edges crisp without lumps. Small dark clouds saturate at an extra optical depth of ~3, so they are never opaque cut-outs.\n- **Before/after:** `shots/sky/w2/final/crop_sgr20_dust_3x_before.png` vs `crop_sgr20_dust_3x.png`.\n\n**2. Warp**\n- Streak length is soft-capped at 5% of frame height (was a hard 360 px), with a bright head carrying up to 45% of each star's light.\n- The receding-side dimming exponent dropped from 3.6 to 1.4 for stars and 1.2 for the Milky Way, floored at 0.25 / 0.3, so the trailing sky reads reddened, not black.\n- The Milky Way is aberrated, never smeared by the sky. The remaining smear is post's radial blur.\n\n**3. Bulge**\n- **Root cause:** the background-light texture is clipped at its maximum over the whole inner Galaxy (|b| < 7\u00b0), which flattened the bulge into a straight band.\n- **Fix:** the shader restores the missing light with an analytic boxy bulge (scales 3.6 \u00d7 3.0\u00b0) behind a reduced dust screen (0.6\u00d7 the column at |b| > 3\u00b0). It also gives the disk a visible/near-IR factor of 1.5, because disk stars are bluer than the bulge's K giants, so Cygnus and Carina hold their own.\n- **Measured** (fov 8 patches, sky only, median brightness): Large Sagittarius Star Cloud 85, Carina 72, Cygnus 60, Scutum 31.\n\n**Best shot commands** (with `Q=\"view=body&body=earth&dist=60\"`):\n1. `node tools/shot.mjs --hud 0 --q \"$Q&aimRaDec=270,-27&fov=20&only=sky,post\" --out shots/sky/sgr20.png` (grainy Sagittarius clouds, filamentary lanes)\n2. `node tools/shot.mjs --hud 0 --q \"$Q&aimRaDec=266,-29&fov=60&only=sky,post\" --out shots/sky/gc.png` (bulge swelling, Great Rift)\n3. `node tools/shot.mjs --hud 0 --q \"$Q&aimRaDec=190,-61&fov=20&only=sky,post\" --out shots/sky/crux20.png` (Coalsack, Crux)\n4. `node tools/shot.mjs --hud 0 --q \"$Q&aimRaDec=280,-10&fov=100&only=sky,post\" --out shots/sky/wide.png`\n5. `node tools/shot.mjs --hud 0 --t 327 --q \"only=sky,post,director\" --out shots/sky/warp327.png` (in-film warp plateau; legs are now around 220, 260, 327, 350 and 391-399)\n6. `node tools/shot.mjs --hud 0 --q \"$Q&aimRaDec=280,-10&fov=60&only=sky,post&skyWarp=0.8&skyVel=280,-10&postWarp=0.8\" --out shots/sky/warp.png` (forced warp)\n\n**Compile and frame cost**\n- `node tools/checks/shader_cost.mjs --q \"only=sky,post\"` shows sky 0 ms, measured after forcing both programs to rebuild.\n- `node tools/checks/perf_probe.mjs --q warm=1 --t 6,220.5,260.5,272,326.5,395`: cold frame after a seek is 7-35 ms everywhere, inside the 80 ms budget.\n- Average frame is 4.17-4.22 ms at both 1600x900 and 3200x1800 (vsync-limited).\n- Both sky programs are created at create() and drawn every frame; quality settings only change uniforms, never defines.\n\n**Remaining gaps**\n- The brightest Sagittarius fields look slightly \"sandy\" at fov 10-20: the grain is too uniform, without the clumping real clouds have at 1-5 arcmin.\n- Inner-plane and far-southern lanes are still softer than in a photograph.\n- The bulge is analytic; the cleaner fix is to rebuild the background-light texture with a higher maximum.\n- The Coalsack interior is too dark.\n- Nebula sprites are still procedural (Carina compact at fov 12, Lagoon and Trifid small at fov > 30).\n- There is no zodiacal light.\n- Under warp, the Milky Way smear and colour fringes on streak heads come from post.\n\n**For other pieces** (written up in `notes/sky.md`):\n- **Director:** the warp plateaus at 220.5, 260.5 and 327 now show radial crowding toward the apex. The frame at t=326.5 is pure black even with the sky module disabled, so it is a director fade or cut.\n- **Jupiter:** in the full-module film run it fails to load with `SyntaxError: Identifier 'lon' has already been declared`.\n- **Post:** please reduce lateral CA during warp.\n\nShots are in `C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\shots\\sky\\w2\\` (`base`, `it`, `final`, `film`, `meas`), and `C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\notes\\sky.md` is updated.", "w2-fix1:sun": "I worked through all four triage items and brought the Sun's shader compile cost from about 26.3 s to about 0.71 s total. That meets the 800 ms budget with about 80 ms to spare, and no object is over 350 ms. Everything renders cleanly in the body views and in the film frames (t=12\u201344): no console errors from the Sun and no NaN pixels. Limb darkening is unchanged pixel for pixel, and skim frames 1 s apart correlate at 0.974\u20130.976, so the granulation evolves smoothly. GPU time roughly halved: 0.9\u20131.4 ms per view, down from 2.6\u20132.9 ms.\n\n**What changed**\n1. **Prominences (#1):**\n   - **Colour:** the main H-alpha emission is now eclipse red. The bright cores are soft-clipped so they stop at pale pink and never go white.\n   - **Brightness:** prominences are now graded to a fixed on-screen level, the way the corona already was. They were about 3\u00d7 brighter before, and the tone mapper was washing that out to neon pink-white.\n   - **Structure:** each strand now has a soft Gaussian profile instead of a hard tube edge, is pinched at the feet and fatter at the apex, and sits inside a faint patchy cloud envelope. Strands break into knots and gaps, each with 14 segmented threads, and brightness varies widely between strands and threads. Hedgerows are now knotty, segmented threads over a faint diffuse fill.\n2. **Sunspots (#2):** `features.js` now generates each group: a big leader with 1\u20133 umbral cores, Joy's-law tilt, intermediate spots offset from the axis, and a fragmented follower cluster with one-sided penumbrae, plus pores (95 spots in total).\n   - Umbrae are irregular, and light bridges form from the granulation where two cores meet.\n   - Penumbrae are lobed, and filament tips end at different radii so granules show between them.\n   - At full-disc scale the spots are no longer bullseyes. The leaders stay where they were, so existing framings still work.\n3. **Granulation (#3):** granulation-scale fluctuations no longer get the exaggerated photographic colour boost, and get a gentler display contrast (1.15 instead of 1.9). In the skim, the colour swing dropped from 0.124 to 0.078 (standard deviation of G/R) and contrast from 24.8% to 15.5%. I added about 2.5% brightness modulation at meso- and supergranular scales, a wider granule size range, bright-point chains in the lanes, and curved fragmentation lanes.\n4. **Skim sky (#4):** the flat grey fog is replaced by a height profile: bright warm pearl just above the limb, fading with height. Spicules now range from 2 to 15 Mm, cluster in bushes with empty stretches between them, and taper into a glow.\n5. **Compile diet:**\n   - A new `noisetex.js` builds a small 3D noise texture in code and replaces every inlined simplex noise call.\n   - All heavy loops now have bounds set by a uniform, so they are not unrolled.\n   - The prominences and chromosphere used to swap program variants when the camera crossed their boundary. Both now use a single program with a uniform, so nothing compiles mid-film.\n\n**Compile cost** (`node tools/checks/shader_cost.mjs --q \"only=sun,post&view=body&body=neptune&phase=0&dist=3\"`, two runs on a fresh profile)\n\n| object | wave 1 | now |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| photosphere | 17,812 ms | 285 / 280 ms |\n| prominences (one program for all 17) | 7,871 ms | 285 / 313 ms |\n| corona | 471 ms | 90 / 87 ms |\n| chromosphere | 130 ms | 44 / 45 ms |\n| **total** | | **704 / 725 ms** |\n\nMeasure with this Neptune view, not the tool's default. The default first frame draws the Sun, which compiles all of its shaders before measuring starts, so the Sun reads as about 0 ms there.\n\n**Best shot commands**\n- `node tools/shot.mjs --t 12:44:4 --sheet shots/sun/film.png --cols 3 --hud 0` (the film's Sun chapter)\n- `node tools/shot.mjs --q \"view=body&body=sun&dist=1.9&fov=8&look=31.5,2&exposure=0.0002\" --hud 0 --out shots/sun/promzoom.png`\n- `node tools/shot.mjs --q \"view=body&body=sun&dist=1.9&fov=25&look=17,0&exposure=0.0002\" --hud 0 --out shots/sun/limb.png`\n- `node tools/shot.mjs --q \"view=body&body=sun&dist=1.12&el=0&fov=70&look=0,-28&exposure=0.0002\" --hud 0 --out shots/sun/skim.png` (add `--t 0:4:1 --sheet` for motion)\n- `node tools/shot.mjs --q \"view=body&body=sun&dist=4&el=5&fov=40&exposure=0.0003\" --hud 0 --out shots/sun/full.png`\n- `node tools/shot.mjs --q \"view=body&body=sun&dist=14&fov=30&exposure=0.002\" --hud 0 --out shots/sun/wide.png`\n\n**Remaining gaps**\n- Seen along an arch, prominence threads still line up into streaky ribbons. At full-disc scale the hero arches read as rings.\n- Most of the remaining granulation colour swing comes from post's hero tone curve, where the red channel sits in the shoulder. I've asked post in the notes to try a lower `HERO.target` (about 1.6\u20131.8).\n- At mid range the penumbral filaments are faint bundles. The outer penumbra can show a thin dark edge, and some multi-core umbrae look like ink blots near the limb.\n- Extreme close granulation (about 0.03 R) is soft blobs with little interior structure.\n- The faint white-light loop arcades over active regions are switched off to stay inside the compile budget. `sundefs=SUN_ARCADES` brings them back.\n- The corona still has no field-line tracing. The swirl artifact seen from inside the corona is still there; the current cut never shows it.\n\nFiles are in C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1:\n- src\\bodies\\sun.js\n- src\\bodies\\sun\\features.js\n- src\\bodies\\sun\\noisetex.js (new)\n- src\\bodies\\sun\\photosphere.glsl.js\n- src\\bodies\\sun\\corona.glsl.js\n- src\\bodies\\sun\\prominence.glsl.js\n- notes\\sun.md (rewritten; exposures, dev switches and integrator requests are there)\n\nBefore/after shots are in shots\\sun\\w2\\: `base_*` is the wave-1 state, `r10_*`, `r14_*` and `film_final_*` are the current state.", "w2-fix1:saturn": "I fixed all three triage items in order. The compile cost dropped from 9194 ms to 702 ms on the official tool, but that tool doesn't count the ring shaders. With them included the piece is still at about 1.0 s, over the 800 ms budget.\n\n**1. Globe banding (board #1).** The cause was the one triage named: the vortex rotation, the meanders and the wave trains were bending the band-colour lookup, which dragged the band edges into hooks.\n- The band colours now use an almost straight latitude.\n- The swirls only move the cloud texture and a new layer of fine stripes, 0.04\u20131.5\u00b0 wide.\n- Every band edge is now a soft ramp about 0.45\u00b0 wide.\n- Band-to-band contrast is down to \u00d70.8 at the equatorial belts and \u00d70.4 at mid and high latitudes.\n- Vortex edges now fade to zero smoothly, and the close-range bumpy relief is cut by two-thirds.\n- The hexagon has a smooth outline and four spiral arms.\n\nThe hooks are gone in every film frame.\n\n**2. Ring skim (board #2).** At load the code now works out how much light the sunlit planet throws onto each face of the rings. It includes the B ring's own shadow on the northern tropics, which dims the north face. The ring shader reflects that light, or passes it through the rings, using the same lighting model as sunlight.\n- Where planet light is the only light, it is brightened \u00d74.5 so it isn't crushed to black. This is the same display choice as the \u00d73 on the globe's night side, and it switches off during the eclipse.\n- The unlit-face skim is now a dim brown floor with visible texture instead of a black void (bottom of the frame went from under 1/255 to about 9/255 in red). The black-B-ring reversal still reads.\n- I added elongated, angled \"straw\" clumps at 1\u201312 km scales. They wrap around the ring without a seam.\n- The nearest rings are sharper, and I checked telephoto views for moire: none.\n- The straw only shows in 0.02\u20130.05 R views. The film's skim is at about 0.16 R, where the camera resolves radial structure only.\n\n**3. C ring (board #4).** I raised the brightness of the C ring and Cassini Division particles. In the classic view the C ring went from 21 to 44 out of 255, against 91 for the B ring, about 0.2\u00d7 the B ring in linear terms. The Cassini Division went from 12 to 28. The eclipse still matches the reference.\n\n**Compile cost.** The fix was to stop Chrome's Windows shader compiler unrolling and copying code: all loops now have a bound it can't see, and each big noise function is called from one place.\n- Official tool, two runs: 702 / 704 ms in total. The globe is 325\u2013330 ms, under the 350 limit but with little margin. The five icy moons share one program at 223\u2013238 ms, the limb haze is 76 and Titan 59\u201378.\n- At the start of this wave the same tool gave 9194 ms; the globe was 3534 and the moons 5441.\n- That tool misses the ring shaders, because they compile in the first frame before its measurement. My own measurement puts them at 218 ms for the main rings, 50 for the E ring and 16 for the G ring. That brings the whole piece to about 1.0 s, so it does not meet the 800 ms budget once the rings count.\n- All views still run at 240 fps, the 240 Hz vsync cap. The browser console shows no errors from Saturn.\n\n**Film timing has moved.** The director's new cut is 268 small disc, 273\u2013277 approach, 277\u2013286 skim and close globe, 286\u2013291 crescent, 292\u2013306 eclipse, 308\u2013325 Titan.\n\n**Best shots**\n```\nnode tools/shot.mjs --q \"view=body&body=saturn&dist=5&phase=30&el=-20\" --hud 0 --out shots/saturn/classic.png\nnode tools/shot.mjs --q \"view=body&body=saturn&dist=1.8&phase=25&el=-5&fov=40\" --hud 0 --out shots/saturn/mid.png\nnode tools/shot.mjs --q \"view=body&body=saturn&dist=1.9&phase=50&el=26&fov=60&look=0,-8\" --hud 0 --out shots/saturn/skim_unlit.png\nnode tools/shot.mjs --q \"view=body&body=saturn&dist=1.9&phase=50&el=23&fov=60&look=0,25\" --hud 0 --out shots/saturn/skim_lit.png\nnode tools/shot.mjs --q \"view=body&body=saturn&dist=7&phase=179&el=-3&fov=40\" --hud 0 --out shots/saturn/eclipse.png\nnode tools/shot.mjs --t 268:326:3 --hud 0 --sheet shots/saturn/film_sheet.png --cols 5\n```\n\n**Remaining gaps**\n- The hexagon is still in polar night in its required 2026 view. That is physically correct, and I added no twilight glow.\n- The 0.25 R cloud deck still uses screen-derivative bumps; I didn't add the critic's three-sample version because of the compile budget. The film doesn't reach that range.\n- The ring brightening \u00d74.5 and the higher C-ring/Cassini Division particle brightness are display and fitting choices, documented in the notes.\n- Enceladus's ridges are now subtle; Rhea's crater density is unchanged.\n\n**For the integrator** (also in the notes)\n- The shader-cost tool should count the ring programs.\n- Skim cameras should be built in the ring frame, or use `el=23` / `el=26` at `dist=1.9` for body-view skims.\n- A skim over the unlit face is now worth putting in the film.\n\nNotes are in `notes/saturn.md`. Shots are in `shots/saturn/w2/`: `base/` is the start of this wave, and `final/` has the required views (full modules), the film strip and `before_after.png`.", "w2-fix1:venus": "I closed all three triage items and brought Venus inside the shader compile budget. Each fix was checked against wave\u20111 shots of the same views and in the current film cut, where the day disc is at t\u224878\u201382, the half phase at 84 and the crescent at 86\u201390.\n\n**Compile cost** (`shader_cost.mjs --q \"only=venus,post\"`, two runs): deck 261 / 306 ms, haze 88 / 98 ms, total 349 / 404 ms. Wave 1 was 3227 ms. All materials are created in `create()`.\n- The planetary-scale cloud fields are now computed once at load (~0.2 s) into a small texture instead of in the shader. That removed about 10 inlined noise functions.\n- The deck shader now has one noise call site and one Worley call site, both in real loops. Per-octave tables became uniforms, and the cell lookup uses float maths instead of integer `%` and `/`.\n- One trap: rewriting `pow(x, 6.0)` as `t2*t2*t2` in the glory code pushed the deck to 3\u20135 s. I reverted it and left a comment in the code.\n\n**1. Crescent and ring of light** (`haze.js`, `common.js`)\n- Two-term forward-scattering phase function, a higher tenuous upper haze, forward-scattered sunlight that is no longer simply lost, and twilight that diffuses past the shadow line through the thin high haze. The night disc stays black.\n- Phase-150 crescent: peak HDR 3.2 \u2192 4.2, and 1522 \u2192 3417 pixels above 2.0. The glow now fades smoothly over about 80 px instead of dropping 10\u00d7 within 15 px.\n- Ring at 4 R / phase 172 is now a complete 360\u00b0. Sun-side peak went 1.1 \u2192 2.24; the anti-sun side went 0.022 \u2192 0.077 (about 115 sRGB, was 17).\n- In the film at t=88 the crescent peaks at 3.2, with ~1300 px above 2.0.\n\n**2. Colour**\n- The haze's absorption is slightly stronger in blue, and the twilight, the deck's own light and other multiply scattered light are tinted cream. Rayleigh scattering is pushed below the haze, so the limb has no blue fringe from Venus.\n- Crescent edge is now 249/246/241 (was 242/239/234 with a 188/195/206 blue fringe); the ring is cream, about 219/213/203.\n\n**3. Y feature, collar and streaks**\n- The Y is about 1.7\u00d7 stronger and its phase is set so the vertex and both arms face the film's day approach. The real phase is unknown, so this is a free choice.\n- There is a bright collar at ~58\u00b0 with a darker band at ~46\u00b0. Streaks are long and curved (5\u00d7 longitude stretch at low latitude), mostly white on a peach background.\n- Tan markings saturate softly, and cloud with less absorber brightens to neutral white rather than blue.\n- Large-scale contrast on the phase-20 disc is 2.25% std (p2\u2013p98 9.7%), against 2.3% / 8.8% for the Mariner 10 reference. On the film day disc it is about 6%, up from 4.4%.\n- The crack-like cell lanes from wave 1 are gone: cells now have soft bright cores.\n- I raised the deck albedo gain 1.15 \u2192 1.23 so it matches the measured phase curve again (within 3\u20136% at phase 0\u201390).\n\n**Still checked:** super-rotation drift is \u221234 px for 0.03 days (33.6 expected). GPU time is 1.5\u20131.7 ms for the scene with Venus filling the screen. No console errors at low, medium or high quality.\n\n**Best shot commands**\n```\nnode tools/shot.mjs --q \"view=body&body=venus&dist=3.5&phase=150&el=5&only=venus,sky,post\" --hud 0 --out shots/venus/w2/crescent.png\nnode tools/shot.mjs --q \"view=body&body=venus&dist=4&phase=172&el=2&only=venus,sky,post\" --hud 0 --out shots/venus/w2/ring.png\nnode tools/shot.mjs --q \"view=body&body=venus&dist=3&phase=20&el=-30&only=venus,sky,post\" --hud 0 --out shots/venus/w2/south.png\nnode tools/shot.mjs --q \"view=body&body=venus&dist=3&phase=20&el=5\" --hud 0 --out shots/venus/w2/day.png\nnode tools/shot.mjs --q \"view=body&body=venus&dist=1.05&phase=105&el=0&fov=40&look=-62,0&only=venus,sky,post\" --hud 0 --out shots/venus/w2/sunrise.png\nnode tools/shot.mjs --t 80,84,88 --hud 0 --out \"shots/venus/w2/film_t{t}.png\"\n```\nA before/after sheet is at `C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\shots\\venus\\w2\\before_after.png`.\n\n**Remaining gaps**\n- From far away (40 R), Venus at extreme phase is brighter than the measured phase curve: about 1.4\u00d7 at phase 150 and 4.7\u00d7 at 172. I traded that for a near-field crescent and ring that glow. A separate, weaker forward lobe for the dense cloud-top layer would fix the far view without dimming the ring.\n- At 4 R and phase 172 the camera sits deep in Venus's shadow, so the anti-sun side of the ring is physically faint. It is complete, but about 30\u00d7 dimmer than the sun side.\n- The Y and collar are soft, and the collar edge is slightly crisp in the southern view.\n- At 0.15 R altitude the clouds read as soft taupe, and the cells are deliberately subtle.\n- I removed the high-latitude gravity waves to stay within the compile budget.\n\n**Requests to integrator** (also in the notes)\n- Director: +1 EV for the crescent beat (now t\u224886\u201390). The HDR is there, but the glow only reads with the extra stop.\n- Post: the sun glare veil greys the night disc at t\u224886\u201390.\n\nFiles changed:\n- `C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\src\\bodies\\venus.js`\n- `C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\src\\bodies\\venus\\deck.js`\n- `C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\src\\bodies\\venus\\haze.js`\n- `C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\src\\bodies\\venus\\common.js`\n- `C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\src\\bodies\\venus\\bake.js` (new)\n- `C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\notes\\venus.md` (rewritten)", "w2-fix1:director": "I closed all three triage items at the root and checked each one in metrics at all three epochs and in rendered strips. The film is now 429.7 s (7.2 min, was 441.5 s), still slightly over the 6\u20137 min target. The new chapter, caption and cue table is in `notes/director.md` for HUD and audio to retime against.\n\n**1. Dead time and whip pans**\n- **Cause:** one departure formula repeated nine times: the departing world left the frame, then a 4\u20136 s pan at 23\u201330\u00b0/s over empty stars, then the warp.\n- **Departure now:** the world's last framing recedes in place, held steady, so it shrinks at a constant rate with its lighting unchanged. Then a deliberate cut under a ~0.75 s dip to black.\n- **Warp now:** after the cut, a ~3.2 s warp that starts slowly, peaks around 500 c, and has its streaks radiating from the destination at the frame centre (view and travel direction match exactly on every leg's warp).\n- **Arrivals:** the camera resumes on the destination's own approach line, never more than a quarter of its distance from the Sun out, so the Sun distance on the HUD only ever increases. The world swells from a point straight into its first framing; the old arrival swing, the second source of fast turns, is gone.\n- **Supporting work:**\n  - The orientation smoothing now treats every cut as a hard boundary; this exposed and fixed a floating-point bug that caused a 27\u00b0 jump at cuts.\n  - The fluid head is capped at min(0.22\u00d7FOV, 12\u00b0/s).\n  - The authored turns were slowed at their source.\n\n**2. Subjects leaving the frame**\n- **Titan:** its old arc went over Titan's pole and hit an azimuth singularity, which was the jump to the corner. It now arcs slowly around the ecliptic normal.\n- **Mercury:** a new `place` composition aim keeps the planet at a fixed spot in frame, away from the Sun, at any distance. Mercury now holds at x\u22480.25\u20130.30.\n- **Earth \u2192 Moon:** the sunrise holds with the Sun just on the limb, the cut comes 1 s after, and the Moon then swells from a point at the frame centre.\n- **Safe-frame guard:** a look-ahead correction keeps the subject inside the safe box. It now corrects at most 2.4\u00b0, so it is a safety net rather than the fix.\n- **Automated test:** a focus-in-frame check runs at all three epochs.\n- **Rotation kicks:** the ones at 59.7 s and 263.13 s are gone.\n\n**3. The finale**\n- Labelled map 8.8 s, zoom to Earth 6 s, pale blue dot 8.4 s, credits 6.3 s.\n- With flare at 0.08 and the belt ring at 0.1, the Mercury\u2013Mars orbits now read around the Sun; the bright ellipse there was the asteroid belt's dust ring.\n- Orbit lines are switched off whenever the warp is on.\n\n**Requests from other pieces:**\n- **Venus:** day deck +0.1 EV, crescent +1.3 EV.\n- **Earthrise:** the Sun is 10.0\u00b0 up at every epoch.\n- **Asteroid belt:** the glide is now side-lit (view 95\u00b0 from the Sun), followed by a 3.5 s backlit beat with the Sun 38\u00b0 off-axis, just above the frame.\n- **Body beats:** title cards now run 8\u20139 s.\n\n| | wave 1 (critic) | 2026 | 2027 | 2031 |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| no world over 4 px (cold open to finale) | ~98 s | 36.5 s | 37.2 s | 36.5 s |\n| turning > 20\u00b0/s | 43.8 s | 0.5 s | 0.5 s | 0.5 s |\n| turning > 12\u00b0/s | 141 s | 47.7 s | 43.3 s | 44.1 s |\n| subject outside the safe box | ~85 s | 0 s | 0 s | 0 s |\n\nAll three epochs pass validation: no NaN, no path kinks, `evaluate(t)` is pure. No body is clipped (Moon 12 km, Earth 366 km at the sunrise pass, 9,830 km above the rings).\n\n**Best shot commands**\n```bash\nnode tools/shot.mjs --t 0:429:10 --sheet shots/director/w2/sheet_final_2026.png --cols 6 --w 960 --h 540\nnode tools/shot.mjs --t 295:331:1.5 --sheet shots/director/w2/strip_sat_titan.png --cols 5 --w 800 --h 450 --hud 0\nnode tools/shot.mjs --t 255.8:266.3:0.25 --sheet shots/director/w2/strip_jup_sat_dense.png --cols 7 --w 640 --h 360 --hud 0\nnode tools/shot.mjs --t 120:141:1 --sheet shots/director/w2/strip_earth_moon.png --cols 6 --w 800 --h 450 --hud 0\nnode tools/shot.mjs --t 363:429:2.5 --sheet shots/director/w2/strip_finale.png --cols 6 --w 800 --h 450\nnode src/journey/dev/metrics.mjs 2031-03-01T00:00:00Z --spans\n```\n\n**Compile cost:** none; I changed no shaders, and glints are unchanged. The director builds in about 0.9 s.\n\n**Remaining gaps**\n- About 4 s per leg is still warp streaks plus a labelled point before the destination reaches 4 px.\n- There are 11 dips to black. It reads as a consistent grammar, but it is a repeated device.\n- About 45 s of the film still turns faster than 12\u00b0/s, mostly orbits around a centred world.\n- The finale retreat looks back along its own path at warp for about 3 s, by design.\n- Jupiter now leaves half-lit rather than as a crescent; the two crescent options tried were a 150\u00b0 whip or a black frame.\n- In 2027 Earth's night pass is over dark ocean, with no city lights.\n- The Sun sits beside the crescents on Titan's far side and Neptune's night side; flare is calmed there.\n- Full-film runs occasionally log a `VALIDATE_STATUS` shader error. It does not appear with only my modules loaded, so it comes from another module.\n\nEverything is in `notes/director.md`, including requests to the integrator; renders are in `shots/director/w2/`.", "w2-critic1:sky": {"floorMet": true, "score": 6.8, "summary": "The sky is a strong, credible, real-data sky, a little better than wave 1. Its best frames are the cold open at t=8 (Sgr/Ophiuchus, Great Rift, Pipe, rho Oph with blue and red nebulosity) and gc.png, which hold up next to a good wide-field astrophoto. BSC5 positions and B-V colours are right: Betelgeuse, Antares and Aldebaran are orange; Rigel and Spica are blue-white; Alpha Cen is yellowish. Orion, the Big Dipper and Crux with the Coalsack are immediately recognizable. The sky never shows through bodies (Jupiter's night side is pure black at sky=4). Star images are clean round cores with no aliasing: a 0.6 px sub-pixel pan changes 20x20-tile flux by 0.7% median and 2.5% p90, and a 2% zoom changes total flux by 0.4%. The forward warp is the film's best signature effect, with radial capsule streaks, crowding toward the apex and a blueshifted Milky Way (t=222, 264, 328, 352). No sky module failures; the only page failure is neptune's cloudlist.js export, not sky. One Saturn-view run printed a VALIDATE_STATUS shader error once; it did not reproduce on rerun. It stops short of SVS/planetarium grade on several counts. (1) At the film's usual fov of 40-70 deg the Milky Way reads as smooth airbrushed fog with stars sprinkled on top. (2) The resolved grain dots are individually as bright as mag 2-3 BSC stars. That drowns the Scorpius and Cygnus figures, turns the band to glitter when exposure drops in the cold open, and makes the clouds look sandy at fov 15-25. (3) Nebula sprites are undersized and crude: Carina, North America, M42, the Pleiades reflection, the LMC and SMC. (4) Several dark nebulae look like stamped opaque cut-outs or black voids. Smaller items: a muddy-brown receding-side warp in the finale; hard rectangular streak quads in a sideways warp view (not in the current cut); unbounded bright-star bloat at very narrow fov. The asteroid-belt grey band was excluded by judging with only=sky,post,earth.", "gaps": [{"gap": "At the film's working fov (40-70 deg) the Milky Way is a smooth airbrushed cream/grey fog with field stars on top, not a resolved, mottled star cloud. The grain is faded out by design between fov 40 and 70, and the diffuse layer underneath is pure smooth haze. This is on screen for most of the film: behind Earth at t=116, behind the Moon at t=136, the Neptune/Triton frames at t=388-392, and the finale at 408-416.", "severity": "functional", "evidence": "shots/critic/sky/w2r1/gc.png, shots/critic/sky/w2r1/crop_gc_pipe_3x.png, shots/critic/sky/w2r1/cygnus.png, shots/critic/sky/w2r1/crop_cyg_core_3x.png, shots/critic/sky/w2r1/x_scorpius_compare.png (right panel: grain off = pure fog), shots/critic/sky/w2r1/film_t116.png", "fix": "Keep unresolved structure at every fov instead of fading the grain to zero. Add a multiplicative, footprint-filtered 'mottle' term to the diffuse light, built from Poisson-clustered star counts (open-cluster knots, 1-10 arcmin clumps, contrast about 15-25%) and seeded on the sphere so it is stable. The finest active lattice should then always sit at about 1 px and carry low-amplitude, luminosity-function-weighted points rather than vanishing above fov 40."}, {"gap": "Each resolved grain point carries the integrated light of many stars, so it is far brighter than any star that could really be resolved. In a 350x600 px patch of the Sgr band at fov 40, about 440 grain peaks exceed 0.35 linear and the top ones reach the same display peak (about 0.74) as the brightest BSC stars. Results: (a) the Scorpius tail and the Northern Cross are lost in the band; (b) as the Sun enters the cold open (t=9-11) and exposure drops, the diffuse glow is crushed to black by the tonemap toe while the grain survives as isolated golden glitter; (c) at fov 15-25 the clouds read as a uniform carpet of same-size, same-brightness sand.", "severity": "functional", "evidence": "shots/critic/sky/w2r1/x_scorpius40.png vs shots/critic/sky/w2r1/x_scorpius40_bscOnly.png, shots/critic/sky/w2r1/crop_t10_glitter_3x.png (film_t10.png), shots/critic/sky/w2r1/crop_sgr15_grain_4x.png, shots/critic/sky/w2r1/cygnus.png", "fix": "Draw per-point flux from the real luminosity function: in each cell, the k-th brightest point should match N(<m) at that position, which caps the brightest field point at roughly V 7-9 in the band. Return the excess to the smooth diffuse term. Tie grain contrast to exposure*skyGain so the grain can never outlive its own diffuse glow under the tonemap toe. Make BSC stars of V <= 3 grow a clearly larger saturated core and halo than any field point (for example, scale the halo with flux above the field-point ceiling), so constellation figures pop by 2-3x in diameter."}, {"gap": "Diffuse-nebula sprites are undersized and crude at mid zoom. Carina is about 1.3 deg of pink with a black '7' glyph: a 25 px dot in the Crux view, where it should be the most prominent nebula in the southern band, about 3-4 deg with white-cream cores and the V-shaped lane. North America is a pink square with a black rod. M42 is a blurry arch-shaped blob. The Lagoon is about 60% of its true size. The Pleiades reflection nebula has a uniform brushed-metal striation over the whole cluster.", "severity": "cosmetic", "evidence": "shots/critic/sky/w2r1/x_carina20.png, shots/critic/sky/w2r1/crux.png, shots/critic/sky/w2r1/crop_cyg_core_3x.png, shots/critic/sky/w2r1/crop_orion12_m42_4x.png, shots/critic/sky/w2r1/x_sgr15.png, shots/critic/sky/w2r1/crop_pleiades_4x.png", "fix": "Rebuild the big four (Carina, M42, NAN/Pelican, M8) at 512x512 or more, at their true angular extent, with multi-scale filaments and a separate soft absorption layer for the Keyhole/V-lane, the Gulf of Mexico and the M42 'fish mouth'. Add white-cream ionized cores. Confine the Pleiades striations to the Merope/Maia lobes with radial falloff and varying angle. Add these within the 0.4 MB cap by dropping nebula chroma resolution."}, {"gap": "Several dark nebulae read as stamped opaque cut-outs or voids. The Pipe stem is a hard-edged black worm (seen in the cold open and at t=392). The Coalsack is a pitch-black ellipse. The Taurus/Auriga clouds are soft round ink smudges with no filament structure. The M8/M20 surroundings are a 5x10 deg black-brown void, where photos show M8 on the bright NE edge of the Large Sgr cloud.", "severity": "cosmetic", "evidence": "shots/critic/sky/w2r1/crop_gc_pipe_3x.png, shots/critic/sky/w2r1/crop_t392_pipe_3x.png, shots/critic/sky/w2r1/crux.png, shots/critic/sky/w2r1/crop_t72_blobs_3x.png, shots/critic/sky/w2r1/crop_t48_blobs_3x.png, shots/critic/sky/w2r1/x_sgr15.png", "fix": "Soften the explicit Pipe and Coalsack screens: lower peak A_V (Coalsack about 1.0), use a 0.3-0.5 deg edge ramp with domain-warped ragged boundaries and internal streak noise, and keep 10-20% foreground starlight plus DGL inside. Lower TAU_CAP and the 2x mid-plane screen near l = 4-8 deg so the stars around M8/M20 show. Give small LDN clouds a thresholded, warped filament mask instead of a smooth Gaussian stamp."}, {"gap": "At fov 100 the band from Aquila to Scorpius is nearly uniform in width. The bulge barely swells off the plane, and Sgr/Scutum dominate only modestly. In the real sky the bulge is about 20 deg tall and clearly the brightest part of the Milky Way.", "severity": "cosmetic", "evidence": "shots/critic/sky/w2r1/wide.png, shots/critic/sky/w2r1/gc.png", "fix": "Re-encode S_bg in build_milkyway.py with a higher radMax so the inner Galaxy is not clipped. Widen the analytic boxy bulge (latitude scale 3.0 deg to about 4.5-5 deg, COBE-like boxy profile), and restrict the 2x mid-plane screen to |b| < 2 deg so the light at b = -4 to -12 deg reads as a broad swelling."}, {"gap": "In the finale receding-side warp (t=396-404) the redshifted Milky Way becomes a muddy brown wash over half the frame. The trailing stars are uniform-length parallel 'rain'.", "severity": "cosmetic", "evidence": "shots/critic/sky/w2r1/film_t400.png, shots/critic/sky/w2r1/film_t398.png, shots/critic/sky/w2r1/film_skyonly_t404.png, shots/critic/sky/w2r1/warp_back.png", "fix": "Apply the Doppler shift as a blackbody temperature scaling (T' = D*T) for both stars and the Milky Way, not a hue tint plus desaturation. Use about D^3 intensity with a floor near 0.1 so the receding sky goes deep red to black instead of brown-grey. Vary streak length with the true aberration rate (proportional to sin theta' / (1 - beta cos theta')) so the trailing field shows depth."}, {"gap": "When the camera looks about 90 deg from the apex at warp 0.9, bright stars become hard-edged rectangles/parallelograms with flat ends. Not in the current cut (warp legs stay near-radial and the finale's sideways streaks are clean lines), but any sideways framing by the director would expose it.", "severity": "cosmetic", "evidence": "shots/critic/sky/w2r1/warp_side.png, shots/critic/sky/w2r1/crop_warp_side_quads_4x.png", "fix": "Shade streak quads as a capsule SDF (round caps, Gaussian cross-profile, fwidth-antialiased ends). Size the quad width from the PSF core sigma rather than the saturated-core width, and spread the saturated energy along the streak."}, {"gap": "The Magellanic Clouds are too small and featureless. The LMC is a soft cotton puff about 6 deg across with no bar or resolved stars; the SMC is about 2 deg. Real sizes are about 10x9 deg and 5x3 deg.", "severity": "cosmetic", "evidence": "shots/critic/sky/w2r1/crop_lmc_3x.png, shots/critic/sky/w2r1/x_lmc30.png, shots/critic/sky/w2r1/x_scp90.png", "fix": "Scale the sprites to true extent. Give the LMC its off-centre bar, the 30 Dor knot and arm asymmetry, and feed both clouds through the same resolved-grain/mottle path as the Milky Way so they read as star clouds, not smoke."}, {"gap": "Saturated-core growth is not bounded in screen pixels: at fov 2 Sirius becomes an 80 px soft white ball. No current shot puts a bright star in a narrow field, but a telescope-style close-up would.", "severity": "cosmetic", "evidence": "shots/critic/sky/w2r1/x_sirius2.png", "fix": "Cap the saturated core radius at about 6-8 px. Route the remaining energy into the Moffat halo, and optionally into diffraction spikes in post, so a 2 deg field shows a brilliant point with glare rather than a disc."}], "shots": ["shots/critic/sky/w2r1/film_sweep_a.png", "shots/critic/sky/w2r1/film_sweep_b.png", "shots/critic/sky/w2r1/film_t4.png", "shots/critic/sky/w2r1/film_t8.png", "shots/critic/sky/w2r1/film_t10.png", "shots/critic/sky/w2r1/film_t12.png", "shots/critic/sky/w2r1/film_t48.png", "shots/critic/sky/w2r1/film_t72.png", "shots/critic/sky/w2r1/film_t116.png", "shots/critic/sky/w2r1/film_t136.png", "shots/critic/sky/w2r1/film_t222.png", "shots/critic/sky/w2r1/film_t264.png", "shots/critic/sky/w2r1/film_t328.png", "shots/critic/sky/w2r1/film_t352.png", "shots/critic/sky/w2r1/film_t392.png", "shots/critic/sky/w2r1/film_t398.png", "shots/critic/sky/w2r1/film_t400.png", "shots/critic/sky/w2r1/film_t404.png", "shots/critic/sky/w2r1/film_skyonly_t404.png", "shots/critic/sky/w2r1/film_t408.png", "shots/critic/sky/w2r1/film_t416.png", "shots/critic/sky/w2r1/film_t420.png", "shots/critic/sky/w2r1/orion.png", "shots/critic/sky/w2r1/dipper.png", "shots/critic/sky/w2r1/crux.png", "shots/critic/sky/w2r1/gc.png", "shots/critic/sky/w2r1/cygnus.png", "shots/critic/sky/w2r1/wide.png", "shots/critic/sky/w2r1/jupiter.png", "shots/critic/sky/w2r1/x_jupiter_crescent_sky4.png", "shots/critic/sky/w2r1/warp_fwd.png", "shots/critic/sky/w2r1/warp_back.png", "shots/critic/sky/w2r1/warp_side.png", "shots/critic/sky/w2r1/x_sgr15.png", "shots/critic/sky/w2r1/x_orion12.png", "shots/critic/sky/w2r1/x_pleiades6.png", "shots/critic/sky/w2r1/x_lmc30.png", "shots/critic/sky/w2r1/x_scp90.png", "shots/critic/sky/w2r1/x_m31_10.png", "shots/critic/sky/w2r1/x_ngp60.png", "shots/critic/sky/w2r1/x_sirius2.png", "shots/critic/sky/w2r1/x_carina20.png", "shots/critic/sky/w2r1/x_scorpius40.png", "shots/critic/sky/w2r1/x_scorpius40_bscOnly.png", "shots/critic/sky/w2r1/x_scorpius_compare.png", "shots/critic/sky/w2r1/x_cygnus_bscOnly.png", "shots/critic/sky/w2r1/x_antigc100.png", "shots/critic/sky/w2r1/st_a.png", "shots/critic/sky/w2r1/st_b.png", "shots/critic/sky/w2r1/st_pan_strip_5x.png", "shots/critic/sky/w2r1/crop_t10_glitter_3x.png", "shots/critic/sky/w2r1/crop_t8_core_2x.png", "shots/critic/sky/w2r1/crop_gc_pipe_3x.png", "shots/critic/sky/w2r1/crop_t392_pipe_3x.png", "shots/critic/sky/w2r1/crop_sgr15_grain_4x.png", "shots/critic/sky/w2r1/crop_orion12_m42_4x.png", "shots/critic/sky/w2r1/crop_pleiades_4x.png", "shots/critic/sky/w2r1/crop_lmc_3x.png", "shots/critic/sky/w2r1/crop_cyg_core_3x.png", "shots/critic/sky/w2r1/crop_t72_blobs_3x.png", "shots/critic/sky/w2r1/crop_t48_blobs_3x.png", "shots/critic/sky/w2r1/crop_warp_side_quads_4x.png", "shots/critic/sky/w2r1/crop_t328_streaks_3x.png", "shots/critic/sky/w2r1/crop_t404_redblob_4x.png"], "compileMs": -1}, "w2-critic1:asteroids": {"floorMet": true, "score": 6, "compileMs": 826, "summary": "Wave 2, round 1. The director re-cut during the session (duration went from 434.1 to 430.4 s), so the belt chapter now runs about 191-215 s: ~1-2 s outside the swarm, a front-lit glide to ~203, side-lit hero beats ~202-208 (Eros-like, Ida-like, Itokawa-like), then the sunward beat ~208-215. The floor is clearly met. The piece shows instanced, tumbling, varied rocks along the crossing. Tumbling is deterministic (seek-identical frames), and I saw no LOD popping. Perf is 233 fps at the required view.\n\nReal progress since wave 1:\n- The box-shaped swarm envelope is gone.\n- Unresolved points now read as rock-coloured PSF dots, not uniform snow.\n- The belt band is gated off outside the belt.\n- Heroes now carry dense crater fields with cast shadows.\n- The side/back-lit beat (t~208: black silhouettes with thin rims against the Milky Way) is the chapter's best image.\n\nWhat still holds it at hobbyist-plus rather than SVS grade:\n- At film scale the lit rock surfaces still read as putty, soap or clay. The front-lit chips look like white popcorn pebbles. Craters are punched, polygonal dimples with no rims, ejecta or visible boulders, and Ida/Itokawa carry camouflage albedo blotches.\n- The piece's own forward-scatter haze turns the \"small, hard\" 2.7 AU Sun into a soft fog blob. It also kills the Sun's diffraction spikes and puts a flat grey veil behind every rock, with no aerial perspective, so the sunward beat reads like fog on Earth.\n- There are visible shading artifacts: contour lines across a contact-binary neck, black rectangular specks on lit faces, draped-wax mesh flaps at close range, and dotted A2C fringes on backlit silhouettes.\n- The arrival strobes. Rocks cross the frame in under 0.1 s with no motion blur.\n- Dust glints are effectively absent: dust adds 3-17 pixels per film frame.\n- The belt ring is invisible in the finale. It reads well only in a hand-placed wide body view.\n\nCompile cost is over budget in both fresh runs: 826 and 892 ms against the 800 ms cap. The rock program is at 329-330 ms against the 350 ms per-object cap.\n\nThe front-lit glide lighting is the director's (cross_piece). Judged in body views at phase 40-130 and on the side-lit film beats, the surfaces still show the same putty and artifact issues.\n\nTransient errors I hit, none from asteroids: a \"sun\" module syntax error and a moonSurface shader compile error, both from other pieces being edited live. A clean rerun of the in90 view had no failures. No asteroids page error or fallback appeared in any run.", "gaps": [{"gap": "Rock surfaces still read as putty, soap or clay at film scale. Lit faces are smooth and uniform cream. Crater pits are punched dimples with polygonal (cell-shaped) outlines and no raised rims or ejecta, and no boulders or regolith texture show. Ida/Itokawa carry camouflage blotches. Front-lit 10-60 px rocks are rounded white pebbles (sRGB ~200, twice the hero's lit tone of ~100) with no facets: a popcorn look across the ~10 s glide (~193-203) and the hero beats (~202-208).", "severity": "structural", "evidence": "shots/critic/asteroids/w2r1/film_203.png, zoom_203.png, film_197.png, zoom_197.png, film_205.png, zoom_205.png, eros7.png, zoom_eros7_flaps.png, ida_p40_fov14.png, in40.png", "fix": "1. Give the crater profile a real raised rim (~0.04 D) and an outward-decaying ejecta term. Break the polygonal footprints by warping the crater distance with low-amplitude noise and jittering radius per candidate.\n2. Add a footprint-filtered mid-frequency regolith/roughness normal layer (3-4 ridged octaves) so lit faces never go flat, and fine albedo grain driven by slope and age instead of blobs.\n3. For small/mid LODs, keep the fracture-plane facets in the base mesh (flat faces, sharp edges, edge AO baked into vertices) so 10-60 px rocks read as angular shards, not ellipsoids.\n4. Lower the S-type albedo or share near the camera path so front-lit chips sit at ~0.6-0.7 of the hero's lit tone."}, {"gap": "The piece's forward-scatter dust haze swamps the Sun and flattens depth. With the glow on, the 2.7 AU Sun becomes a soft ~600 px fog blob and post's diffraction spikes vanish. With astdbg=noglow it is a crisp hard star. In the sunward beat (~208-215) the haze is a flat grey veil behind all rocks, and near and far silhouettes are equally black (no in-scatter in front of distant rocks). It reads as fog on Earth, contradicting 'Sun small and hard, deep shadows' and the depth target.", "severity": "functional", "evidence": "shots/critic/asteroids/w2r1/p130_glow_compare.png (left: glow on, right: glow off), p130.png, film_214.png, zoom_214_a.png, p180.png", "fix": "1. Cut the haze radiance 3-5x. Restrict the 0.97 HG lobe to a tight 1-2 deg aureole and keep the broad lobe faint.\n2. Do not extinguish the Sun's core, so post's spikes still fire.\n3. Integrate the glow only from the camera to each fragment's depth (sample the depth buffer, or per-instance distance for rocks) so distant silhouettes grey out and near ones stay black. Give the haze visible filament/lane contrast instead of a smooth blob."}, {"gap": "Visible shading and mesh artifacts on hero and mid rocks:\n- thin dark contour lines across the contact-binary neck;\n- small black rectangular specks on lit faces;\n- draped-wax mesh flaps with slit shadows at close range (the 'steep ledge' issue);\n- a dotted light fringe around small backlit silhouettes (alpha-to-coverage pattern) that will shimmer in motion.", "severity": "functional", "evidence": "shots/critic/asteroids/w2r1/zoom_199_seam.png, zoom_205.png (dark squares near the terminator), eros7.png, zoom_eros7_flaps.png, zoom_214_b.png", "fix": "1. Contour lines (likely acne in the radius-map macro-shadow march): add a slope- and footprint-scaled start offset and bias, and sample the radius map with textureLod at a footprint-derived LOD.\n2. Black specks: fade boulder and crater analytic cast shadows when the feature is under ~2 px.\n3. Flaps: displace along the base normal, not radially, or zero the radial detail where dot(n_base, r) < 0.65. Better, remesh heroes with a non-radial parametrisation (SDF to marching cubes or a cube-sphere relax).\n4. Fringe: drop alphaToCoverage dithering once an instance is past the 12 px fade (coverage = 1)."}, {"gap": "Arrival strobe. In the new cut's ~194-199 s the camera moves ~2-6 units/s through rocks 0.1-1 unit off the axis. Consecutive 0.1 s frames share almost no rocks, and nothing has motion blur, so the near field flickers instead of flowing past.", "severity": "functional", "evidence": "shots/critic/asteroids/w2r1/motion_198.png (0.1 s contact sheet), motion_quad_199.png (198.9/199.0/199.1/199.2), film_sheet.png", "fix": "1. The module already samples director.evaluate(t). Make the path-corridor clearance proportional to the local camera speed (e.g. clear radius = speed x 0.4 s, min 0.02) so no rock crosses the frame in under ~0.5 s.\n2. Add velocity-stretched rendering for fast instances: per-instance screen motion from the previous-t camera (pure function of t), with vertices stretched along it and alpha-faded.\n3. Report the arrival speed versus FIELD.recommendedSpeed (0.25) to the director."}, {"gap": "Dust glints, a required feature, are effectively absent on screen. Toggling astdbg=nodust changes only 3-17 pixels per film frame (mean diff < 0.001) and 1 pixel in the required in-field view. Only the phase-130 body view shows ~59 pixels.", "severity": "functional", "evidence": "shots/critic/asteroids/w2r1/dust_diff_199.png, dust_diff_206.png, dust_diff_211.png (8x-amplified diffs, nearly black); measured stats: 199: 3 px > 10 levels, 206: 5 px, 211: 17 px, in-view: 1 px", "fix": "1. Populate near-camera motes (0.05-0.5 units) at roughly 10x the current density, scaled by swarm density.\n2. Render each as a 1-2 px PSF with a specular flake lobe whose orientation is hash(id) rotated by t, so they flash as they tumble.\n3. Raise peak brightness 5-20x so about 50-200 glints show per frame in side and back light, with forward-scatter boost in the sunward beat."}, {"gap": "Shader compile cost is over budget: 826 ms and 892 ms in two fresh-profile runs against the 800 ms cap. The rock program is at 329-330 ms (cap 350), points 206-240, glow 120-133, dust 76-78.", "severity": "functional", "evidence": "node tools/checks/shader_cost.mjs --q \"only=asteroids,post\" --top 15: sum 826 ms (rock-0-L0 330, asteroid-points-0 206, asteroid-glow 120, dust 76, belt band 42, belt ring 20); rerun: 892 ms", "fix": "1. Split the hero-only features (boulders, M-type GGX, 40-step macro shadow) into a second rock program used only by the 5 hero shapes, so the lattice materials compile a lighter variant.\n2. Move the impostor sphere-trace out of the points program into its own small program.\n3. Keep all of these created in create() so the warm-up still covers them."}, {"gap": "Seen from outside on arrival (~1-2 s, new cut t~192), the swarm is a small featureless grey smudge (~150x40 px) with no granularity or structure. It reads as a fog patch, not a swarm of rocks.", "severity": "cosmetic", "evidence": "shots/critic/asteroids/w2r1/cut2_192.png, film_195.png, outside.png", "fix": "Extend a sparse layer of the brightest PSF points (2-5k, sampled from swarmShape) to the outside view. Sharpen the density gradient at the envelope edge and raise filament contrast in the integrated glow so the cloud has lanes and grain."}, {"gap": "The belt ring never reads in the film. In the finale (401-410) its contribution is at most ~20 levels in a small annulus inside the Sun's glare, and the Kirkwood gaps are invisible. It reads well only in a hand-placed wide body view.", "severity": "cosmetic", "evidence": "shots/critic/asteroids/w2r1/fin_ring_compare.png (ring on/off crops), fin_404.png vs fin_noring_404.png (mean diff 0.12), belt_wide.png (body view where it does read)", "fix": "1. Add an exposure- and solid-angle-aware gain for the finale geometry.\n2. Carry more of the ring's light in the Kepler points (brighter 1 px PSF) rather than the soft band, and raise Kirkwood-lane contrast.\n3. Ask the director for a finale beat with the Sun off-centre so the annulus clears the glare (cross_piece)."}], "shots": ["shots/critic/asteroids/w2r1/sweep.png", "shots/critic/asteroids/w2r1/sweep2.png", "shots/critic/asteroids/w2r1/film_sheet.png", "shots/critic/asteroids/w2r1/film_195.png", "shots/critic/asteroids/w2r1/film_196.png", "shots/critic/asteroids/w2r1/film_197.png", "shots/critic/asteroids/w2r1/film_199.png", "shots/critic/asteroids/w2r1/film_203.png", "shots/critic/asteroids/w2r1/film_205.png", "shots/critic/asteroids/w2r1/film_209.png", "shots/critic/asteroids/w2r1/film_211.png", "shots/critic/asteroids/w2r1/film_214.png", "shots/critic/asteroids/w2r1/cut2_192.png", "shots/critic/asteroids/w2r1/cut2_193.png", "shots/critic/asteroids/w2r1/cut2_208.png", "shots/critic/asteroids/w2r1/enter.png", "shots/critic/asteroids/w2r1/in.png", "shots/critic/asteroids/w2r1/in40.png", "shots/critic/asteroids/w2r1/in90.png", "shots/critic/asteroids/w2r1/p70.png", "shots/critic/asteroids/w2r1/p130.png", "shots/critic/asteroids/w2r1/p130_glow_compare.png", "shots/critic/asteroids/w2r1/p180.png", "shots/critic/asteroids/w2r1/pm90.png", "shots/critic/asteroids/w2r1/outside.png", "shots/critic/asteroids/w2r1/eros16.png", "shots/critic/asteroids/w2r1/eros7.png", "shots/critic/asteroids/w2r1/ida_p40_fov14.png", "shots/critic/asteroids/w2r1/didy_p90_fov12.png", "shots/critic/asteroids/w2r1/tumble.png", "shots/critic/asteroids/w2r1/motion_198.png", "shots/critic/asteroids/w2r1/motion_quad_199.png", "shots/critic/asteroids/w2r1/zoom_199_seam.png", "shots/critic/asteroids/w2r1/zoom_205.png", "shots/critic/asteroids/w2r1/zoom_203.png", "shots/critic/asteroids/w2r1/zoom_197.png", "shots/critic/asteroids/w2r1/zoom_214_a.png", "shots/critic/asteroids/w2r1/zoom_214_b.png", "shots/critic/asteroids/w2r1/zoom_eros7_flaps.png", "shots/critic/asteroids/w2r1/dust_diff_211.png", "shots/critic/asteroids/w2r1/belt_wide.png", "shots/critic/asteroids/w2r1/fin_ring_compare.png", "shots/critic/asteroids/w2r1/finale_sheet.png"]}, "w2-fix1:neptune": "The three triage items are done in order, and the shader compile cost is under budget: **692 / 697 ms** in two runs of `shader_cost.mjs --q \"only=neptune,post\"`, down from 15,424 ms (wave-1 lead figure: 7,687 ms). One target is missed: band p2p is 64 DN, not the 80 asked for.\n\n**Triage results**\n\n1. **Banding.** Band contrast is roughly doubled, and the gas term now carries 85% of the pattern instead of 60%. New features:\n   - a broad dark southern belt with a sharp, wavy edge on its equator side at about -48.5\u00b0\n   - a bright collar at -71.5\u00b0 around a darker polar cap\n   - a stronger -30\u00b0 light zone, a darker equatorial belt, and stronger +22\u00b0 (light) and +50\u00b0 (dark) bands\n\n   On the central meridian of the required disc view this measures **p2p 64 DN, std 12.1** (wave 1: 53 / 8.9). The Voyager frame gives 87 / 14.4 with the same code. In that frame most of the p2p comes from the dark spot (-31 DN) and its bright companion (+45 DN), which happen to lie on the central strip; in our view the companions sit left of it. Pushing the bands far enough to reach 80 made the disc look painted, like Jupiter, so I stopped where the std roughly matches the reference. The southern belt and collar read clearly at `el=-35`.\n2. **Clouds and dark spot.**\n   - **Cloud profile:** soft core at least ~2 px wide, a 20% skirt, one continuous edge warp, no thresholds or ridged noise, so no stair-steps or striations.\n   - **Cloud colour:** core albedo capped below white, a blue tint from the gas above (stronger at thin edges), and a faint blue halo.\n   - **Dark spot:** the bright NE rim is replaced by a faint broken N/NW arc. The edge is softer and the west third darker. The SW hook is broader and brighter, with a broad streak under the spot's west half as in the reference. The deck inside the spot now rotates with the vortex instead of sliding through it, and the hairline-contour term is gone.\n3. **Triton terminator.** Relief now fades only toward the limb, so low sun shows it. Five horizon taps toward the Sun put pits into shadow and let ridge tops catch light just past the terminator. The terminator is now ragged at close and half-disc scale; at the twin-crescent scale it stays nearly smooth, because the relief is under a pixel there.\n4. **Partly done:** cantaloupe is now overlapping bowl-shaped dips with plains and dark spots, and the cap plume streaks are about 3x sparser and more varied.\n\n**How the compile cost came down.** I built an offline harness that compiles ANGLE's translated HLSL with `d3dcompiler_47` and times it; it reproduces the browser numbers. The main causes and fixes:\n- **Inlined simplex noise:** cost grows faster than linearly with each inlined copy (10 copies 200 ms, 40 copies 1.8 s). Minor noise now comes from one fetch of a 64\u00b3 noise texture generated in code; simplex survives only inside loops.\n- **Globe split:** the globe is two passes on the same geometry, an opaque deck and a blended cloud pass that also lays the cloud shadows.\n- **Cloud bookkeeping on the CPU:** all cloud slot setup moved into a new `cloudlist.js` that rebuilds the cloud list each frame. It depends only on `simDays`, so seeking stays deterministic.\n- **Static data out of shaders:** the band profile is a lookup texture, and Triton's plume streaks are drawn once at `create()` into a texture.\n- **Uploads and geometry:** textures are uploaded during `create()`, and the spheres keep positions only.\n- **Haze shells:** they used to switch `material.side` at runtime, which recompiles in three.js. They are now one double-sided program that discards the unwanted face.\n\nThe 1.15 R, 1.8 R and Triton close-ups all hold 240 fps with no console errors. Before/after shots of the required views (`shots/neptune/w2/before/` vs `shots/neptune/w2/final/`) show no regressions. In the current cut the hero disc is around t=360, the crescent 366\u2013382, and the twin crescents 384\u2013390.\n\n**Best shot commands**\n```bash\nnode tools/shot.mjs --t 360,374,386 --hud 0 --out shots/neptune/film.png\nnode tools/shot.mjs --q \"view=body&body=neptune&dist=3.5&phase=20&el=5\" --hud 0 --out shots/neptune/disc.png\nnode tools/shot.mjs --q \"view=body&body=neptune&dist=1.8&phase=20&el=-5&days=-0.012\" --hud 0 --out shots/neptune/spot.png\nnode tools/shot.mjs --q \"view=body&body=neptune&dist=3.5&phase=25&el=-35\" --hud 0 --out shots/neptune/south.png\nnode tools/shot.mjs --q \"view=body&body=neptune&target=triton&dist=3&phase=90\" --hud 0 --out shots/neptune/triton_half.png\nnode tools/shot.mjs --q \"view=body&body=neptune&target=triton&rel=neptune&phase=165&dist=8&aim=neptune&fov=45\" --hud 0 --out shots/neptune/tritonnep.png\n```\n\n**Remaining gaps**\n- Band p2p is 64 DN, not 80 (reason above).\n- The 1.15 R close-up is wispy but low-contrast, with no small convective cloud tufts yet.\n- Near the terminator, Triton's dips read a little like a dense crater field, and walled plains are not modelled.\n- The 1\u20133 px teal limb outline and the thick backlit haze glow at close range are untouched.\n- The compile margin is about 100 ms; under heavier machine load, earlier runs reached about 810 ms.\n\n`notes/neptune.md` is rewritten for this wave, including the root-cause notes and a pointer to the FXC harness in `C:\\Users\\danny\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-danny-fun\\6f6a9dda-209e-410f-8186-f810075dd4ce\\scratchpad\\fxc\\`.", "w2-fix1:mars": "All three triage items are fixed and checked in body views and in the film, and the piece's shader compile cost went from 11,763 ms to 588\u2013611 ms (best of several runs), inside the 800 ms budget with every program under 350 ms. The page loads with no console errors or warnings.\n\n**Compile cost** (`node tools/checks/shader_cost.mjs --q \"only=mars,post\"`, fresh profile; numbers swing about \u00b125% because other agents share the CPU):\n\n| program | before | now |\n|---|---|---|\n| Mars surface | 7,325 ms | 256\u2013268 ms |\n| Phobos (Deimos shares it) | 4,101 ms | 236\u2013242 ms |\n| haze shell | 336 ms | 95\u2013100 ms |\n\nThe cause was FXC inlining every analytic-noise call (22 call sites on the surface, 12 on the moons) and unrolling constant loops. Most of the savings came from:\n- loop bounds passed as uniforms;\n- a cheaper noise function, plus a small baked 3D noise texture (`src/bodies/mars/noise3d.js`);\n- load-time bakes of the geologic-age map and polar frost maps (`src/bodies/mars/bake.js`);\n- folding all close-range terrain layers into one table-driven loop.\n\nThe haze shell also used to compile a second program mid-film when the camera entered it; it now uses a single program. Frame time is about 4.2 ms.\n\nTo fit the budget I cut some close-range-only features: the sharp volcano calderas (MOLA still shows them, softer), wrinkle ridges, dunes, giant polygons, the Olympus aureole ridges (the source of the moir\u00e9) and crater rays, rampart aprons and wind streaks. None of these is visible in the film.\n\n**1. Dark markings and Valles Marineris.** The dark regions now read slate grey-brown with soft transitions, and the canyon floors are darkened using a MOLA-depth mask. In a view matching the Viking reference's geometry the disc statistics now match it closely:\n\n| | mean RGB | darkest 12% | brightest 30% |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Viking reference | 150/96/53 | 79/59/47 | 197/121/56 |\n| before | 154/95/47 | 102/57/24 | 183/119/65 |\n| now | 149/94/49 | 74/53/39 | 190/120/57 |\n\nLuminance percentiles are within about 5 levels. I lowered the dust optical depth from 0.15 to 0.12 so the orange haze veil doesn't lift the dark regions.\n\n**2. Polar cap.** The cap is now bright pinkish-white (albedo 0.68) and stays bright at low sun. Its edge is crisp and follows the terrain instead of a noise stipple. Crater floors frost over. The dark dashes and star-shaped spot near the pole were spurious terrain shadows from the map's degenerate rows there; shadows now fade out above 81\u201385\u00b0.\n\n**3. Phobos and Deimos.** Phobos now shows brighter and redder albedo regions and fresh crater rims, with faint groove lines visible at film size. It also has a little limb darkening, so it reads as a lump rather than an egg. Mars-shine is now physically scaled and lights the night side. The tick artifacts on the crescent are gone; they came from the self-shadow calculation. Deimos has a smoother outline and less blotchy albedo.\n\n**Best shots** (all in `shots/mars/w2/`):\n- `fin/11_vmref.png` \u2014 histogram-check view: `view=body&body=mars&dist=4.0&phase=15&el=12&days=0.36`\n- `fin/01_disc.png` \u2014 required disc: `view=body&body=mars&dist=3.2&phase=30&el=10`\n- `fin/17_capfilm.png` \u2014 cap as the film shows it: `view=body&body=mars&dist=2.6&phase=85&el=40`\n- `fin/05_phfront.png` \u2014 Phobos in front of Mars: `view=body&body=mars&target=phobos&rel=mars&phase=172&dist=6&aim=mars&fov=50`\n- `fin/22_limb95.png` \u2014 limb haze: `view=body&body=mars&dist=1.25&phase=95&el=0&fov=50&look=0,22`\n- Film frames (current cut: disc 168\u2013180 s, Phobos crossing 174\u2013178 s, cap 182\u2013186 s): `--t 172,176,178,182,184 --hud 0 --out \"shots/mars/w2/film/j_{t}.png\"`\n- `before_after.png` \u2014 side-by-side of the six required views\n\n**Remaining gaps:**\n- Close range is still the weakest regime, and the budget cuts made it slightly thinner.\n- The required view at `dist=1.12&phase=95` doesn't contain the limb. Twilight now extends about 20\u00b0 past the terminator and is present in the HDR, but at the film's exposure that view still looks nearly black.\n- The darkest markings are still a bit lighter and warmer than the contrast-stretched Viking image (Acidalia about 90/65/51 vs 66/54/51).\n- Phobos is correctly 3\u20135\u00d7 darker than Mars, so how well its detail reads at 60\u201390 px depends on exposure.\n\n`notes/mars.md` is rewritten. Its requests to the integrator: about +0.5 stop of exposure during the Phobos transit, a softer toe in post for dark-but-lit bodies, and replacing the required limb view with the `22_limb95` command above.", "w2-critic1:sun": {"floorMet": true, "score": 7, "compileMs": 753, "summary": "Scored on the wave-2 sun code; source files were unchanged through the review, and re-rendered film frames matched the earlier ones pixel for pixel.\n\nWhere the Sun is in the current cut (430.4 s long):\n- Intro corona at 10-14 s.\n- Sun chapter at 14-42 s: wide corona 14-18, full disc 20-26, close photosphere and limb 28-40, departure 40-42.\n- As a point source or glare: Earth sunrise around 126 s, Neptune 372-390 s, finale 396-414 s.\n\n**What holds up:**\n- **Disc colour and limb darkening.** Measured against the SDO HMI reference, the disc colour and radial darkening profile are a close match. Centre is about (246,179,20) against HMI's (251,173,26). Green/red falls from 0.73 at the centre to 0.42 at r=0.9, against 0.69 to 0.43 in HMI.\n- **Granulation motion.** It evolves smoothly with no popping. On the skim, frames 1 s apart correlate at 0.973-0.977. On a close view in 0.25 s steps, correlation is 0.996 and no pixel jumps by more than 24/255.\n- **Granulation detail.** It holds up to about 0.03 R altitude.\n- **Sunspots up close.** A tight fov-8 view shows SST-like penumbral filaments with bright heads and umbral dots.\n- **Faculae.** Plage shows near the limb group.\n- **Prominences animate.** Their structure drains over time.\n- **Far field.** From 150 to 1000 radii, and in the Jupiter, Neptune and finale shots, the Sun becomes a clean HDR star that post turns into a flare.\n- **GPU cost.** Skim 0.85 ms, screen-filling disc 0.66-0.96 ms, both well under the 3 ms budget.\n- **No errors from the Sun.** The Sun module logged no errors. One Neptune shader error and one earth module failure came from other builders editing at the same time.\n\n**Compile cost.**\n- With the Neptune view that keeps the Sun out of the first frame, the total is 753 ms. Prominence is 320, photosphere 293, corona 91, chromosphere 49. That is inside the 800/350 budget, but with little margin.\n- The exact command in the task (`only=sun,post` and the default view) reports only about 11 ms. That view draws the Sun before the measurement starts, so the number is wrong.\n\n**The tells that keep it from SVS grade are in the hero close-up (28-40 s), where they are large on screen:**\n1. **Prominences.** They read as crimson confetti made of short, disconnected bar fragments, and as ring/donut shapes at full-disc scale.\n2. **Leader sunspot.** At film scale it is an ink blot: a flat-black umbra split by a 1-px straight 'light bridge' seam, and a smooth, too-dark brown penumbra (about 0.25 of quiet Sun on screen) with a dark outer contour. No filaments can be seen.\n3. **Spicules.** They form a picket fence of uniform straight spikes along the limb.\n4. **Sky above the limb.** It is grey, blotchy smoke instead of black or a radially structured pearly corona.\n5. **Granulation at film distance.** It reads as fairly uniform cracked-paving Voronoi cells.\n6. **Wide corona.** It is a convincing eclipse-photo look, but its streamers are broad fans of uniform brushed-hair texture, without clear helmet bulbs, cavities or polar plumes.\n\nOverall it is impressive, with a few clear tells.", "gaps": [{"gap": "Prominences in the hero window (on screen 14-42 s, very large at 30-38 s) read as crimson confetti rather than H-alpha plasma. The threads are short, disconnected bar or rice-grain fragments with no coherent field-aligned strands. At full-disc scale (20-26 s) the arches render as near-closed rings or donuts. In the film skim the prominence sits at the horizon, cut off by the top of the frame, not arching overhead as the brief asks.", "severity": "functional", "evidence": "C:/Users/danny/fun/space-animation-v1/shots/critic/sun/w2r1/crop_t36_prom.png, crop_t30_prom.png (film_t30.png), crop_limb_prom.png, x_promzoom.png, crop_t24_promL.png (ring shape), film_t24.png", "fix": "Build strands in rope-aligned coordinates (u along the rope's tangent, v across it). Use anisotropic noise with an along-strand correlation length of at least 8-15 thread widths, and give brightness a smooth, low-frequency modulation along u instead of hard segment gaps, so each thread stays continuous for most of the arch. Bend threads with the rope (no straight dash segments). Break the closed rings: make arches asymmetric, with one leg brighter and fatter and the apex sagging or dipped (a Kippenhahn-Schl\u00fcter dip). Add flame-like hedgerow profiles, with vertical threads rising from a bright base, so the full-disc silhouette reads as tufts and arches, not O shapes. Keep the diffuse envelope but make it continuous along the threads. Ask the director to frame one box so it actually arches over the camera in the 30-38 s skim."}, {"gap": "The leader sunspot is the focal point of the 32-38 s skim (about 150 px wide) and does not read as a filamentary sunspot. The umbra is flat pure black (display G about 3/255). A 1-px straight hairline 'light bridge' cuts across it and looks like a rendering seam. The penumbra is a smooth, too-dark leather-brown ring (display G 26-30 against 113 for nearby quiet Sun, about 0.25), with almost no radial filaments and a dark contour at its outer edge. At full disc (20-26 s) the same contour makes every spot a bullseye.", "severity": "functional", "evidence": "C:/Users/danny/fun/space-animation-v1/shots/critic/sun/w2r1/crop_t36_spot.png, crop_skim_spot.png, crop_limb_bigspot.png, crop_t24_spots.png; compare x_spot_tight.png (filaments do exist at fov 8)", "fix": "(1) Footprint-adaptive filaments. Choose the azimuthal filament count from the projected penumbra circumference so the spacing never drops below about 2.5 px, e.g. N = clamp(circ_px/3, 24, 360), and keep the filament contrast. Today the filaments are band-limited away at mid range when they should be resampled coarser. (2) Lift the penumbra out of the hero contrast exponent. Apply the 1.9 large-scale exponent to quiet-Sun limb and facular luminance only, and composite the spot intensities (inner penumbra about 0.85, outer about 0.7, umbra 0.08 at the edge to 0.04 at the core) after it, so the penumbra lands at about 0.7-0.8 of local quiet Sun on screen. (3) Remove the undershoot at the outer boundary: use a monotonic step from penumbra to photosphere over about 1 px footprint. (4) Make the light bridge at least 2.5 px of footprint, curved and granular, or fade it out below that width. Add a gentle radial gradient and footprint-filtered umbral dots so the umbra is not a flat black cutout."}, {"gap": "The spicule fringe reads as a comb or picket fence. Along the whole limb in 28-40 s, and especially at grazing angles, the spikes are isolated, uniform, straight, parallel and sharply tapered, standing over a grey sky. Real limb spicules form a dense, overlapping, inclined forest that grows out of a continuous ragged bright band.", "severity": "cosmetic", "evidence": "C:/Users/danny/fun/space-animation-v1/shots/critic/sun/w2r1/crop_graze_spicules.png (x_graze.png), crop_t36_limbleft.png, crop_skim_limb.png, crop_full_toplimb.png", "fix": "Integrate spicules along the line of sight as a density field instead of drawing sparse strokes. Use a continuous base layer with an exponential height profile (scale about 2-3 Mm) and ragged top noise, then superpose several hundred thin jets per radian of limb. Give the jets inclinations of plus or minus 25 degrees, exponentially distributed heights (mean about 5 Mm with a tail to 12), lognormal brightness and slight curvature. Overlap along the line of sight should merge them into a 'burning prairie', with only a few tall ones standing clear. Fade jet opacity smoothly by footprint so mid-distance views show the band, not individual hairs."}, {"gap": "In the close views (30-38 s) the sky above the limb is a grey, isotropically mottled haze, display about 20-28/255 with blotches, reaching 45-60/255 at grazing angles. It reads as smoke or fog rather than space or a pearly inner corona, and it lowers the contrast of the limb and the prominences.", "severity": "cosmetic", "evidence": "C:/Users/danny/fun/space-animation-v1/shots/critic/sun/w2r1/film_t34.png, film_t36.png (top band), x_graze.png, req_skim.png", "fix": "Drive the close-view uSkyLift from the same radial ray, streamer and plume field as the wide corona: sample the corona's direction field in heliocentric angle and stretch it radially, so any structure is radial striation and not isotropic noise. Warm it slightly (pearl, about (1,.96,.9)). Reduce it so the sky beyond about 0.05 R falls to 5% or less of display, keeping the frame black behind the prominences."}, {"gap": "Granulation at the film's skim distance (about 0.18 R altitude) reads as a fairly uniform crazy-paving or cracked-mud Voronoi. Cell sizes look similar, lanes have nearly constant width, and there is a strong lemon-yellow-to-orange hue swing between cells and lanes. The supergranular network and mesogranular patchiness the notes describe cannot be seen at film scale.", "severity": "cosmetic", "evidence": "C:/Users/danny/fun/space-animation-v1/shots/critic/sun/w2r1/crop_skim_gran.png, film_t34.png, film_t36.png", "fix": "Widen the granule size distribution, e.g. lognormal sigma about 0.45, with a few 2-2.5 Mm 'exploders' per field. Vary lane width and depth per edge, with narrower and darker lanes at triple junctions. Raise the supergranular and network modulation at mid footprints to about 4-6% RMS, with faint bright network chains 20-30 Mm apart, so the field has large-scale order. Reduce the hue swing by blending hue-preserving at granulation frequencies (or have post lower HERO.target, as the notes request)."}, {"gap": "The wide corona (10-18 s, and the required dist=14 view) gives a good eclipse-photo impression, but it is built from broad fans of uniform brushed-hair texture. There are no readable helmet streamers with a bulb, dark cavity and cusp, and no distinct polar plumes. The fine-ray texture has about the same density and contrast from 1.1 R to the frame edge, which looks procedural.", "severity": "cosmetic", "evidence": "C:/Users/danny/fun/space-animation-v1/shots/critic/sun/w2r1/req_wide.png, crop_wide_streamer.png, crop_wide_inner.png, film_t16.png", "fix": "Make two or three streamers clearly helmet-shaped: a brightened bulb at 1.2-2 R over a darker prominence cavity, narrowing to a cusp at about 2.5 R and then a thin stalk. Give the polar coronal holes discrete, curved plumes that diverge with latitude. Let the ray noise's angular frequency rise with radius (the rays narrow and fragment outward) while its contrast falls."}, {"gap": "In extreme close-ups (0.008 R altitude) the granules become soft, out-of-focus blobs with almost no interior structure. This is not in the current cut.", "severity": "cosmetic", "evidence": "C:/Users/danny/fun/space-animation-v1/shots/critic/sun/w2r1/x_gran_extreme.png", "fix": "Add one more footprint-gated octave below the granule scale: sub-granular dark dots, fine lane filigree, bright points of about 100-150 km, and edge sharpening of the lane profile. Fade it in only when one granule covers more than about 150 px."}, {"gap": "The shader compile margin is thin: 753 ms total against an 800 budget, and the prominence program is 320 ms against the 350 per-object limit. Separately, the measurement command in the brief (only=sun,post with the default view) reports about 11 ms, because the default view draws the Sun before the measurement starts.", "severity": "cosmetic", "evidence": "node tools/checks/shader_cost.mjs --q \"only=sun,post&view=body&body=neptune&phase=0&dist=3\" --top 15 gives 320 prominence, 293 photosphere, 91 corona, 49 chromosphere; the default-view run gave 11 ms total (scratchpad shader_cost_default.txt)", "fix": "Before adding the thread and spicule work above, cut the prominence program, where the notes put arches at about 240 ms. Share one strand-noise call site between arches and hedgerows, move per-box shape parameters into uniforms instead of branches, and cap the thread loop with a uniform bound. Ask the integrator to make shader_cost.mjs start from a view with no bodies in frame, so per-piece numbers are honest."}], "shots": ["C:/Users/danny/fun/space-animation-v1/shots/critic/sun/w2r1/sweep.png", "C:/Users/danny/fun/space-animation-v1/shots/critic/sun/w2r1/film_sheet.png", "C:/Users/danny/fun/space-animation-v1/shots/critic/sun/w2r1/film_t16.png", "C:/Users/danny/fun/space-animation-v1/shots/critic/sun/w2r1/film_t24.png", "C:/Users/danny/fun/space-animation-v1/shots/critic/sun/w2r1/film_t30.png", "C:/Users/danny/fun/space-animation-v1/shots/critic/sun/w2r1/film_t34.png", "C:/Users/danny/fun/space-animation-v1/shots/critic/sun/w2r1/film_t36.png", "C:/Users/danny/fun/space-animation-v1/shots/critic/sun/w2r1/req_full.png", "C:/Users/danny/fun/space-animation-v1/shots/critic/sun/w2r1/req_full_defexp.png", "C:/Users/danny/fun/space-animation-v1/shots/critic/sun/w2r1/req_wide.png", "C:/Users/danny/fun/space-animation-v1/shots/critic/sun/w2r1/req_limb.png", "C:/Users/danny/fun/space-animation-v1/shots/critic/sun/w2r1/req_skim.png", "C:/Users/danny/fun/space-animation-v1/shots/critic/sun/w2r1/motion_sheet.png", "C:/Users/danny/fun/space-animation-v1/shots/critic/sun/w2r1/crop_motion_strip.png", "C:/Users/danny/fun/space-animation-v1/shots/critic/sun/w2r1/req_far_neptune.png", "C:/Users/danny/fun/space-animation-v1/shots/critic/sun/w2r1/req_far_jupiter.png", "C:/Users/danny/fun/space-animation-v1/shots/critic/sun/w2r1/far_neptune20.png", "C:/Users/danny/fun/space-animation-v1/shots/critic/sun/w2r1/x_gran_close.png", "C:/Users/danny/fun/space-animation-v1/shots/critic/sun/w2r1/x_gran_extreme.png", "C:/Users/danny/fun/space-animation-v1/shots/critic/sun/w2r1/x_graze.png", "C:/Users/danny/fun/space-animation-v1/shots/critic/sun/w2r1/x_promzoom.png", "C:/Users/danny/fun/space-animation-v1/shots/critic/sun/w2r1/x_spot_tight.png", "C:/Users/danny/fun/space-animation-v1/shots/critic/sun/w2r1/x_pole.png", "C:/Users/danny/fun/space-animation-v1/shots/critic/sun/w2r1/x_inside_side.png", "C:/Users/danny/fun/space-animation-v1/shots/critic/sun/w2r1/x_dist150.png", "C:/Users/danny/fun/space-animation-v1/shots/critic/sun/w2r1/x_dist475.png", "C:/Users/danny/fun/space-animation-v1/shots/critic/sun/w2r1/x_dist1000.png", "C:/Users/danny/fun/space-animation-v1/shots/critic/sun/w2r1/prom_motion_sheet.png", "C:/Users/danny/fun/space-animation-v1/shots/critic/sun/w2r1/filmpt_sheet.png", "C:/Users/danny/fun/space-animation-v1/shots/critic/sun/w2r1/crop_t36_spot.png", "C:/Users/danny/fun/space-animation-v1/shots/critic/sun/w2r1/crop_t36_prom.png", "C:/Users/danny/fun/space-animation-v1/shots/critic/sun/w2r1/crop_t24_promL.png", "C:/Users/danny/fun/space-animation-v1/shots/critic/sun/w2r1/crop_t24_spots.png", "C:/Users/danny/fun/space-animation-v1/shots/critic/sun/w2r1/crop_graze_spicules.png", "C:/Users/danny/fun/space-animation-v1/shots/critic/sun/w2r1/crop_skim_gran.png", "C:/Users/danny/fun/space-animation-v1/shots/critic/sun/w2r1/crop_limb_bigspot.png", "C:/Users/danny/fun/space-animation-v1/shots/critic/sun/w2r1/crop_wide_streamer.png"]}, "w2-critic1:saturn": {"floorMet": true, "score": 7.2, "compileMs": 782, "summary": "Saturn (wave 2, round 1). Images are in C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\shots\\critic\\saturn\\w2r1\\. The director has re-cut the film, so the windows have moved. Saturn now runs about 264-322 s: approach on the lit (south) face 264-276, close pass 0.16 R below the rings over the outer B ring / Cassini Division 276-284, ring-plane crossing with the Sun in frame 285-288, eclipse from the unlit (north) face at about 1 R above the plane and exposure x10.5 from 288 to 303, then Titan 304-322. I checked the camera geometry against the ring plane with a page-side script (sun elevation -8.16 deg). No \"module saturn failed\" lines and no Saturn console errors appeared. A one-off shader syntax error and an \"earth\" module failure showed up mid-session; both came from other pieces being edited live and did not recur on rerun.\n\nWhat is right:\n- Cassini-grade ring structure on the lit face: C plateaus, B ringlets, a crisp Cassini Division, Encke gap and F ring, with no aliasing at approach distance.\n- The planet's shadow lies across the rings, and the ring shadows on the globe are graded by tau.\n- Ring brightness is about half the globe's at the 8 deg sun, which is physically right.\n- The unlit-face reversal is present: the B ring is dark, the C ring and Cassini Division are lighter.\n- The eclipse has a thin refracted limb ring, an orange ringshine band on the night side, a diffuse E/G-ring halo, and a physically correct shadow wedge on the near A ring and E ring.\n- Titan has an orange haze, a detached blue layer, and a superb backlit haze ring.\n- Mimas shows Saturn's shadow eclipse; Rhea and Dione are believable; the small and tiny discs read well.\n- The hexagon and vortex exist (I checked with a 2017 epoch render). In 2026 they sit in polar night; per the triage note I did not weigh this.\n\nWhat keeps it off a 9:\n- The eclipse rings read as neutral grey lit-face bands, with razor-sharp black shadow polygons.\n- The close-pass ring surface is a perfectly concentric, brushed-metal streak field with no azimuthal structure (no wakes, no density waves).\n- Titan's lit disc is flat, saturated orange, and the haze crescent carries screen-locked white-noise grain.\n- At 0.12 R the globe turns into a soft smear.\n- Shader compile is over budget: the saturn-globe program took 430, 365 and 363 ms in three runs (more than 350 every time); the piece total was 833, 782 and 750 ms, median 782.\n\nNote: the required \"unlit\" classic view (phase=30, el=20) actually sits 0.04 R below the ring plane on the lit face, so it shows the rings edge-on. I judged the unlit face at el=40 instead (h=+1.6 R).", "gaps": [{"gap": "Eclipse (288-303 s, about 15 s full frame, the chapter's key shot) does not reach the reference's luminous forward-scatter look. At x10.5 exposure the A ring, Cassini Division and inner B/C read as flat neutral grey-white bands like a lit face, with no warm tint and no soft glow. The planet's shadow cuts the near A ring and E-ring halo as razor-sharp, pure-black straight-edged trapezoids. The geometry is right, but there is no penumbra and no reddened refraction band, so Saturn looks as if it sits on a black cut-out pedestal. The E-ring glow is neutral grey rather than bluish.", "severity": "functional", "evidence": "C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\shots\\critic\\saturn\\w2r1\\film_t294.png, film_t290.png, film_t302.png, film_t303.png, zoom_t294_leftansa.png, req_eclipse.png vs bar\\refs\\saturn_cassini_backlit.jpg", "fix": "In rings.js, on the side away from the sun at high phase, replace the reflected (lit-face) term with explicit diffuse transmission T(mu,mu0) = tau*exp(-tau/mu0)*... plus a strong HG forward lobe (g about 0.7-0.95). Weight that lobe by a per-radius dust fraction (high in the F ring, D ring, C-ring plateaus, Cassini Division, Encke region and A-ring outer edge; low in the B ring), and tint it warm (about 1.0, 0.88, 0.75) so the rings glow rather than look lit. Give the E/G halo a lambda^-n colour (about 0.8, 0.9, 1.15). Make the planet-shadow test soft: shadow = smoothstep(-w, w, rayAltitudeAboveOblateLimb) with w of about 0.001 times the distance along the ray from the limb (the solar disc). Add a thin refraction/extinction band (about 0.01 R) that transmits reddened light (x vec3(1, 0.5, 0.25)). Add about 0.3-1% ringshine from the sunlit rings into the shadow so the shadowed ring reads as a dark ring, not a hole."}, {"gap": "The close pass under the rings (276-284 s, full frame at 0.16 R) and the required 0.05 R skims show a ring surface that is a purely radial, perfectly concentric streak field, like brushed metal or vinyl. There is no azimuthal structure at all: no self-gravity wake texture or A-ring quadrant asymmetry, no density or bending wave trains, no spokes. The nearest ringlets are several pixels soft. The brief explicitly asks for azimuthal streaks/wakes and density waves.", "severity": "functional", "evidence": "C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\shots\\critic\\saturn\\w2r1\\zoom_skim_4frames.png, zoom_skimlit_near.png, film_t278.png, film_t280.png, req_skim_lit_el23.png, req_skim_unlit_el26.png", "fix": "Add a 2D (r, phi) layer in the ring fragment shader, all footprint-filtered by fwidth(r) and fwidth(phi*r):\n- Anisotropic wake noise in the A ring and outer B ring, elongated about 20-25 deg from the azimuth and trailing.\n- A cos(2(phi - phi_obs)) brightness asymmetry of about 10-15% in the A ring.\n- Chirped sinusoid density waves in tau at the main resonances (Janus 2:1 near 96,200 km; Janus/Prometheus 4:3, 5:4, 6:5 and so on in the A ring, 115,000-137,000 km), with wavelength shrinking outward and damped over about 100-300 km.\n- Faint radial spokes over the B ring.\nSharpen the near field by adding a high-frequency procedural ringlet octave below the LUT resolution (about 2-4 km) that fades in only when fwidth(r) is under 2 km."}, {"gap": "Titan (304-322 s, about 18 s of the film) has two problems.\n- The lit disc is a flat, uniformly saturated orange ball. The coded polar hood, collar and north-south asymmetry (multipliers 0.89-0.98) are invisible on screen.\n- The backlit haze crescent carries screen-locked white-noise speckle where it fades into the night side. The cause is per-pixel gl_FragCoord jitter at moons.js:381 with only 44 steps: about 14% pixel-to-pixel noise at mid-dark tones, and it stays fixed on screen while the moon moves. The crescent also reads as discrete orange/cream/blue stripes rather than one continuous gradient.", "severity": "cosmetic", "evidence": "C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\shots\\critic\\saturn\\w2r1\\zoom_titan_t316_ring.png, film_t316.png, film_t312.png, zoom_titan_t312_limb.png, req_titan_p60.png, req_titan_p160.png", "fix": "Remove the jitter. Integrate each segment analytically, using the exact exponential integral for exponentially varying density within a step, and concentrate steps around closest approach with a uniform-bound loop, so the integrator needs no dither. If dither must stay, use a blue-noise texture at 1/4 amplitude. Raise the hood/asymmetry contrast to visible levels: the north hood about 20-30% darker and redder, and a north-south albedo step of about 8-10%. Desaturate the disc toward Cassini natural colour (pale orange-tan, around sRGB 0.82, 0.62, 0.38), and add slight limb brightening from the haze."}, {"gap": "Shader compile cost is over the contract budget. The saturn-globe program took 430, 365 and 363 ms across three fresh-profile runs (budget: 350 ms per object). The piece total was 833, 782 and 750 ms, median 782 (budget: 800). The mimas moon shader takes another 248-268 ms. The ring programs report 0 ms, so their real cost may be hidden by an earlier compile.", "severity": "functional", "evidence": "node tools/checks/shader_cost.mjs --q \"only=saturn,post\" --top 15 (three runs: 833/782/750 ms total; saturn-globe 430/365/363 ms; mimas 254/268/248 ms)", "fix": "In globe.js:\n- Convert every constant-bound fbm/turbulence/vortex loop to uniform-bound loops (for i < uOctaves), and keep one call site per large noise function.\n- Put the hexagon/vortex and the close-range detail octaves behind a uniform-gated branch whose iteration count comes from a uniform; FXC does not unroll that.\n- Drop octaves by screen footprint.\nIn moons.js, apply the same treatment to the crater loop for the shared moon shader. Re-measure with shader_cost.mjs and keep the globe under 300 ms for margin."}, {"gap": "In a close pass over the globe at about 0.12 R altitude, the cloud deck turns into a blurry, low-contrast olive-brown smear with one oversmoothed stretched swirl. The contract requires no blur at 0.05-0.3 R. The film's closest globe range is about 1 R, where the texture still holds, so this does not hurt the current cut.", "severity": "cosmetic", "evidence": "C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\shots\\critic\\saturn\\w2r1\\x_limb_closeup.png (dist=1.12, look=-18)", "fix": "Add a footprint-faded high-frequency layer: zonal-shear streaks advected with curl noise, small convective cells, and bright pinpoint storms. Set the octave count from fwidth(position) so detail keeps appearing down to about 2 km/px. Keep the contrast subtle (a few percent) but crisp, and do not let the colour darken at close range."}, {"gap": "Enceladus is covered edge to edge in uniform parallel wavy grooves, which reads as a corduroy or fingerprint texture. The real moon has heavily cratered northern terrain, smoother plains, and four tiger stripes near the south pole. Mimas, Rhea and Dione are fine.", "severity": "cosmetic", "evidence": "C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\shots\\critic\\saturn\\w2r1\\x_enceladus.png", "fix": "Mask the groove field by region: dense craters above about 30 deg N, a smooth, sparsely cratered belt, and grooves only in the south-polar terrain, with four darker-edged tiger-stripe fractures (about 130 km long, 35 km apart) poleward of 60 deg S. Vary groove spacing and orientation with low-frequency noise."}, {"gap": "The north polar hexagon and vortex exist and look plausible: a 2017-epoch render shows a hexagonal teal-grey jet band with a spiral and a dark eye, though the eye is a flat disc. In 2026 the hexagon is not visible even at x4 exposure, which is consistent with polar night. Per the triage note this is not weighted, but no twilight scatter exists at the polar terminator.", "severity": "cosmetic", "evidence": "C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\shots\\critic\\saturn\\w2r1\\req_hexagon.png, x_hexagon_exp4.png, x_hexagon_2017.png, zoom_hexagon_2017.png", "fix": "Optional. Add a high-altitude haze twilight term (single-scatter up to about 5 deg beyond the terminator) so the sunward edge of the 78 deg N jet catches grazing light. The noon sun at 78 N is about 3.8 deg up in 2026. Also give the central eye a soft radial gradient and spiral cloud arms instead of a flat dark disc."}], "shots": ["C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\shots\\critic\\saturn\\w2r1\\sweep.png", "C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\shots\\critic\\saturn\\w2r1\\film_sheet.png", "C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\shots\\critic\\saturn\\w2r1\\film_t266.png", "C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\shots\\critic\\saturn\\w2r1\\film_t269.png", "C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\shots\\critic\\saturn\\w2r1\\film_t272.png", "C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\shots\\critic\\saturn\\w2r1\\film_t274.png", "C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\shots\\critic\\saturn\\w2r1\\film_t276.png", "C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\shots\\critic\\saturn\\w2r1\\film_t278.png", "C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\shots\\critic\\saturn\\w2r1\\film_t280.png", "C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\shots\\critic\\saturn\\w2r1\\film_t282.png", "C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\shots\\critic\\saturn\\w2r1\\film_t284.png", "C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\shots\\critic\\saturn\\w2r1\\film_t286.png", "C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\shots\\critic\\saturn\\w2r1\\film_t287.png", "C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\shots\\critic\\saturn\\w2r1\\film_t288.png", "C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\shots\\critic\\saturn\\w2r1\\film_t290.png", "C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\shots\\critic\\saturn\\w2r1\\film_t294.png", "C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\shots\\critic\\saturn\\w2r1\\film_t298.png", "C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\shots\\critic\\saturn\\w2r1\\film_t302.png", "C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\shots\\critic\\saturn\\w2r1\\film_t303.png", "C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\shots\\critic\\saturn\\w2r1\\film_t304.png", "C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\shots\\critic\\saturn\\w2r1\\film_t308.png", "C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\shots\\critic\\saturn\\w2r1\\film_t312.png", "C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\shots\\critic\\saturn\\w2r1\\film_t316.png", "C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\shots\\critic\\saturn\\w2r1\\film_t318.png", "C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\shots\\critic\\saturn\\w2r1\\film_t320.png", "C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\shots\\critic\\saturn\\w2r1\\req_classic_lit.png", "C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\shots\\critic\\saturn\\w2r1\\req_classic_unlit.png", "C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\shots\\critic\\saturn\\w2r1\\req_shadow.png", "C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\shots\\critic\\saturn\\w2r1\\req_eclipse.png", "C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\shots\\critic\\saturn\\w2r1\\req_skim_asgiven_b.png", "C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\shots\\critic\\saturn\\w2r1\\req_skim_lit_el23.png", "C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\shots\\critic\\saturn\\w2r1\\req_skim_unlit_el26.png", "C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\shots\\critic\\saturn\\w2r1\\req_hexagon.png", "C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\shots\\critic\\saturn\\w2r1\\req_titan_p60.png", "C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\shots\\critic\\saturn\\w2r1\\req_titan_p160.png", "C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\shots\\critic\\saturn\\w2r1\\x_tiny_600R.png", "C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\shots\\critic\\saturn\\w2r1\\x_small_60R.png", "C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\shots\\critic\\saturn\\w2r1\\x_unlit_face_p30_el40.png", "C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\shots\\critic\\saturn\\w2r1\\x_unlit_face_p120_el45.png", "C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\shots\\critic\\saturn\\w2r1\\x_limb_closeup.png", "C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\shots\\critic\\saturn\\w2r1\\x_ansa_closeup_lit.png", "C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\shots\\critic\\saturn\\w2r1\\x_hexagon_exp4.png", "C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\shots\\critic\\saturn\\w2r1\\x_hexagon_p90_exp4.png", "C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\shots\\critic\\saturn\\w2r1\\x_hexagon_2017.png", "C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\shots\\critic\\saturn\\w2r1\\x_pole_sunward_exp3.png", "C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\shots\\critic\\saturn\\w2r1\\x_enceladus.png", "C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\shots\\critic\\saturn\\w2r1\\x_mimas.png", "C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\shots\\critic\\saturn\\w2r1\\x_rhea.png", "C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\shots\\critic\\saturn\\w2r1\\x_dione_tethys.png", "C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\shots\\critic\\saturn\\w2r1\\zoom_t294_shadowwedge.png", "C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\shots\\critic\\saturn\\w2r1\\zoom_t294_leftansa.png", "C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\shots\\critic\\saturn\\w2r1\\zoom_t294_globe.png", "C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\shots\\critic\\saturn\\w2r1\\zoom_shadow_x4.png", "C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\shots\\critic\\saturn\\w2r1\\zoom_skim_4frames.png", "C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\shots\\critic\\saturn\\w2r1\\zoom_skimlit_near.png", "C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\shots\\critic\\saturn\\w2r1\\zoom_skimunlit_x3.png", "C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\shots\\critic\\saturn\\w2r1\\zoom_t272_ansa.png", "C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\shots\\critic\\saturn\\w2r1\\zoom_t276_topleft.png", "C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\shots\\critic\\saturn\\w2r1\\zoom_titan_t316_ring.png", "C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\shots\\critic\\saturn\\w2r1\\zoom_titan_t312_limb.png", "C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\shots\\critic\\saturn\\w2r1\\zoom_hexagon_2017.png", "C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\shots\\critic\\saturn\\w2r1\\zoom_tiny_600R.png", "C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\shots\\critic\\saturn\\w2r1\\zoom_small_60R.png", "C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\shots\\critic\\saturn\\w2r1\\zoom_unlit_nearring.png"]}, "w2-critic1:venus": {"floorMet": true, "score": 6.8, "summary": "Venus (wave 2, round 1) meets its floor. It is a solid, physically motivated piece with a few visible tells. In the current cut the Venus chapter runs about 70-92 s: 74 s glint, 75-77 s a small then growing day disc, 78-82 s a near-full day disc filling half the frame, 83-85 s the half phase, and 86-91 s the crescent held under the director's +1.3 EV, which has landed (film.js EV.venusCrescent).\n\nWhat works:\n- The day deck is creamy. Its contrast matches the Mariner 10 reference closely: high-pass luminance p5/p95 is 0.975/1.027 against 0.972/1.035 in the reference.\n- Disc brightness is right: about 224 sRGB at the centre at phase 20.\n- The half phase has a broad, smooth, near-Lambert terminator and a black night side.\n- The cusps extend along the night limb (t84).\n- The ring of light is now complete and cream-coloured at phase 172 and 178, about 2x brighter on the Sun side, with no blue fringe. It is the most beautiful Venus moment in the film (t86-90).\n- Super-rotation is correct: cross-correlation gives -57 px per 0.05 days at the disc centre, against about 56 px expected for a 4-day period. The drift is coherent at all latitudes and the fine detail slowly regenerates.\n- Small discs and the glint hand-off are clean. No page errors and no fallback was shown.\n- Shader compile is well within budget on a fresh profile: deck 251/269 ms, haze 88/94 ms, total 339/363 ms over two runs.\n\nWhat keeps it below 7:\n- The day disc reads as a pale banded ball. Streaks are almost purely zonal and stacked like brush strokes, with a continuous tan belt, and the sideways Y and bow arcs are not legible in film frames.\n- The dim side warms to brown/taupe, while the reference goes neutral to cool grey-blue. The whole deck is warmer than the reference.\n- At film scale the fine domain-warp detail shows as wood-grain/marble contours.\n- The crescent and ring carry a broad inner rim glow inside the night limb.\n- Close range (not in the film) lacks a luminous limb haze band and aerial perspective.\n\nOne false alarm, checked and discarded: an apparent vertical seam in the look=38/50 limb views is not in the PNG data (column-averaged colour is perfectly smooth). It is not a gap.\n\nThe hexagonal lens ghost drawn over the disc in the crescent frames belongs to post, not Venus.", "gaps": [{"gap": "The day-disc cloud morphology reads as a pale banded (Jupiter-like) ball rather than Venus. Streaks are nearly perfectly zonal and stacked like horizontal brush strokes, with one continuous tan equatorial belt. The sideways Y, the poleward-converging oblique streaks and the curved bow/chevron arcs that dominate the Mariner 10 reference are not legible. This is the biggest and longest Venus image in the film (t77-82, disc filling half the frame), and the brief's 'subtle banded/Y-shaped structure' target is not met there.", "severity": "functional", "evidence": "shots/critic/venus/w2r1/compare_ref_day_film78.png (ref | phase-20 body view | film t78); shots/critic/venus/w2r1/film_t78.png; shots/critic/venus/w2r1/film_t80.png; shots/critic/venus/w2r1/req_day.png; shots/critic/venus/w2r1/film_t75_crop.png (tan belt dominates the small disc)", "fix": "In bake.js, rotate the streak fields' long axis with latitude: about 0 deg at the equator, rising to 20-35 deg off zonal by 35-55 deg latitude so streaks spiral into the collar, and add the same tilt to the low-frequency warp. Cut the low-latitude meridional stretch from 5x to about 3x so streaks stop stacking into strata. Break the tan belt into one or two broad patchy peach regions with irregular edges, as in the reference, instead of a zonal band. Lift the Y arms' and the arc frame's planetary-scale amplitude about 1.5x while lowering the zonal mean's banding term, so that total p2-p98 stays about 9-10 %. Add a set of 2-3 broad curved bright arcs (the bow) on the hemisphere the film's day approach sees. Then check legibility at the film's real framing (t78-82), not only at phase 20."}, {"gap": "The dim-side and overall colour diverge from the reference. Toward the terminator and limb the render warms to brown/taupe: B/R 0.83 at 0.8 R and 0.73 at 0.9 R at phase 40; brown (110,99,85) at the half-phase terminator; taupe (120,110,95) in the limb close-up. The Mariner 10 reference goes neutral and then cool grey-blue toward its dim limb (B/R 1.00 at 0.6 R, 1.03 at 0.83 R, 1.08-1.16 at the edge). The deck is also uniformly beige (mean R-B 13.5 against 6.4 sRGB in the reference), with no cool-white streaks. This is seen in every day-disc frame (right side) and in the half-phase beat t83-85.", "severity": "cosmetic", "evidence": "shots/critic/venus/w2r1/x_day_phase40.png; shots/critic/venus/w2r1/req_half.png; shots/critic/venus/w2r1/film_t83.png; shots/critic/venus/w2r1/req_limb.png; bar/refs/venus_mariner10_or_akatsuki.jpg (profile at y=550, x=500-950)", "fix": "Give the upper haze a sub-micron mode-1 spectral slope, extinction proportional to lambda^-1 to -1.5 (spec about (0.88, 1.0, 1.18) instead of near-grey), so long slant paths at mu<0.3 add a faint cool component. Stop applying the uMsTint^(0.35+0.15 tau_view) yellowing to the single-scattered haze term at large emission angles; keep the cream tint only on the deck's own multiple-scattering term. Pull the deck's absorber/peach colour and uAbsCol back toward neutral at low absorber values so absorber-poor streaks read white to faintly cool. Targets: deck centre B/R about 0.96, dim limb B/R about 1.0-1.05, half-phase terminator grey-yellow rather than brown."}, {"gap": "Fine cloud detail at film scale shows procedural 'wood-grain/marble' contour swirls: concentric knot-like lines from domain-warped iso-noise. They are visible when the disc fills half the frame (t80-82) and strongly at the polar collar and in close views as fibrous hair-like strokes. This is a CG tell, even at about 1-2 % contrast.", "severity": "cosmetic", "evidence": "shots/critic/venus/w2r1/film_t80_crop_deck.png; shots/critic/venus/w2r1/x_pole_n.png; shots/critic/venus/w2r1/x_pole_s.png; shots/critic/venus/w2r1/x_graze105.png; shots/critic/venus/w2r1/req_limb.png", "fix": "In deck.js, clamp each fine octave's warp displacement to at most about 0.4x that octave's wavelength (scale the baked-field warp by octave), so the warp cannot fold the field into contour loops. Replace isotropic warped simplex for the 280-30 km octaves with flow-aligned anisotropic noise: sample along the local streak direction from the bake's gradient, stretched 3-4x along the flow and with soft edges. Halve the ridged share in the 300-30 km band. Fade octaves 3-5 toward a smooth soft-cell texture near the polar collar, where the current warp gradient is largest."}, {"gap": "In the crescent beat (t86-91, mid-size disc, about 5 s) the crescent and ring carry a broad inner 'rim glow' that runs around the whole night limb. The result reads slightly like a Fresnel rim-lit sphere rather than an optically thin haze ring on a black disc. At phase 172 light extends about 10 % R inside the ring (sRGB 203 at the ring, 35 at 20 px in, 9 at 34 px, R about 375 px); physically, haze above the night-side deck is sunlit only within about 9 deg of the terminator (about 1.2 % R projected). At phase 150 the crescent has a long tail to about 250 px (5-12 % of peak) beyond the 58 px geometric crescent. In the film the post glare adds to this and makes the night disc a muddy warm grey.", "severity": "cosmetic", "evidence": "shots/critic/venus/w2r1/req_ring172.png; shots/critic/venus/w2r1/x_ring178.png; shots/critic/venus/w2r1/x_cres150_ev3.png; shots/critic/venus/w2r1/x_cres156_ev13.png; shots/critic/venus/w2r1/film_t86.png; shots/critic/venus/w2r1/film_t89.png", "fix": "In haze.js, shadow the haze samples against the deck sphere with a proper penumbra (Sun angular radius about 0.7 deg at Venus) so in-scattering above the night-side deck dies within the geometric shadow height. Restrict the twilight-diffusion and forward-diffused-beam terms to points whose solar depression is below acos(Rd/r) plus a small margin. Cap the uMsF broadened-lobe term's horizontal reach (it is what spreads the glow inward). The ring then stays a 2-5 px band hugging and slightly outside the limb, and the crescent keeps a soft but defined inner edge. Re-check at phase 150/172 against matched EV."}, {"gap": "Close range is unconvincing, though the current cut does not use it (closest film approach is 1.55 R). From 1.05 R the limb is a plain 40 px fade to black with no brightened haze band and no detached-layer structure. From inside the 200 km haze shell (1.02 R), the sky directly above a fuzzy horizon is pure black with stars: no aerial perspective, no glowing haze. The contract requires bodies to hold up in a close pass at 0.05-0.3 R.", "severity": "functional", "evidence": "shots/critic/venus/w2r1/x_graze105.png (profile x=940-995 at y=450: 167 to 5 with no limb peak); shots/critic/venus/w2r1/x_inside_haze.png", "fix": "When the view ray grazes (impact parameter between Rd and Rt), integrate the full tangent path: the slant optical depth there is about 25x vertical and should give a bright cream limb band peaking at the cloud-top altitude, with a faint detached layer at about 94 km. Inside the shell, add a sky term for rays that leave the atmosphere (in-scatter along the whole exit path), so the horizon sky is bright haze fading upward, not black."}, {"gap": "The haze material switches material.side between FrontSide and BackSide when the camera enters the shell. For a THREE.ShaderMaterial, flipSided is part of the program cache key (WebGLPrograms.js layer 12), so this builds a second haze program (about 90 ms) at first entry. That violates the contract's 'all materials and programs exist by the end of create()' rule. It does not trigger in the current cut (minimum 1.55 R against a 1.033 R shell), so it causes no film hitch today.", "severity": "functional", "evidence": "src/bodies/venus.js update(): hazeMat.side = inside ? THREE.BackSide : THREE.FrontSide; node_modules/three/src/renderers/webgl/WebGLPrograms.js lines 577-580; shots/critic/venus/w2r1/x_inside_haze.png", "fix": "Create the haze as two meshes, front-side and back-side, each with its own material sharing uniforms, both built in create(), and toggle .visible between them. Alternatively use DoubleSide with gl_FrontFacing branching in the shader, so no program change ever happens mid-film."}], "shots": ["shots/critic/venus/w2r1/film_sweep_60_120.png", "shots/critic/venus/w2r1/film_t75.png", "shots/critic/venus/w2r1/film_t75_crop.png", "shots/critic/venus/w2r1/film_t77.png", "shots/critic/venus/w2r1/film_t78.png", "shots/critic/venus/w2r1/film_t80.png", "shots/critic/venus/w2r1/film_t80_crop_deck.png", "shots/critic/venus/w2r1/film_t80_crop_limb.png", "shots/critic/venus/w2r1/film_t82.png", "shots/critic/venus/w2r1/film_t83.png", "shots/critic/venus/w2r1/film_t84.png", "shots/critic/venus/w2r1/film_t84_cusp.png", "shots/critic/venus/w2r1/film_t85.png", "shots/critic/venus/w2r1/film_t86.png", "shots/critic/venus/w2r1/film_t87.png", "shots/critic/venus/w2r1/film_t88.png", "shots/critic/venus/w2r1/film_t89.png", "shots/critic/venus/w2r1/film_t90.png", "shots/critic/venus/w2r1/film_t91.png", "shots/critic/venus/w2r1/seq/s_t89.png", "shots/critic/venus/w2r1/req_day.png", "shots/critic/venus/w2r1/req_half.png", "shots/critic/venus/w2r1/req_crescent.png", "shots/critic/venus/w2r1/req_ring172.png", "shots/critic/venus/w2r1/req_limb.png", "shots/critic/venus/w2r1/req_motion_d0.png", "shots/critic/venus/w2r1/req_motion_d0.5.png", "shots/critic/venus/w2r1/req_motion_d1.png", "shots/critic/venus/w2r1/motion_strip.png", "shots/critic/venus/w2r1/x_motion_d0.05.png", "shots/critic/venus/w2r1/x_motion_d0.1.png", "shots/critic/venus/w2r1/compare_ref_day_film78.png", "shots/critic/venus/w2r1/x_day_phase40.png", "shots/critic/venus/w2r1/x_cres150_ev3.png", "shots/critic/venus/w2r1/x_cres156_ev13.png", "shots/critic/venus/w2r1/x_ring178.png", "shots/critic/venus/w2r1/x_limb_yawp50.png", "shots/critic/venus/w2r1/x_limb_yawm50.png", "shots/critic/venus/w2r1/x_limb_yawp38.png", "shots/critic/venus/w2r1/x_limb_yawp38_enh.png", "shots/critic/venus/w2r1/x_limb_yawp50_ev3.png", "shots/critic/venus/w2r1/x_pole_n.png", "shots/critic/venus/w2r1/x_pole_s.png", "shots/critic/venus/w2r1/x_glory_phase3.png", "shots/critic/venus/w2r1/x_graze105.png", "shots/critic/venus/w2r1/x_inside_haze.png", "shots/critic/venus/w2r1/x_term_zoom.png", "shots/critic/venus/w2r1/x_small30.png", "shots/critic/venus/w2r1/x_tiny120.png", "shots/critic/venus/w2r1/x_tiny120_crop.png"], "compileMs": 363}, "w2-critic1:post": {"floorMet": true, "score": 7, "summary": "Post scores 7.0. It clearly meets its floor (HDR, bloom, filmic tonemap) and in most of the film it looks very good. No page errors came from post. The Neptune and Sun modules failed once each during my runs; those were other pieces mid-edit, and I re-ran the affected frames.\n\nWhat works:\n- **Sun behind a limb.** The sunrise over Earth's limb (t=126.0-126.6) is the best post work in the film. The probe makes the sun a dimmed red spark at the limb that blooms into a 14-point starburst with heavy veiling glare, and it reads like a real orbital photo. The grazing set (aim=sun, phase 105-107) dims and reddens the sun correctly as it sets through the atmosphere. Sun behind Saturn's rings (t=286.2-287.6) sparkles through the gaps and then cuts off.\n- **Hero Sun.** Its limb-darkening profile matches the SDO HMI reference within a few levels (R/G at radius 0.85: 224/119 vs the ref's 253/119).\n- **Tone and dither.** The AgX tonemap holds the Blue Marble Earth well. There is no banding at all: dithered run lengths average about 1.8 px, and stretched gradients are smooth on Neptune's terminator, the off-frame glare and the finale beam.\n- **Letterbox.** Bars are exactly 2.388:1, true black, with an anti-aliased edge.\n- **Warp quality.** Trails are smooth and coherent from frame to frame, the destination stays crisp at the focus, and nothing is nauseating.\n- **Off-screen exit.** The sun fades out gracefully as it leaves the frame (exit sweep), and nothing flares when it is hidden.\n- **Cost.** Post takes 0.38 ms on the GPU with warp and letterbox forced on; the required perf run gives 4.19 ms/frame, 239 fps.\n\nWhat hurts:\n1. **Square sun.** When the sun's disc is more than about 10 px in radius, the starburst turns it into a tilted white square, and the spikes disappear. This happens at the real Mercury and Venus geometry. postDebug=raw confirms the scene disc is round, so this is post.\n2. **Frozen warp.** During a steady warp the streak pattern does not move; it reads as a still zoom-blur photo, not travel. This affects every leg, about 45 s of full-frame film.\n3. **Ghosts look like a preset.** The same violet heptagon outline and green dot appear in every sun-in-frame shot. At higher exposure the heptagon becomes a flat lilac sprite.\n4. **No Mercury glare.** The Mercury beat (\"blinding just off-frame\") has no optical trace: with the sun 33-41 degrees off-axis, the sunward edge is 0.19/255.\n5. **Small tells.** Edge colour fringing splits 1-px stars into separate R, G and B pixels near the frame edge. The finale beam is a single grey band where the Voyager reference shows a faint, coloured, multi-beam veil.\n\nThe film was being re-cut while I worked (duration 434.1 s, then 430.4 s), so the times quoted are approximate.", "gaps": [{"gap": "When the solar disc is larger than about 10 px in radius (and not in hero mode), the diffraction starburst renders the Sun as a tilted white SQUARE and the spikes vanish. Seen at the real Venus geometry (rpx 13: fov 25), Earth fov 20 (rpx 12) and fov 8 (rpx 30), and Mercury fov 45 / 15 / 6 (rpx 15 / 49 / 122; at fov 6 the halo becomes a rounded square). Cause: the 'disc convolution' samples the mip-mapped PSF at LOD log2(1.2*rpx/H/psfTexel). The mip box filter turns the PSF's concentrated peak into a square plateau 2^lod texels wide, and uPSFRot (0.21 rad) tilts it. In the current cut the Sun stays 1-3 degrees outside the frame at Venus (t=87-91) and 33-41 degrees out at Mercury, so the bug is mostly latent. Any re-cut that brings the Sun into frame near Mercury or Venus (which the shot list asks for), or any telephoto Sun, shows it.", "severity": "structural", "evidence": "shots/critic/post/w2r1/disc/mercury_fov45.png, disc/mercury_fov15.png, disc/mercury_fov6.png, disc/earth_fov_montage.png, disc/zoom_venus_fov25.png; disc/mercury_debug_montage.png (raw = round disc, postDebug=flare = square)", "fix": "Stop using mip LOD as the disc convolution. Cap uPSF.z so one PSF texel is at most about a quarter of the disc radius. Do the convolution in the shader with a small Vogel/golden-angle disc of 12-16 taps spread over rpx, rotated per pixel by blue noise. Alternatively, precompute 3-4 disc-convolved PSF levels at start-up by multiplying the FFT by the disc's jinc transfer function (psf.js already has the FFT), then lerp between levels by rpx. In either case, subtract or mask the PSF's central energy inside about 1.5 disc radii (the disc itself is already in the scene), and blend kStar toward the analytic aureole above about 40 px so no texel structure is ever visible."}, {"gap": "Warp streaks are frozen. During a steady warp the post radial blur (on top of the sky's own streaks) gives a static zoom-blur of a static sky: the same streaks sit in the same places from 323.0 to 324.5 s, and only their length and the slow aim drift change. In playback every leg reads as a still photograph with radial blur, not as flying through space. This covers every leg (about 45 s of full-frame film: 42-45, 68-72, 93-96, 161-163, 192-194, 215-224, 261-268, 323-330, 351-356, 395-404).", "severity": "functional", "evidence": "shots/critic/post/w2r1/temporal_warp_steady_montage.png (t=323-324.5, identical streak field), temporal_warp_montage.png, warp/w_262.png, warp/w_193.5.png, warp_postwarp0_compare.png", "fix": "Add outward flow driven by distance travelled. Take a pure phase phi(t) from the director's speedC integral, which seeks cleanly. In the comb pass, modulate tap weights with a travelling window in log-r, for example w_i *= 0.6 + 0.4*sin(2*pi*(s_i - phi*k) + hash(ray angle)). Streaks then visibly race outward, and each angular sector has its own phase so there is no strobing. Alternatively, draw a sparse post-side layer of deterministic 'speed dust' points on rays from the focus of expansion: radius = fract(seed + phi*rate) on a log scale, fading in near the focus and out at the edge. Keep the destination point crisp as now. Coordinate with sky so the two streak systems do not double up."}, {"gap": "The ghost chain still reads like a flare preset. Every sun-in-frame shot carries the same violet 7-sided polygon (thin bright outline, near-empty interior), a small green dot and a large faint lune. At higher exposure the polygon becomes a flat, semi-opaque lilac sprite. On screen: Venus 86-92 (six seconds, mid-right of frame), sunrise 126-127, Titan 316-323, Neptune 369-392 (faint at flare 0.4) and finale 396-404, about 25 s in all.", "severity": "cosmetic", "evidence": "shots/critic/post/w2r1/film_88.png, film_titan_322.5.png, warp/w_400.png, warp/w_403.png, req_glare150.png, req_glare150_exp8.png, extra_jupiter_sun_exp3.png", "fix": "Make the stop ghosts read as defocused light, not vector outlines. Give the interior a soft, slightly uneven fill (about 25-40 % of the rim in display, not 4-8 % linear), and soften the rim to 3-6 px with a gentle asymmetric caustic (brighter on the side facing the axis). Vary ghost size and hue more with off-axis angle. Lower the polygon's saturation to at most 0.2 and drive its brightness by the sun's actual frame brightness, so high exposure gives a brighter soft disc, not an opaque sprite. Consider dropping the always-present green dot or limiting it to a narrow field window."}, {"gap": "The Mercury chapter's off-frame Sun has no optical signature. The shot list asks for the Sun 'huge and blinding just off-frame', and the director even sets flare 1.5, but at 33-41 degrees off-axis the sunward frame edge sits at 0.19/255, the same as the far edge. Across the 26-s chapter you cannot tell where the Sun is. The director placing the Sun 33 degrees out is part of the cause, but post's off-axis glare model falls to effectively zero there (k_glare about 0.2 of an already tiny term).", "severity": "functional", "evidence": "shots/critic/post/w2r1/film_56.png (edge-column measurement: left 0.19, right 0.10 /255), film_mercury_sheet.png, scratchpad sunscan (t=54-57: out 33-41 deg, k 0.03-0.21)", "fix": "Add a physically motivated off-axis veiling term with a long tail: L_veil = E_sun * c * (1 + theta/8deg)^-2 * hood(theta), with c set so a sun 10 degrees out gives about 4-6 % of white and 35 degrees out about 1-2 % at the edge. Shape it as a broad gradient from the sunward edge, with a soft lens-hood cutoff at about 60-70 degrees. Keep it exposure-linear so pushed night-side shots veil more. Tell the director that a 10-15 degree offset gives the 'blinding just off-frame' beat."}, {"gap": "Lateral chromatic aberration is applied as discrete R/B offsets, so 1-2 px stars near the frame edges become separate red, green and blue pixels, which reads as a digital RGB split. Seen in every wide star field, at the corners and edges.", "severity": "cosmetic", "evidence": "shots/critic/post/w2r1/zoom_film424_dot.png, zoom_corner_tr_ca.png", "fix": "Make the CA a short spectral smear along the radial direction instead of two shifted taps. Take 3-5 taps over the displacement, weighted toward red at the outer end and blue at the inner end (as the warp pass already does for point-like taps), so a star becomes a soft 2-3 px coloured gradient. Optionally cap the displacement at about 0.8 px at 1600x900."}, {"gap": "The finale pale-blue-dot beam (t=420-429, full frame, about 9 s) is one grey, monochrome soft band on pure black. The Voyager reference shows faint coloured scattered-light beams (slightly pink-white, with fainter parallel companions) over a lifted, noisy veil that grades toward the sunward side. The current beam reads as a smear rather than as stray light in a camera.", "severity": "cosmetic", "evidence": "shots/critic/post/w2r1/film_424.png vs bar/refs/pale_blue_dot_revisited.jpg; zoom_film424_glow_stretch.png", "fix": "Tint the beam slightly warm or pink, with a faint blue rim from dispersion. Add 1-2 fainter parallel beams at different widths and offsets (internal reflections from the barrel and baffles). Add a low veiling gradient across the frame from the sunward edge, at about 2-4 % of white with grain. Keep Earth's single pixel inside the brightest beam, as the director intends."}], "shots": ["shots/critic/post/w2r1/sweep.png", "shots/critic/post/w2r1/req_glare150.png", "shots/critic/post/w2r1/req_glare165.png", "shots/critic/post/w2r1/req_limb176.png", "shots/critic/post/w2r1/req_corner.png", "shots/critic/post/w2r1/req_neptune_band.png", "shots/critic/post/w2r1/zoom_neptune_term_stretch.png", "shots/critic/post/w2r1/zoom_corner_glare_stretch.png", "shots/critic/post/w2r1/zoom_glare150_sun.png", "shots/critic/post/w2r1/zoom_glare150_ghosts.png", "shots/critic/post/w2r1/req_glare150_exp8.png", "shots/critic/post/w2r1/film_intro_sheet.png", "shots/critic/post/w2r1/intro_montage.png", "shots/critic/post/w2r1/film_15.png", "shots/critic/post/w2r1/film_mercury_sheet.png", "shots/critic/post/w2r1/film_56.png", "shots/critic/post/w2r1/film_venus_sheet.png", "shots/critic/post/w2r1/film_88.png", "shots/critic/post/w2r1/film_84.png", "shots/critic/post/w2r1/film_earth_sheet.png", "shots/critic/post/w2r1/film_112.png", "shots/critic/post/w2r1/sunrise_montage.png", "shots/critic/post/w2r1/sunrise/s_126.3.png", "shots/critic/post/w2r1/film_127.png", "shots/critic/post/w2r1/graze_montage.png", "shots/critic/post/w2r1/roll_montage.png", "shots/critic/post/w2r1/exit_montage.png", "shots/critic/post/w2r1/disc/mercury_fov45.png", "shots/critic/post/w2r1/disc/mercury_fov15.png", "shots/critic/post/w2r1/disc/mercury_fov6.png", "shots/critic/post/w2r1/disc/mercury_debug_montage.png", "shots/critic/post/w2r1/disc/earth_fov_montage.png", "shots/critic/post/w2r1/disc/zoom_venus_fov25.png", "shots/critic/post/w2r1/disc/neptune_sun.png", "shots/critic/post/w2r1/film_saturn_sheet.png", "shots/critic/post/w2r1/film_saturn_occl_sheet.png", "shots/critic/post/w2r1/ringocc_montage.png", "shots/critic/post/w2r1/film_290.png", "shots/critic/post/w2r1/film_296.png", "shots/critic/post/w2r1/film_titan_322.5.png", "shots/critic/post/w2r1/extra_jupiter_sun_exp3.png", "shots/critic/post/w2r1/film_neptune_finale_sheet.png", "shots/critic/post/w2r1/film_424.png", "shots/critic/post/w2r1/zoom_film424_glow_stretch.png", "shots/critic/post/w2r1/zoom_film424_dot.png", "shots/critic/post/w2r1/zoom_corner_tr_ca.png", "shots/critic/post/w2r1/warp/w_94.png", "shots/critic/post/w2r1/warp/w_193.5.png", "shots/critic/post/w2r1/warp/w_221.5.png", "shots/critic/post/w2r1/warp/w_262.png", "shots/critic/post/w2r1/warp/w_400.png", "shots/critic/post/w2r1/warp/w_403.png", "shots/critic/post/w2r1/film_jupiter_warp_sheet.png", "shots/critic/post/w2r1/warp_postwarp0_compare.png", "shots/critic/post/w2r1/temporal_warp_montage.png", "shots/critic/post/w2r1/temporal_warp_steady_montage.png", "shots/critic/post/w2r1/temporal_warp_uranus_montage.png"], "compileMs": -1}, "w2-diet:uranus": "The piece now compiles in **453 ms** (budget 800), down from **7367 ms** (5888 in your wave-1 run). The largest single object is the globe at **269 ms** (cap 350). The black limb pixels are fixed at the cause, and 16 of the 17 comparison views are pixel-identical apart from those fixed pixels. All three parts were measured.\n\nOne decision you should know about: in the film the moons use a lean shader. The full-detail moon shader would cost about 1.0 s on its own, which alone is over the cap. A script that runs your current cut shows no moon is ever bigger than 3.2 px in radius (Ariel at t=344 s). So the film builds a lean version: same lighting, albedo patterns and relief, but no resolved craters, coronae or faults. At film size it matches the full version to within 0.8% in brightness. Moon close-up views (`target=<moon>` or `umoons=full`) still build the full shader. The choice is fixed at `create()`, so nothing is created lazily. If the director adds a moon close pass, set `FULL_MOONS_IN_FILM = true` in `moons.js`; the piece would then cost about 1.4 s.\n\n**Compile cost** (`node tools/checks/shader_cost.mjs --q \"only=uranus,post\"`, lower of 2 runs)\n\n| object | before | after |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| globe | 2166 ms | 269\u2013285 ms |\n| moons | 3139 ms (Miranda) + 1969 ms (other four) | 130 ms (one lean shader for all five) |\n| haze | 93 ms | 53 ms |\n| rings | 0 ms | 0 ms |\n| **total** | **7367 ms** | **453 ms** (second run 472) |\n\nWhat actually slows the Windows shader compiler (I measured it offline on the translated shaders):\n- **Duplicated code:** every inlined copy of a noise call counts. The globe had about 35 copies of `snoise`, roughly 75% of its code. Each noise function now has one call site, driven by a table of samples.\n- **Branches inside loops:** this was the biggest multiplier. In a test, the same 16 noise samples took 1520 ms behind `continue`s and 383 ms with no branches. The loops now use arithmetic selects with a single guard around each heavy call.\n- **Dead ends:** compiler flags, branch attributes and the `discard` trick have no effect here.\n\nAll of this is written up for other pieces in `notes/uranus.md`.\n\n**Black pixels:** in the haze shell, a ray exactly tangent to the cloud deck rounds to \"touching the deck\" while the ray\u2013sphere test reports a miss. The old code then used the miss value (1e20) as a hit distance, which overflowed to a zero vector and produced 0/0 in the sunlight calculation. The resulting NaN poisoned the anti-aliased pixel, and post-processing turned it black. The fix: the deck branch now requires a real hit, a tangent miss is treated as a graze at the cloud top, and the airmass function guards against zero length. The NaN finder (`udbg=9`) is clean in all 17 views, and the dark-pixel scan finds 0 (was 4 and 7).\n\n**Image comparison:** 17 views, the required five plus Miranda, the other moons, the crescent, limb, skim and rings. Both sets were rendered back to back with the sky excluded, because the sky changed between runs from another builder's work.\n- **16 of 17 views:** mean absolute difference \u2264 0.001 on 0\u2013255; the only differing pixels are the former black ones.\n- **Oberon:** mean difference 0.045, 0.1% of pixels shifted by about 1 px along its fault walls. I swapped each rewritten function back in turn and none of them caused it. It comes from the compiler's float ordering in the hash at Oberon's large fault seed, not from a logic change.\n\n**Frame time:** at 1600\u00d7900 everything still sits at the 240 Hz vsync cap. At 4K the branch-free loops cost more per pixel: globe close-up 5.6 \u2192 6.4 ms, Miranda close-up 6.4 \u2192 9.1 ms (full shader, close-up views only).\n\nBest commands:\n```bash\nnode tools/checks/shader_cost.mjs --q \"only=uranus,post\" --top 8\nsh src/bodies/uranus/tools/w2views.sh shots/uranus/w2_after_ns \"&only=uranus,post\"\npython -I src/bodies/uranus/tools/imgdiff.py shots/uranus/w2_before_ns shots/uranus/w2_after_ns shots/uranus/w2_diff_ns 16\nnode tools/shot.mjs --hud 0 --q \"view=body&body=uranus&dist=2.2&phase=20&el=30&fov=40&udbg=9\" --out shots/uranus/nan.png\nnode tools/shot.mjs --hud 0 --q \"view=body&body=uranus&target=miranda&dist=3&phase=50\" --out shots/uranus/miranda.png\nnode src/bodies/uranus/tools/moonsize.mjs\n```\n\nRemaining gaps:\n- The globe has about 20% headroom under the 350 ms cap on this loaded machine.\n- The full moon shader (1.0 s) is outside the budget by design.\n- Lean moons would look smooth if ever framed above about 10 px.\n\nRequests to the integrator are in the notes: compile the whole scene in parallel at warm-up (`compileAsync`), and pass the branch-in-loop finding to the other pieces. The film run also reported an unrelated earth-module syntax error from another builder's in-progress edit.\n\nEverything is in `C:\\Users\\danny\\fun\\space-animation-v1\\`:\n- **Code:** `src/bodies/uranus/globe.js`, `moons.js`, `shared.js`, `rings.js`\n- **Notes:** `notes/uranus.md` (new wave-2 section)\n- **Dev tools:** `src/bodies/uranus/tools/` (cost.sh, fxc.py, hlsl.mjs, segs.py, stub.py, ctime2.html, dump.html, moonsize.mjs, imgdiff.py, darkpix.py, w2views.sh)\n- **Shots:** `shots/uranus/w2_before_ns/`, `w2_after_ns/`, `w2_diff_ns/`, and the before/after crop `w2_after_ns/nan_fix_before_after.png`"}, "interrupted": ["w2-fix1:earth", "w2-fix1:jupiter", "w2-fix1:audio", "w2-fix1:mercury", "w2-critic1:director", "w2-fix1:hud", "w2-fix2:sky", "w2-critic1:neptune", "w2-critic1:mars", "w2-fix2:sun", "w2-fix2:saturn"]}

const BY = Object.fromEntries(PIECES.map((p) => [p.key, p]))
const SCENE_PIECES = new Set(['sun', 'mercury', 'venus', 'earth', 'mars', 'jupiter', 'saturn', 'uranus', 'neptune', 'asteroids'])

function compileNote(key) {
  if (!SCENE_PIECES.has(key)) {
    if (key === 'post' || key === 'sky')
      return `SHADER COMPILE COST: your passes are not scene objects in the warm-up probe, but they are compiled at first use like everything else. Make sure every program you will ever use is created and drawn once at create()/first frame (no lazily created passes or define changes mid-film, which would hitch playback). Check with node tools/checks/perf_probe.mjs --q warm=1 --t <times> (cold frame after a seek must be < 80 ms everywhere).`
    return ''
  }
  const ms = COMPILE[key]
  const cur = ms == null ? 'not attributable in the wave-1 probe (it compiled at boot) — measure it' : `${ms} ms (wave-1 lead measurement, whole film)`
  return `SHADER COMPILE BUDGET (new, hard requirement). Chrome on Windows compiles every program through ANGLE -> HLSL -> FXC at its FIRST DRAW, and FXC fully unrolls loops with constant bounds and inlines every function call, so large raymarch / fbm shaders take seconds each. The film now warms every shader behind the splash, and that warm-up takes 61 s in total today — unacceptable for a first-time viewer. Measure your piece with: node tools/checks/shader_cost.mjs --q "only=${key},post" --top 25 (it uses a fresh Chrome profile every time and takes 1-2 min; other agents share the CPU, so run it twice and take the lower numbers). Your piece now: ${cur}. Budget: the piece's objects total <= 800 ms, no single object > 350 ms. Proven FXC remedies: make heavy loop bounds NON-constant (for (int i = 0; i < uSteps; i++) with an int uniform — GLSL ES 3.00 allows it — so FXC emits a real loop; a constant bound with an early break still unrolls); collapse nested loops; call large fbm/noise functions from one call site and reuse the result; move per-pixel-constant work to uniforms/JS; avoid dynamic indexing into large local arrays; keep implicit-derivative texture reads out of non-uniform loops (textureLod/textureGrad inside loops). Also: every material your piece will ever draw must exist in the scene graph by the end of create() (toggling .visible / LOD switching is fine; creating meshes or materials lazily later, or changing defines at runtime, is not — those would hitch the film). Prove the look is unchanged (or better) after the diet with before/after shots of the same views.`
}

function crossFor(key) {
  const xs = XP.filter((c) => new RegExp('\\b' + key + '\\b', 'i').test(c.owner))
  if (!xs.length) return ''
  return 'Requests routed to you from other pieces\' critics (triage cross-piece list):\n' + xs.map((c) => `- ${c.gap} (reported by: ${c.reported_by}; owner note: ${c.owner})`).join('\n')
}

function focusBlock(key) {
  const d = TRI[key]
  if (!d) return ''
  return `TRIAGE FOCUS for this wave (ordered — do them in this order; they are what the film viewer notices most):\n${(d.focus || []).map((f, i) => `${i + 1}. ${f}`).join('\n')}\n\nWhy this piece got this round: ${d.why}\nFilm window(s) where this piece is on screen (current cut; the director is re-cutting, so re-locate by chapter/focus): ${d.film_window}${d.redirect_criterion ? `\nNote from triage on judging: ${d.redirect_criterion}` : ''}`
}

const WAVE2_CONTEXT = `WAVE 2. This piece already exists and met its floor in wave 1 (three blind critique rounds). You are a fresh builder continuing it: read notes/<piece>.md and your own files first (they are long; skim the structure, read closely what you change).

What changed in the project since wave 1:
- tools/shot.mjs now reuses pooled Chrome profiles, so the GPU shader cache survives between runs: a warm run takes ~5 s instead of ~40 s. Use it freely; --fresh forces a cold profile.
- main.js has a shader warm-up behind the splash (outside capture mode; in capture mode only with warm=1): every object in the scene graph is drawn once off screen, then one frame every 4 s of the film.
- The director is re-cutting the film in parallel (removing dead time, slower turns, longer hero holds), so chapter times will move. Find your chapter via the focus/chapter that shot.mjs prints per --t (e.g. a coarse sweep --t 0:470:4), never by old timestamps alone.
- The asteroid builder is gating the grey belt ring that fogged many wave-1 views. If you still see a uniform grey band along the ecliptic in your views, it is theirs, not yours; you may add only=<id>,sky,post to exclude it.`

function builderPrompt2(p, round, verdict) {
  const w1 = W1[p.key] || {}
  const base = `${COMMON_BUILDER}\n\n${p.brief}\n\nThe independent critic will check at least these views (make them stunning), and will also judge the piece in the film:\n${p.shots}\n\n${WAVE2_CONTEXT.replace('<piece>', p.key)}\n\n`
  const cn = compileNote(p.key)
  const xp = crossFor(p.key)
  if (round === 1) {
    return `${base}${focusBlock(p.key)}\n\n${xp}\n\n${cn}\n\nThe wave-1 final critic verdict for context (score ${w1.score}):\n${JSON.stringify({ summary: w1.summary, gaps: w1.gaps }, null, 1)}\n\nYour job this round: close the triage focus items in order, root-caused (not papered over), verified both in body views and in film frames${cn ? ', and bring the shader compile cost inside the budget' : ''}. If time remains, continue down the wave-1 gap list. Do not regress anything that works — compare before/after shots. Keep notes/${p.key}.md current (replace stale sections; it must stay readable for the integrator). Your final reply: what changed, the best shot commands, the measured compile cost (if applicable), and remaining gaps.`
  }
  const v = verdict ? JSON.stringify(verdict, null, 1) : '(the critic failed to return a verdict — review the piece yourself against the target and the triage focus, and improve its weakest aspects)'
  return `${base}${focusBlock(p.key)}\n\n${xp}\n\n${cn}\n\nThis is fix round 2 of wave 2. A previous wave-2 builder already worked this round's focus; a fresh blind critic then reviewed the result. Their verdict:\n${v}\n\nClose the critic's BIGGEST gap first and root-cause it, then work down the list, keeping the triage focus in mind (what the film viewer sees most). Verify every fix by rendering and looking, in body views and film frames. Do not regress what works. Keep notes/${p.key}.md current. Final reply: what changed, best shot commands, compile cost (if applicable), remaining gaps.`
}

function criticPrompt2(p, round) {
  const d = TRI[p.key] || {}
  const w1 = W1[p.key] || {}
  const filmPart = p.key === 'audio'
    ? `Render the score against the CURRENT cut (the director may have re-cut the film; the score reads the live chapter/cue table).`
    : `Judge in the FILM, not only in body views: locate this piece's chapter in the current cut (shot.mjs prints focus/chapter for every --t; the director may have re-cut the film, so first sweep e.g. --t 0:470:4 --sheet ... and pick this piece's frames), capture its film frames densely at the film's real distances and exposures, and weight every gap by how long and how large it is on screen in the film. Also capture the required body views. Previously reported film windows (old cut): ${d.film_window || 'n/a'}.`
  const compilePart = SCENE_PIECES.has(p.key)
    ? `Measure shader compile cost: node tools/checks/shader_cost.mjs --q "only=${p.key},post" --top 15 (fresh profile, 1-2 min). Report the piece's total in compileMs. Over budget (total > 800 ms or any one object > 350 ms) is a functional gap.`
    : `Set compileMs to -1.`
  return `${COMMON_CRITIC}\n\nPiece under review: ${p.key} (stake: ${p.stake}). This is WAVE 2, critique round ${round}. Write images under shots/critic/${p.key}/w2r${round}/.\nWhat the piece is supposed to be (the brief its builder received):\n${p.brief}\n\n${p.shots}\n\n${filmPart}\n${d.redirect_criterion ? `Judging note (from the run's triage; it does not lower the bar, it only stops an unclosable or misattributed item from masking the rest): ${d.redirect_criterion}\n` : ''}${compilePart}\nThe piece scored ${w1.score} in wave 1. That is context only — judge fresh against the bar, on the same harsh scale. shot.mjs now reuses Chrome profiles (warm shader cache); that is expected.`
}

const URANUS_DIET = `${COMMON_BUILDER}\n\nPiece: uranus. Owns src/bodies/uranus.js, src/bodies/uranus/*.\n\n${WAVE2_CONTEXT.replace('<piece>', 'uranus')}\n\nUranus is BANKED at its visual target for this wave (score 7; the film shows it only as a small bullseye). Your job is engineering only, with no change to the look:\n1. ${compileNote('uranus')}\n2. Fix the deterministic black-pixel artifact on the lit limb: 4-6 px of about sRGB (5,7,9) inside a ~190 limb in the rings-close view, locked to the view (likely a NaN/negative sqrt or a divide by ~0 in the haze or ring-shadow math). Find it with the required views and close-up variants; guard the math at the root.\nBefore you change anything, render the required views (view=body&body=uranus&dist=3&phase=20, dist=6&phase=10&el=60, dist=1.6&phase=60, dist=2.2&phase=150, plus the moons view in notes/uranus.md) to shots/uranus/w2_before/, and after your changes render the same queries to shots/uranus/w2_after/ and compare (Read them; also compute mean absolute pixel difference with a small script). Keep notes/uranus.md current. Final reply: before/after compile cost, the NaN root cause, and the image comparison.`

const order = ['director', 'asteroids', 'sun', 'earth', 'jupiter', 'saturn', 'sky', 'post', 'audio', 'venus', 'mars', 'neptune', 'mercury', 'hud', 'uranus']

let resolveDirR1
const dirR1 = new Promise((r) => (resolveDirR1 = r))

// Steps that completed before the pause (notes/_board_wave2_partial.json) are reused, not re-run.
const has = (key, step) => `w2-${step}:${key}` in (DONE.completed || {})
const doneStep = (key, step) => DONE.completed[`w2-${step}:${key}`]
const step = (key, name, run) => (has(key, name) ? Promise.resolve(doneStep(key, name)) : run())

async function runPiece(key) {
  const p = BY[key]
  const d = TRI[key] || { rounds: 1 }
  const out = { piece: key, stake: p.stake, w1: (W1[key] || {}).score, verdicts: [], builds: [] }
  if (key === 'uranus') {
    out.builds.push(await step(key, 'diet', () => agent(URANUS_DIET, { label: 'w2-diet:uranus', phase: 'Fix' })))
    return out
  }
  if (key === 'hud' && !has(key, 'fix1')) {
    log('hud: waiting for the director re-cut (wave 2 round 1)')
    await dirR1
  }
  let b1 = null
  try {
    b1 = await step(key, 'fix1', () => agent(builderPrompt2(p, 1, null), { label: `w2-fix1:${key}`, phase: 'Fix' }))
  } finally {
    if (key === 'director') {
      resolveDirR1()
      log('director re-cut (wave 2 round 1) landed; hud and audio round 2 may start')
    }
  }
  out.builds.push(b1)
  const v1 = await step(key, 'critic1', () => agent(criticPrompt2(p, 1), { label: `w2-critic1:${key}`, phase: 'Critique', schema: VERDICT }))
  out.verdicts.push(v1)
  log(`${key}: wave1 ${out.w1} -> w2r1 ${v1 ? v1.score : 'n/a'}`)
  if ((d.rounds || 1) >= 2) {
    if (key === 'audio') await dirR1
    out.builds.push(await step(key, 'fix2', () => agent(builderPrompt2(p, 2, v1), { label: `w2-fix2:${key}`, phase: 'Fix' })))
    const v2 = await step(key, 'critic2', () => agent(criticPrompt2(p, 2), { label: `w2-critic2:${key}`, phase: 'Critique', schema: VERDICT }))
    out.verdicts.push(v2)
    log(`${key}: w2r2 ${v2 ? v2.score : 'n/a'}`)
  }
  return out
}

const results = await parallel(order.map((k) => () => runPiece(k)))

const board = results.filter(Boolean).map((r) => {
  const last = r.verdicts.filter(Boolean).slice(-1)[0] || null
  return {
    piece: r.piece,
    stake: r.stake,
    w1: r.w1,
    w2: r.verdicts.map((v) => (v ? v.score : null)),
    compileMs: last ? last.compileMs : null,
    final: last ? { score: last.score, floorMet: last.floorMet, summary: last.summary, topGaps: (last.gaps || []).slice(0, 5) } : null,
    lastBuild: r.builds.filter(Boolean).slice(-1)[0] || null,
  }
})
log(board.map((b) => `${b.piece}: ${b.w1} -> ${b.w2.join(' -> ') || 'diet'}`).join(' | '))
return board
